      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</head>
        <p>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Owen Watkins, who spoke at the May anniversary of Wesleyan Foreign Missions, is some- times called the Methodist Moffat. He is a short, dapper, bearded, Boer-looking Welshman, who knows the whole of South Africa by heart, in virtue of a residence there of nearly twenty years. He is as much at home leading a bullock team on a new trek as he would be leading a Methodist class meet- ing. He has penetrated into Zambesian regions where no white man had ever trod before, and has on occasion been interviewed by the Colonial Office in reference to the current political problems of Bechuanaland, Swaziland, and the Transvaal. He , has met more lions than Lord Randolph Churchill ever did, and has taken a great interest in the recent developments of his mission in Mashonaland despite a long spell of ill-health, lie has long had a great ambition to carry his work right up to the I banks of the Zambesi.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ilaweis, who followed up "Judas Iscariot " with " Pun ins Pilate " at St. James's, Marylebone, on Sunday, handled the Roman procurator like Izaak %Veit on's frog—as if he loved him. As Judas was a low-minded money gtubbing man, led by these instincts into the blunder of the betrayal, so was Pilate a sharp Roman magistrate, who was anxious to do what was right so far as be could without personal inconvenience to himself. The two character sketches were smart, and seemed to interest large congregations, among which were a great many clergymen. Mr. 'lime is did not announce the next candidate for whitewashing among what he called the "traditional blackguards " of sacred history. ---- Pastor Johannes Palmer's lines have not, fallen in very pleasant places. He is Legationspredikant, or, as we should. say, chaplain to the Swedish Embassy, officiating in the little church in Prince's-square, St.Georges's-in-the-East,where Scandinavian sailors most do congregate. It is here the great Emmanuel Swedenborg lies buried; and Sunday was a high- day and holiday. A large congregation gathered in the little building, among them being the Swedish Minister at the Court of St. James's and Madame Akerman. The well-known singer, Miss Janson, sang two solos, and Pastor Palmer preached an excellent sermon from Exodus xx., 2-3, wherein he referred to the occasion of the gathering, which was to commemorate the Convension of Upsala in 1593. At this convention the Lutheran doctrines were finally accepted in the Scandinavian peninsula, and, in the words of the President, Sweden became " one man" in this respect. The 11 anniversary ot the event was celebrated in 1693 and 11793, and was commemorated throughout Sweden lon Sunday.</p>
        <p>The pastor jean eloquent preacher. As a rule the discourses at the little church of Ulrica Eleanor&amp; do not soar into theological or ecclesiastical heights but on Sunday was an exceptional event, and the pastor rose to the occasion, waxing not a little con- troversial. Rome, of course, was the house of bondage in the text, and Luther was the Moses of the Exodus. lie pointed out in elaborate detail how Sweden three centuries ago, shook herself elem. of this thraldom, and had ever since been a free and evangelical country. Of course nearly all the congregation hailed from Ultima Thule ; and the few strangers present found their knowledge of Swedish taxed to its utmost extent in order to follow the preacher's fluent periods.</p>
        <p>Mrs. F. A. Steel, the author of the clever sketches of Indian life appearing in "Macmillan," has spent most of her life in India, living and working chiefly in the Jungle stations. She was employed by Government to inspect the female schools, and was the only woman member of the Education Commit- tee. Iler own private schools were distinctly un- proselytising, either the Koran or the Shastras being taught in them, and hence her unusual op- portunities for knowing the social lives of the natives. Whilst there Mrs. Steel wrote much for the Indian papers, and published a volume of folk- lore, as well as a cookery book (now in its third edition). On her return to England a wish to utilise her experiences resulted in the above-named sketches of native life.</p>
        <p>We are threatened with anew Australian lecturer Mr. Guy Boothby. This is the adventurous gentle- man who, after visiting the Malay Archipelago,New Guinea. and the pearl fisheries in Torres Straits, struck right across the Australian continent from Normanton to the Gulf of Carpentaria, and after a perilous journey through the great drought,reached Adelaide. after thirteen months of trivel. Mr. Boothby's only companion was his privs'e secre- tary, Mr. C. T. Longley -Taylor.</p>
        <p>Professor Robert Kenna way Douglas, who com- mences a course of three lectures on " Modern Society in China " at the Royal Institution this afternoon, was born in the year of her Majesty's coronation, and has been in the public service for thirty-five years. He was in the British Consular service in China for six years, being appointed act- ing vice-consul at Taku in 1862,a year or two after the famous capture of the forts by the British and French allies. Since his return in 1864 he has been in charge of the Chinese department at the British Museum. He is of more than the average height, clean shaven, with the true look of the student,and a slight tendency to stoop. His style of dress is of that easy cut which is affected by art professors and Mark-lane corn dealers. He is very chummy and genial with those who are fortunate enough to be his intimates, and does a good deal of magazine writing on him special subject.</p>
        <p>Mr. It. W. Perks, M.P., who presided at the annual meeting of the Wesleyan Missionary Society on Monday, is a partner of Mr. Henry Fowler and a soic-in-law of Mr. William Mowburn, of both of whom, curiously enough, thers are portraits in this year's Aeademy. He took honors in classics and modern languages for the London University 8.A., and is very proud of being an associate of the In- stitute of Civil Engineers. He has been profession- ally connected with the Severn Tunnel, the Man- chester Ship Canal, the Inner Circle Railway, the Central Pacific, the Barry Docks, and the under- ground railways of Paris. He is superintendent of a village Sunday school, and as secretary of the Wesleyan Committee of Privileges he has con- trollad the Parliamentary affairs of his communion for many years. He is a measured speaker, who poses as the candid friend at Methodist gatherings, being very intolerant of the effete conservatism of the older party.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Louis Stevenson was still in Sydney at latest mail dates. He has been interviewed there by the " Presbyterian" on the subject of the revival of the traffic in Kanakas for the Queensland sugar plantations. Like all who have been brought into contact with the actual working of the thinly- disguised slavery under the British flag, the popu- lar novelist is of opinion that it cannot be carried on without grave abuses. "I have the greatest difficulty," he says, "in being satisfied with any possible supervision of the labour traffic by the Queensland Government, for this reason. A ship arrives with a Government agent on board in the Western Pacific Islands. It is impossible that be should know one language out of eighty. How can he tell whether these people (the Kanakas) come of their o'wn free will or under the fear of the club and the cooking-oven? He never can. As a matter of fact, if these people have consented at all, they have consented in the single endeavour to save their lives."</p>
        <p>Mr. Stevenson confirms the accuracy of what has been emphatically denied by the apologists for the traffic in England, that after the Kanakas have served them three years on the Queensland planta- tions" they are as often as not returned to the wrong islands, which involves death, and which no government agent is sufficiently well posted up to prevent." Mr. Stevenson adds that it is a" common trick," when it is desired that the Kanakas should re-volunteer for a fresh term of service, to bring them up opposite an enemy's bay and tell them that they are to be landed thrzre. Knowing that they would be killed and eaten if left ashore, they, as the lesser of the two evils, engage to serve for a further term of three years on the Queensland plantations. Mr. Stevenson does not hesitate to say, I believe, in fact, that to-day, and possibly as we sit here talking unvizored, kidnapping is being practised in these islands."</p>
        <p>It is to be feared that Mr. Stevensen, who has spent six years in the South Sea Islands, has taken a more correct view of the situation than Dr. Montgomery, Bishop of Tasmania and son-in-law of Archdeacon Farrar, who recently spent six weeks cruising amongst these same islands, and who so dogmatically declares in the May number of the "New Review " that "the era of outrage and kid- napping and deceit has at length passed away, never to return ;" that " the age of brutality is past, so far as Queensland and Fiji are concerned," and that the "natives understand the whole ques-</p>
      </div>
