      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 6, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 6, 1893.</p>
        <p>tion." Bishop Montgomery is hardly justified in basing such a pyramid of bold assertions on the hasty superficial observations of a flying excur- sion trip. • ANECDOTES OF TWO IRONMASTERS. The miserable death of young Baird, at New Or- leans, the other day, has revived many stories of that relative of his who, many years ago, to the sur- prise of all who knew him, endowed the Church of Scotland with half a million sterling. The day after the gift was male known Mr. Baird was met in the streets of Glasgow by another great ironmaster, Merry, 11. P., for the Falkirk Burghs, and the con.veras- tion which followed between them showed that both knew more about pig-iron than Scripture. "Why, Baird," exclaimed Mr. Merry, " they tell me you've given half a million to the Kirk. I didn't know you were a religious man. I'll bet you five pounds you can- not repeat the Lord's Prayer.' The story goes that Mr. , Baird accepted the bet, and immediately began to re- I cite the metrical version of the twenty-third Psand, upon finishing which Mr. Merry said, •` Well, I didn't think you could have done it," and handed over the money. Once, upon buying an estate, which included a castle, which contained a large room fitted up as a library, it was represented to Mr. Baird that the shelves ought to be filled with books. He accordingly gave an order to Messrs. Blackwood, the eminent publishers,whose repre- sentative said," I suppose, Mr. Baird, you will have the books bound in morocco." " Morocco ! ' exclaimed the benefactor of the Church ; "no, they must be bound in Edinburgh !" Mr. Merry ran his friend very close in matters of this kind, and knew much more about the Racing Calendar than the Pentateuch. Once, when be- ing heckled during an election, a man suddenly shouted from the gallery, " What is Mr. Merry's opinion of the Decalogue ?" The candidate, turning to his agent, whom he always kept handy at such times' asked," What on earth does the fellow mean by the Decalogue ?" The agent explained that the man probably meant some- thing about Sunday trains and Sunday travelling, upon which the candidate replied to his questioner as follows: —" I beg to inform my friend in the gallery that so far as I am 4 I would abolish the Decalogue al- together.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER,.. INIARB92 NZLLIn, • I have just heard of a somewhat novel ides for the suppression of the crinoline, and although, as I have already informed you, there Is little to fear In the way of a survival of what was termed "The Crinoline Scare," yet the novelty of the idea •• hoidens me to repeat it. It is of America or _ and the principal factors in it are young men. It see= that an Anti-Crinoline Club has been formed by the young men on the other side of the Atlantic and the rules are very strict. No young man who joins the Club will be permitted to escort any lady wearing a crinoline to a theatre, conoert, euchre, or dancing party, or any public or private enter- tainment whatever. No...member will be permitted to call on any lady who receives her visitors in a hoop shirt. At social gatherings it is forbidden to members to dance or talk with any wearers of crinoline. Only the baldest greetings are admitted. Members meeting crinolined acquaintances in the street are desired to become suddenly interested in the overhead wires. The most popular hats at present are of coarsest straw, in a lovely shade of rush-green, a colour which seems to harmonise wonderfully with any and every costume. All the large hats I have seen appear to have hiuh standing clusters of foliage, or small, sharp velvet ears erected exactly over each of the wearer's eyes, giving a most curious effect. One pretty green straw hat in this style has a wide, straight brim, with a jam-pot crown of fine black chip some five inches high. Round the crown is a thick ruche of the most natural-looking violets. At the back of the hat is a small cluster of violets, under the brim, to rest on the hair, and in front are two little high clusters of violet foliage, rising from dainty rosettes of violet-hued velvet. All the hats and bonnets, however, are harmonies of rich glowing tones in flowers, velvets, and straws. Indeed no hat seems complete at present without at least four different colours, the flowers as often as not in two more, and further varieties are introduced by ribbop and lace. The result in the hands of an noskilled milliner is truly terrible.</p>
        <p>"</p>
        <p>40' em.</p>
        <p>origin-, I</p>
        <p>One of prettiest picturesque hat I have seen this 13011110122WW1 of fancy lace straw. twisted In a most becoming fashion, and trimmed with daisies and iris and a band and bow of violet ribbonOhot with green. Shot and shaded, shaded and elicit, is the key. note of all our newest wear at present. Tit I Park was prismatic on Sunday with every primitive colour that ever shone out of a raimbow. And, believe me, those new combinations are unfeignedly hideous. Shot materials are all very well if con. fined to two colours. But this latest fashion ol putting so many hues into one poor yard of,mater Is overdoing it more than somewhat.</p>
        <p>At exceedingly pretty and well.finished dress, which I saw this week, had flaps or basquee drooping from the sides of the bodice over the hips; the two pieces of the back finish the point a little beyond the waist under a bow, and thus is displayed in the wide and double box-pleat of the trained skirt.</p>
        <p>The blouse stands pre-eminent in relation to all the useful and ornamental dainties offered to the public this season, and never before has a more comfortable or a more charming, garment existed. For river and country wear the cambric shirt ha* few equals; while for home, evenings, and theatre going there can be nothing nicer than its more elaborate bodice of gathered crepe or tucked silk. The very newest style of blouse has its draperies crossed over the figure in front, and carried to the back, where it is tied in. ea assertive-looking bow.</p>
        <p>In several of my recent letters to you, I have referred to the variety of novelties in children's dresses, but one of the prettiest little coat I have ever seen was of fancy white cloth with full sleeves of Bengaline and large bow and ends of moir4 rib- bon. A neat and taking Bonnet of white Benga- line, trimmed with ostrich tip and fancy ribbon. Gives a pretty finish to the dresp. The coat can also be made entirely of Beogaline, or a thinner material could be used.</p>
        <p>Among an almost endless variety of outdoor gar. ments which I saw recently VILIS a very stylish coat, lightly made, yet haviug a certain amount of that warmth which is necessary for some time to come. It was made of the new kid-cloth in a shade known as toast-brown; the skirt was full and the waist set in tine gathers, the triple collar of velvet being three shades darker than the cloth. The velvet sleeves were very full and there were wide Directoire lapels of velvet. A cream-coloured cloth waistcoat, handsomely embroidered in shades of brown and gold silk, is to be worn with this coat on a warm day, or it could be fastened up close to the throat. when the wind was cold. This design will look well in two shades of heliotrope, the lighter shape in cloth, the darker velvet, or in tan- olour, with emerald-green velvet.</p>
        <p>Yours very sincerely, JENNM.</p>
        <p>better), and half a teacupful of cold water. Boil well, and keep stirring all the time. Chocolate Drobs. 41In Scrape 2 oz. of chocolate to a powder, an add to i 1 lb. of sugar (sifted is best). Make into a paste with water, and pour into a pan. When it almost boils, take from the fire and keep stirring until it is of the desired thickness. Drop on a buttered plate, allow it to cool, and remove with a knife.</p>
        <p>Melted Butter.</p>
        <p>Put 1 oz. of butter in a small sauizepan. When melted add 4 oz. of flour; stir over the lire one minute to cook the flour; add 4 pint of water by degrees ; stir until it boils and thickens; boil gently three minutes, stir all the time, or it will burn ; stir only one way, or it will curdle; add a little salt, and pour in a basin. Time, ten minutes. Chocolate Almonds. "12`11 "H P Take 4 lb. of good cake chocolate (broker's Into small nieces), and put it into a hot metal mortar ; beat it up well into a paste, and add 4 lb. of caster sugar. Blanch and dry 4 lb. of sweet almonds, and cover each almond with the chocolate paste. Place the almonds on paper until cold. Cost, is. 4d.; enough of 1 lb. of sweets.</p>
        <p>The Coming Parasol. Lace and ribbon are the most popular trimmings for more elaborate parasols, and pretty and novel arrange- ments abound. Extremely pretty parasols are made of white silk or satin, with two flounces of eight or nine- inch wide lace, and inch-wide satin ribbon to match. One flounce is sewn on round the edge of the parasol, and has ribbon run through it about half-way up; the other flounce is gathered up into a fan pleating in the centre of each panel, and is also run with ribbon. Knots of ribbon on the gathers and at the top complete the decoration. These same parasols are made in coloured silk or satin, with ribbon to match, and black lace. The prettiest of all the new parasols (says "Myra's Journal") are these that are composed of light-coloured silk, with bouillones at goffered crepe at the top, round the centre, and at the edge above a lace flounce. The soft creme forms puffs that at a little distance look like small feather tips; the parasols are dainty and elegant beyond description.</p>
        <p>Ten Good Things to Know. 1. That milk, which is turned or changed, may be sweetened and rendered fit for use again by stirring in a little soda.</p>
        <p>2. That salt will curdle new milk; hence, in prepar- ing milk porridge, gravies, &amp;c., the salt should not be added until the dish is prepared. 3. That fresh meat, after beginning to sour, will sweeten if placed out of doors in the cool of the night. 4. That clear boiling water will remove many fruit stains and tea stains. Pour the water through the stain, and thus prevent it spreading over the fabric. 5. That a tablespoonful of turpentine boiled with white clothes will aid in the whitening process. 6. That ripe tomatoes will remove ink and other stains from white cloth; also from the hands.</p>
        <p>7. That boiled starch is much improved by the addi- tion of a little sperm salt, or gum arabic dissolved. 8. That beeswax and salt will make rusty fiat-irons as clean and smooth as glass. Tie a lump of wax in a rag and keep it for that purpose. When the irons are hot, rub them first with the wax rag, then scour with paper or cloth sprinkled with salt. 9. That blue ointment and kerosene, mixed in equal proportions and applied to the bedsteads, is an unfailing bed-bug remedy, as a coat of whitewash is for the walls of a log house. 10. That kerosene will soften bocts and shoes that have been hardened by water, and render them as pli- able as when new.</p>
