      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>concerned</head>
        <p>concerned</p>
        <p>BIG BANK NOTES.</p>
        <p>An American humourist has recently demonstrat- ed, at least to his own satisfaction, that it is possible for a man to live comfortably in London for thirty days merely upon the reputation of being the proud possessor of that inconvenient token of wealth, a bank note for £1,000,000 sterling. It is said that two notes for £lOO,OOO each, and two for £50,000 each, were once engraved and issued. A butcher who had amassed an immense fortune as an army contractor in war time went with one of these £50,000 notes to a private banker, asking for a loan of £5,000, and wished to deposit the large note as security with the hank, stating that it had been in his possession for several years. The ,sum asked for, was of course, handed over, but the financier took occasion to hint to the holder, the folly of which he was guilty in hoarding , such a sum, and so sacrificing the interest. " That is all very true, and sound sense, sir," replied the man, " but I likes the look o' the critter so wery well that I have got Vother one of the same kind at borne." A wealthy but eccentric gentleman in London once framed a bank-post bill for £30,000, and exhibited it in his study. At his death which occurred five years later , the extraordinary picture was promptly taken down trona the wall and cashed by his h:irs. It is said that sever al years ago, at a nobleman's house in the neighbourhood of the Marble Arch, a dispute arose about a certain pas- sage which was declared to be scriptural. A learned dean who was present denying that there was any such tcxt in the Bible, the sacred volume was called for. After considerable search, a dusty old Bible, which had lain on the shelf since the death of the peer's mother, several years before, was produced. When the volume was opened a book-marker was found in it, which, upon examination, proved to be a bank-post bill for £40,000. Why it had been placed there was never discovered ; perhaps tne old lady had thought it a good means of inducing her son to search the Scriptures.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CEMETERY KEEPER SUMMONED. A SUNDAY AFTERNOON INCIDENT. At Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday before Major Hazlehurst, Mr. C. Reynolds, and Mr. James Handley, William Ford, keeper of the cemetery, was summoned for assaulting James Graham Hut- ton, a clerk in the employ of Manchester Ship Canal Co., residing at 62, Greenway-road. Mr. E. W. Humphreys appeared for the complainant and Mr. J. Burton for the defendant.—The complainant stated that on Sunday afternoon the 12th March, he and a friend named Mr. Corbett entered the cemetery smoking and, the defendant said, drew their attention to the fact that smoking was not allowed in the cemetery on Sundays, and they both pleaded ignorance of that fact and put their pipes out. He went to his father's grave and there observed a broken jar lying by the side of the grave. As the jar , disfigured the grave he threw it against the wall, whete there was a stretch of soil planted with shrubs, but no graves. The defendant's son same to them and said "I have been watching you two men and if you are not careful I will make an example of you." He (witness) threw the jar where he did because he knew of no other place. As he and his friend were after- wards passing out of the cemetery, they were stopped by the defendant and his son, the former telling him that if the jar wanted removing, he should have spoken to hini,add be threatened to have them both locked up there and then. At last he (wit- ness) lost patience and said " Come along, it's no use trying to argue with a couple of fools," whereupon Ford seized him by the collar ot his coat and tie, and turned him round. He held him that way for two or three minutes. He told Mr. Corbett to fetch a policeman. There were a great many people in the cemetery and in the road at the time.—Nathaniel Bracegirdlesaid he saw the defendant put his hand on Mr. Hutton's shoulder, and hold him for a couple of minutes.—Mr. Burton con- tended that what had taken place did not constitute an assault as there was no evidence to show that there was an intention to do bodily injury. The complainant did not pretend to say he was injured ; it was merely his dignity that had suffered. He had appealed to the Burial Board and had received a polite reply from Mr. Salkeld, but because they had not instantly dismissed a servant who had acted 25 years without complaint, or taken some other severe steps, he came to that court and asked the Bench to become the instruments of his vindic- tive feeling.—Mr. William Ford, jun., said the jar was not lying on the grave of complainant's father, but alongside, and it was not broken, until complainant placed it on the end of his stick, trespassed across the grass plot and smashed it into five or tix pieces against the wall of the shrubbery. He told complainant it was nGt a suitable place. At the gate his father asked com- plainant for his address, but he refused to give it, and his father got hold of the collar of his coat. It was not true that he seized hold of his tie.—Samuel Stubbs, said he had seen the defendant take hold of the complainant's csat collar, and hold hire for about half a minute. His tie was not torn nor disarranged in the least.—The Bench dismissed the case.—Mr. Burton asked the Bench to order complainant to pay half of the advocate's fee, but they declined saying that it was not the custom of the court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>RUNCORN WATER BILL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN WATER BILL.</p>
        <p>This Bill came on Monday before the unopposed committee of the House of Lords, the Earl of Mor- ley residing. The object of the Bill is to author- ise the Runcorn Improvement Commissioners to acquire the undertaking of the Runcorn, Weston and Halton Waterworks Company. Formal proof having been given, the noble chairman went through the clauses of the Bill. He said there was a long report from the Local Government Board in regard of clause 8.</p>
        <p>The Agent said the were very anxious that tlie rating clause should not be altered in any way. If they were touched they should be re-enacted throughout, which would involve some alteration in the rates. They were quite prepared to accept them as they were at present. It would be hard upon them if their rating powers were reduced.</p>
        <p>The Chairman thought it best to leave it to the Local Government Board to take their own course. He next struck out the clause relating to the payment of direc- tors.</p>
        <p>The agent next urged that they should be empowered to borrow £O.OOO, instead of £75.000. This applica- tion was refused, the words "such sums as are neces- sary for the purpose " being inserted in the clause. After some further amendments of minor importance, the chairman allowed the Bill to pass through ccmmittee. It will accordingly be reported, in due course, for second reading.</p>
        <p>THE BEST NIGHTHAP,—Have your sheets washed with Hudson's Soap, and they will always be sweet as roses, fresh as sea breezes. There will be none of the disagreeable smell, so objectionable in other soaps.—A pure Dry Soap in fine powder.—Advt. (2). At the annual meeting of the Scottish Football Associ- ation,held in Glasgow on Tuesday night,it was agreed by a large majority to legalise professionalism. The repre- sentative of the Celtic Club, who moved the successful proposition, said Scotch football had all along been steeped in organised hypocrisy. PUBLIC NOTICE.—Not one kilt HUNDREDS of people of both sexes with wrecked and debilitated constitutions are yearly restored to sound and robust health again by Messrs. Wilkinson. 3 and 4, Farallon Square, Sheffield. Write to them for advice free when suffering trom weakness, loss of flesh, appetite, disturbed rest, pimples, blotches, indiges- tion, pallid skin, nervousness, and general deblaty. Posi- tive cure for all Blood and Skin Diseases, acquired or consti- tutional. Sixty years' experience. One address (as above) for over 30 years. The' A 1 Mixture," 45. 9d, the case. The Magic Mirror in envelope, four stamps. Sufferers disap- pointed with Elsctric Belts should write to us at once.— Advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>DARKWOOD.</head>
        <p>DARKWOOD.</p>
        <p>A TALE ON LONEMOOR.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXVIII (Continued).</p>
        <p>While this was being done, Gwen and the Lady Georgina were returning to the apartments of the former. They passed up the grand staircase side by side, walked down the length of the great upper ball and were about to turn into tbe corridor that led to their own hall, when they met Pietro, the Maltese valet, face to face. , He stared at Gwen much as his master had done. She passed on with the Lady Georgina. He hal- ted and looked after her with a curious, catlike con- traction of his eyelids. "Who is she ?" he asked himself. "Not the expected governess. There's no governess about her. She looks like the lady, and the other looks her maid. She's some visitor, I suppose." The girls entered the second hall and disappeared from his view; but still he stood there as if entranced.</p>
        <p>, "Something in that girl's face struck me," he mutteced. "Perhaps it is because I notice all young , girls now. There can be no other reason. lam always thinking of Miss Markham's child, who must be something of the age of these young women. I wonder I don't hear from my advertisement. I won- der that Miss Winter' does not communicate with me through my brother. I shall have to send another and different advertisement to the London newspapers."</p>
        <p>He-moved slowly towards his master's rooms just as Lord Darkwood ascended the stairs, He halted again as the marquis approached. "Who was the young lady, signor P" he demanded. with the familiarity which characterised his man- ner towards his master when they were alone together. "You saw her?" said Lonl Doi wood. "She is Miss Myner, my daughter's gamma as" "She a governess Pietro was amazed.</p>
        <p>"You see, my lord," he continued, "she does not look like a governess. I thought her some great lady at the very least." The marquis regarded the Maltese's sleek dark face searchingly. Had Pietro detected any resemblance between this girl and the Charteris family? Evidently not. Lord Darkwood breathed a sigh of relief as Pietro walked on, and continued his own course to the picture gallery, which comprised the third floor of the wing which was occupied. Upon one side of the gallery were hung the family por- traits of the last representatives of the family. One of these representatives had been his uncle, the other had been his cousin, whom he had succeeded in the title. He studied these two portraits closely. The faces of both were fair and haughty, with regular features and noble expression—commanding faces, very like each other in all salient points.</p>
        <p>"How foolish I am I" said the marquis to himself. "I must be mad. How could I have thought that Miss Myner resembled this family? And if she had the resemblance would be nothing. It would be singular indeed if, out of all the people on this earth, no two not akin were alike. Fair people always look more or less like each other. I don't know what idea cduld have got into my head. In fact I hav'n't had a clear idea why I was uneasy." He laughed softly, shaking off the last vestige of anxiety. He walked the length of the great gallery, exulting in the possession of its store of riches, and then descended to the library, again in good humour.</p>
        <p>" This little incident," he mused, taking his seat by a table, "reminds me that somewhere in Eng- land a girl probably lives whose existence is inim- ical to me. I do not know her name or station; but Clare Markham's child whether servant or teacher, whether ignorant or wellbred, is, while she lives, a source of peril to me." . _</p>
        <p>He was very thoughtful for a long time, his face hardening, his mouth set in a grim and hard expres- sion.</p>
        <p>"If I thought that Pietro went into Yorkshire in search of her when he was absent," he finally mut- tered, "I could kill him. Yet very probably he went there. There's no honour among thieves, the adage to the contrary. We have done some evil things together, he knows me pretty thoroughly, and I know him for a double-faced, treacherous rascal, who would even slip a knife under my fifth rib if my death were likely to advantage him more than my life. It is like him to go to Yorkshire to search for the girl. How tlo I know that he didn't do it 7"</p>
        <p>He failed to reassure himself on that point. The longer he reflected, the more convinced he became that Pietro was playing a double game. "I will go to Yorkshire myself secretly," he resolved.</p>
        <p>"I will catch Pietro. lam standing on a preci- pice." He pondered the question, and the more he thought the more he was convinced of the treachery of the valet.</p>
        <p>"He has been writing often to his brother of late," muttered Lord Darkwood. "I saw ink on his finger when he dressed me not two hours since. He must have been writing again to some one. Has he commissioned his brother to search for that girl, to go to Yorkshire, to send particulars in regard to her? If he has written, his letters cannot be gone The letters are dropped into the box and are sent to the post very early in the morning, to catch the mail for London. The letter is likely to be in the box. Why should I not see it? I must know if Pietro is trying to find the girl." He arose and went to the door, listening. All was still in the great hall without. He turned the knob and went into the hall. The lights were burning; the ball porter sat in his tall- backed chair, dozing. Lord Darkwood crossed the floor silently—he always stepped like a cat—and paused before a small bronze box surmounting a pedestal, and resembling closely the street pillar boxes of London. This pillar box was very ornarn- enta‘ land was quite a feature in the hall. His lord- ship possessed one key to it, the butler, who sorted out the letters, sending them away in the leathern postbag, having possession of the other. The marquis had his key in his hand. He softly Inserted it in the lock and opened the box. There were several letters within—some that he had written; one, the address of which was in the handwriting of Mrs. Dover: two or three rudely scrawled addresses had evidently been the hand work of the Lady Georgina, and one or two others had been dropped in by servants. Last of all, he picked up a thin blue missive addressed to " Tomas° Tecino, No. 37, Blank-street, Soho-square, London, We"( He slipped this letter into his pocket. Then he locked the box and silently returned to the library, without having awakened the hall poiter, or having been observed by anyone. _ .</p>
        <p>"I shall proceed. to solve my doubts of Pietro," he remarked, locking his door and taking his seat at his desk. If be is true to me, so much the better. If he i playing any game—and he's sharp enough to do it—l must he on my guard. I shall find that girl myself and dispose of her. not spend another day in a fool's paradise, not !mowing whether or no a sword is suspended above my bead. But before I look for the girl prow: Ptc.ro. Now for it !"</p>
        <p>Ile moistened a sponge in a glass of water, and delicately manipulated the back of the envelope. Under the application of moisten', he presently, with a paper-cutter, lifted the flap ot the envelope, and Pietro's letter lay open to his gaze.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXIX. VERIFIED.</p>
        <p>The Maltese valet had not dispia‘l ed his usual caution and sagacity, it may be thougio, in deposit- ing his letter to his brother—if that letter were important—in the hall pillar-post box. But it must be remembered that he supposed his plans sufficiently secret to escape even the suspic- ions of his master. He had never ft,reseett that Lord Darkwood might choose to inspect the con- tents of the box, and that his lordship would even think of examining his humble correspomimee. It was a distance of eight or nine miles to Shrewsbury, and he could not think of carrvin4 his letters thither to post. He deposited them, us a matter of course, in the usual receptacle plovided for inmates of the castle, and dismissed the subject from his mind.</p>
        <p>Yet that letter betrayed more of his secret pur- poses than was wise to trust to pen aud paper. Fortune, as usual, had favored Lord Darkwood.</p>
        <p>The letter was long, written in a spidery sort of penmanship, upon a thin yet strong blue paper that crackled under the touch. Lord Darkwood understood the Italian language, and readily trans- lated the document.</p>
        <p>"It is strange, Tomaso," read the second para- graph of the effusion, "that the advertisement hat brought no answer. I look for a letter from you with news of her by every post. Is it too soon to expect one Possibly the advertisement is not strong enough. Let us write another. Send to aU the morning papers this notice which I here write. " Information wanted of Miss Gwendoline Win- ter, who left Yorkshire recently for London. If Miss Winter will communicate with T. T., No. 37, Blank-street, Soho-square, London, W., she will bear something greatly to her advantaze from one</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>who knows her whole history, and can help her to find her relatives.' "This, Tomas°, is an improvement upon the notice we first had published. H she has any desire to solve the mystery that surrounds her life from its commencement until now, she will apply to T. T.'</p>
        <p>"Let this notice be published, and as soon as you hear from Miss Winter communicate with me. If she gives you her name and address, telegraph me that you are very. ill—had , a relapse—and I will come on post haste., Mind, and be as secret as the grave. If my lord , suspects we are lost, for he would find the girl antlremove her, and the fortune I expect to win through her would slip through my grasp. My lord never reads the -advertisement , columns, so he'll not see the notice: Be cautious. Tomaso ; ba secret, be_watchfuL"</p>
        <p>There was more in the same strainv but we have the more important portions. Lord Darkwood fairly devoured the words with his eyes, and when he had ,finished he read the tette] anew, and sat afterwards in., a dead silence, his face overspread with that singular grey pallor we have before described, and his eyes staring with a strange Intentness straight before him. "The traitorous wretch!" he muttered, after a long silence. "He went to Yorkshire I He found out that the girl had gone to London! She calls herself Gwendoline Winter! And this Gwendoline Winter is Clare Markham's child!"• There was much fdoa ...10-efleetion in those few brief sentences. Theft animpling almost paralyzed him The girl—Clare Markham's child— still lived, was grown to girlhood, was in London. Perhap&amp; she was a servant, ignorant, inbred, stupid perhaps. So he reasoned.</p>
        <p>A cold sweat started to his forehead. A sense of guilt oppressed him. He knew, the secret history of this Gwendoline Winter. Clare,Markham's awful death lay at his door. He fanced that Clare Mark ham's eyes stared reproachfully at him from the shadows around him. He looked over his shoulder in a nervousness such as he had never before felt, half expecting to see Clare Markham's ghost at his elbow. "What a fool I am growing l" he whispered. "A downright idiot! strange that old stories heard in childhood should crop up in one's mind to frighten one. Ghosts! Bah 1" and he laughted boisterously. "Care Markham is buried a good six feet under the ground—she won't start up to frighten me or to protect the child. The girl—l must think of her. She must be close upon seventeen. What can Pietro want of her ?" He debated the question carefully. "He expects to find her a poor servant," he mused, "ignorant and lowbred. Upon my soul I believe he means to marry her! By heaven, if be were to marry her it would be all up with me." Again he wiped his forehead vigorously. "He will find her, unless [get her out of his way. How can I find her first? His letter must go to his brother; but I open the postbag every morning, and I can intercept the answer and read it. Tomaso shall play into my hands. Yes, the letter must go:" _ _ _</p>
        <p>He took it up, applied some mucilage to the flap of the envelope, and resealed the missive. He had done his work cleverly. It did not appear to have been tampered with. Then, losing no time,. he hurried into the hall and slipped the letter into the box. He paced the library with rapid steps. As his thoughts became clearer, he saw that he was menaced with a great danger. This girl—Gwendo- line Winter—might in hands like those ot Pietro, prove his ruin. His features contracted into a sin- ister hardness, his eyes gleamed wickedly, he drew his breath loud and hard.</p>
        <p>"I must get the girl into my hands," he deter- mined. "I will remove her to a place where none will ever find her. If she were out of his reach Pietro would be faithful to me, and I should have nothing to fear." _ .</p>
        <p>Ile walked to and fro. with increasing rapidity. He seemed on fire within, He had felt so secure• until now—secure in the midst of danger. He • seemed to have awakened. suddenly out of peace-, fill sleep, to find himself environed with perils. Ih.cv dear wore were all, these luxuries to him— his title and honours, his, stately castle, his maDY servants, his tenantry, his wide lands. Was there a possibility that these would be Nvrested from• his grasp? Nay, rather than give them up, lie would sell his. soul to Satan. He would commit any crime—he would riSk any penalty—but he would never willingly go • back to his old life, his barracks in Malta, his.debts„his many annoyances - and privations. "Yet Pietro knows so much about me—things in. which he had no part—that he could have me sent to- prison," he muttered. "I know that he would not d .re proceed against me—l know this—unless he mites forces with that girl to crush me. He • shall not find her. By heaven! I wish I had her in this room at this moment unknown to anyone else. I'd kill her l"</p>
        <p>_ ..... . He hissed the words, between his teeth, and a, murderous fury gleamed. in his small, crafty eyes. And the girl of whom he thus spoke had entered his house that very evening as governess to his- daughter. It was well for Gwen that he did not. suspect her identity. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"It will be three days before this advertisement of Pietro's can appear in the newspapers," be- thought, "and another day must pass before ho• can possibly bear from his brother. In four days E can do much. I will pretend that lam summoned to London. go to Yorkshire, and it will go hard with me but that I get the girl's addr-ess. I'll find • her before the advertisement even appears. And when I find her I'll sweep her from my path as-1 swept her mother before her."</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
        <p>NEW BOOKS, &amp;c. The most comprehensive, and complete edition eve! issued of " Coleridge's Poetical, and Dramatic Works'', has just been published by Macmillan and Co., ape edited, with an extensive biographical introduction, 11 James Dykes Campbell. Among the appendices will be found the original versions of several poems, which in their earliest form possess an independent interest—; sometimes personal, sometimes artistic. The " Notes are voluminous, but in order that the reader may not be unnecessarily or unwillingly disturbed by them, (and _Airi some books they are obtrusive), the editor has pla:ce" them at the end of the volume. The frontispiece 33 portrait of the poet, reproduced directly from ene 0, Peter Vandyke's oil paintings, now in the Nation a,; Portrait Gallery. The price of the book is 7s. 6d.i a° is one that should find a place in everybody's collectlo° • of the Poets.</p>
