        <head>EWE NEW 13AT1tS.</head>
        <p>EWE NEW 13AT1tS.</p>
        <p>Councillor Dram, in acoerdance vkth a notion of motion attired the Chairman ol the Towu'Hall and•Daths Committee to fix the date ,when'the new baths will be opened. Alderman OILANDLEY moved the adoption of the 'Town Hall Park and Baths Committee and said heewould answer Councillor Dixon later on. Councillor BOLTON seconded the minutes. Councillor rFienernuort asked about the enlargement of the Town Hall. He believed that the Ccymenittee had been asked to build offices for the ,clerks ended:lectors connected with the Water Works inepartment and the Gas WorkstDepartment. He thought that as the Com- mittee had been unable to offer anything in the way of site to Mr. Parr, who She believed had withdrawn his , offor—,(" No 'I no ")—he did not -see how they could I now begin to offer sites 'for offices. However, 'what struck him as being-the best thing to do • woe to gets scheme for the centralization of She offices at-the Town Hall and then work up to the scheme as thole funds al- lowed. ,(Hear, hear.) CouncillorSmemurse said there was a great deal of talk about the new baths. So far as his observation went, belied seen many a mill that would hold'so,ooo spindles built in less time than bad been taken in build- ing the new baths. (Hear,' hear.) It was abominable, Ito see the thing going on as it was. With regard to the'," ladditional boiler alone, if tthe work had been done by 'private individual, it would have been done in a month,' I whereas this had been going on four or five months.i People were getting anxious for the opening' of the baths. Why there had been' built a cataract in the Park, as big as 'Niagara in a day and a half, cnd it had been christened after Dr. • Cannel% aud yet they bad been all this time halputting the new baths up. (Laughter.) Councillor ROBERTS said he sympathised -with every- thing Councillor Smethurst had said as to the anxiety of the public for the opening of the baths, but he did not think there had teen the amount of neglect ordelay that:, had been suggested. (Hear, hear.) This job was not simply the erection of the new baths, but an alteration and remodelling of the old baths as well, and as practical men would know this involved a lot of •evork—(hear, hear)—which took up considerable time and trouble. He was not surprised that the timE had been as long as.,lt had. The committee ha 4 done all they' could to urge on the contractors, and he did 'not think the principal con- tractors were the sort of men to let the grass grow under their feet. (Hear, hear.) Alderman Surrroe said he did not know what answer was going to he given to' the question, but' although he very much wished in the interests of the public that the baths should be opened at as early a date as possible,he objected to the thing being rushed through to such an extent that the baths would not have fair. play. Rather than have the work rushed through and made a "botch" of,he should prefer that more time should be given to the contractors in which to do it in. He should have been more satisfied had they had more time to consider the whole subject before starting building new baths, and he should have preferred that in addition to the present new plunge bath they had had a Turkish bath. . (Hear, hear.) The only thing that would make the baths pay in the winter time would ben Turkish bath, and as long as they had not this the baths might as well be Closed in the winter. He did not believe that theheths would be anything like they ought to be when they ware finished. He did not believe in their patchwork system of building but in having a thing done completely and properly. The baths had 'been rushed and pushed and. in his opinion they would never be complete as they ought without considerable expense and trouble. Alderman Fnesiooste said he was very meeth surprised to hear Alderman Sutton speak as he had done. They knew he did all he could to atop the new baths from being built. (Alderman Sutton: io, i did not). Ile said they were good enough for him 50 years ago, and they ought to be good enough for them in this genera- tion. (A Councillor They were not huilt 50 years ago). He could understand Councillor Smethurst saying the people were anxious for the baths to be opened. ,It was quite natur3l. He did not know how Aldennan.Sutton could call the work that was being done patch work. The new bath would be second to none in the country, and he had no doubt it would be a great success and a great boon to the people. As for Turkish baths the only reason the Committee did not go in for them was that they had not the money. Every member of the .Com- mittee saw the necessity of going in for them if they could have found .the money. But it was well ,known to every member of the Council that they were going ahead at a very rapid rate, and it was necessary for them to be careful in the spending of money if they were not to unduly increase the rates of the town. (Hear, hear). Councillor Fairclough had alluded to the en- largement of the Town Hall, to provide accommodation for the clerks of the water and gas department. Well, he did not think the Council would be justified in going into any elaborate scheme for the enlargement.of the Town Hall, for such a scheme would cost £20,000 or £30,000. It was one of those matters in which it was necessary for them to move slowly. (Hear, hear.) It was an error on the part of Councillor Fairclough to say that Mr. Parr had withdrawn his offer, for he believed he would build a new Public Hall in a very prominent and central position in the town—(hear, hear)—where there were plenty of wide streets, the prsper place for such a building, and not at the Town Hall. (Hear, hear.) Councillor BOLTON remarked that the present rate of progress at the new baths might suit some old fogies (laughter)—but the time for the ending of the contract' was last January, and why the baths had not been finished he did not know. The Borough Surveyor saidit' was partly owing to the frost, but he thought there had, been a public-house or two to be built. (Laughter.) Time men were taken away from the baths and put on to a house in Battersby-lane,and there was only one man left' at the baths. (" Oh.") The builders were to blame and not the committee. el. Councillor : Enforce the penalty. ) He would be one to enforce the penalty, because they ought to be having revenue from the baths. Committer Mum said the Committee had found that a lot of bad weather had interfered with the progress of the wotk. Alderman CHANDLET said he was puzzled what to say. One member said the baths had been neglected and the cy)rk was behindhand, while another said the work had been rushed and pushed. The fact was that the baths had progressed as reasonably as could be expected under the circumstances. There had been various causes con- tributing to their non-compketion ere now. The ques- tion as to the line of demarcation was one great point they had to,contend with in the first instance. Another cause of delay was the weather, and altogether, consid- ering that the men of the present day were very difficult to manage, they had got along as fairly perhaps as they could reasonably expect. With regard to the date of opening the baths, after consultation with the borough surveyor and conversation with the contractor, he had every reason to believe that the baths might be opened on the 24th May. (Hear, hear.) They did not commit themselves absolutely to any specific date, but as far as: they could see there was nothing to prevent the opening. being fixed in that week. The minutes were then put and passed.</p>
        <p>adoptien ,c,f the</p>
        <p>the'</p>
        <p>tion dleould be prodded for all the staffs of the Corpora; Viilns sood as posoible. Ratepayers should be able to. pay their gas and water rates in the name building. -(Hear, hear.) Councillor R. T. FAIRCLOIWK proposed that the resolu- tion on pagelll of the agenda paper Should read asiol- llows :—" That the Town Hall, Park, and Baths Com- Imittee be requested to make provision at the Town Hall for the clerks and collectors of thisilepartment, but that a completaschemo for enlarging should first tic sub- mitted." The TOWN CLERK: You cannot Tut the'amendrnent in that way. Councr‘or FAIRCLOUCiII : Then move that the minute be rescinded. The Town CLERK : The lease of the water works offices,will expire before long. Coutmillor R. T. Faineuition-: You should first have a complete scheme for enlarging the Town"flall, and then enlarge by sections as necessary. (Hear, hear.) I move that the resolution on page 11 be struck out until a prope,' scheme is brought forward. Alderman CHANDLEY: : clf toe resolution is allowed to stand, it will come before the Town Hall, Park and Baths Committee, and then, I presume, a report must be erade to the Council.</p>
        <p>RIRE ENGINE ,COMMITTEE. The minutes of the above committee contained the following :—" That an advertisement for a new steam fire engine, capable of deliverisg 450 gallons per minute, be inserted in the' Engineer' and the 'lronmonger.'" Councillor HU22:IIIINSON moved, and Councillor Kum sewn ded, the passing of the minutes. Alderman CHANDIBY asked whether the tesolution relat lug to a new steam fire engine had been thoroughly considered. If they could do without a new engine they should not purchase one ; if they really required one would be thelast to raise apposition. The'TowN CLERK: It is not a purchase; it is only advertising for tenders. (Laughter.) • Alderman CHANDLEI-: With the intention of buying. Ssmarat MEMBERS: Yes. Alderman WRIGHT: I presume it means business. The Town CLIME: To ascertain prices. AldermanCnAkmom That won't do for sne.Either you are net going to buy an engine or you do not intend to have one. I should like to know what you mean. (Hear, hear.) Alderman SUTTON: It caanat be otherwise. Alderman CEtaNDLET: I move as an amendment that the consideration of the matter be deferred. Councillor Bovrox seconded, and asked if it was in- tended to keep two engines, or would a better one be obtained and the old one sold. Councillor Ra.rocnrs understood that if the committee could purchase a new engine at a satisfactory price they intended to do so. He supported the suggestion most cordially. It was within the range of possibility that they might have two fires in Warrington at the same time, requiring all the power they could bring to bear to save life and property, and the cost of a second engine under such circumstances was nothing. Suppose one of the engines broke down ? He understood there was a considerable sum of money in hand towards the cost of the purchase of the second enaine. Alderman IlmmEs.—£2.6B. The cost of the engine will be about £5OO. Alderman SUTTON and Councillor Dixow supported the amendment, and after considerable discussion the matter was put to the vote and the original minutes carried by a large majority. WATER COMMITTEE. The minutes of the Water Committee contained a re- solution to the effect that provision should be made at the Town Hall for the clerks and collectors of the Water Department. ' Alderman FnANCOMB proposed, and Councillor SnAw GREEN seconded the confirmation of the minutes, the latter remarking that ha thought proper accomraoda,</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: They mug do so. Councillor R. T. PAM:LOUGH : I am 'quite satisfied , with the explanation. he Town CLEtor 'You don't propose an amend-1 went ? "Councillor FAIT/CD:MR : No, I don't. 'Councillor BOLTON said the resolution on page 11 was aiittle bit impudent. "The water and gas were bushiest concerns,and it appeared that the respective committees -wanted to get out Of graying rent for their respective :6,.-Tices. (Laughter and cries of "No, no.") Councillor Ronaters, protested against Councillor BOA- -ion's remarks, in which he made an insinuation that the 'committee wanted to be supplied with offices for nothing. They were quite prepared to pay a rent. It was arbso- ,lutely essential that the control of the finances shoeld come directly into the hands of the Borough Treasurer. 'Councillor Fairclouglic motion was one which had-the moral support of every one of the Corporation,and what they wanted was to get a scheme which would dd the greatest amount ofwwork at the least possible cast. (Hear, hear.) lii answer te Aklernasn Sutton, the Town CLARE" said the lease of the 'Waterworks offices would expire in 1111/.3. • Mclennan SUTTON: Then the suggestion whidi has bec ai made is a fair one. .klderman Faericomrs said it was the feeling of -every min-doer of the Corporation that accommodation should be found for alidepartments. The word "rushing" had be en used. The committee were not yet in a position to go into a very elaborate scheme,:such as Councillor Fair- cl: ough proposed some time ago, that might. cost any. thing from ten thousand to one hundred thousand pounds. Councillor'holton evidently did not minder- stand the word "impudent," or he would not have made use of the expresoion. They had not arrived .at any surggestion which they were able to place before the Cor- p oration. The idea should be, not the success e a par- ticular committee, but the success of the Corporation as a -whole. (Hear, hear.) At the present time :the clerks had no room to carry on their work, and the-members of the committee didot wish to cripple them. They required time for the cormideration of a -Scheme that would meet the requirements of the borough. Councillor FAI4SCIOUCH : I did not propose tospend a :large sum of mosey, I pro posed a certain scheme and that you should work up to it. If you want ,to spend £5OO, spend it, but not in ,s higgledy-piggledylashion. (Hear, hear.) Tne minutes were then p assed. ORNERAGRtURIN )sEs COmmiTTEE. At a meeting of the abovr committee, it was resolved, that, at the request of the Museum, Library,' Arts, and Technical Instruction 'Con itnittee, the sans of £494 19s. 9d.,,being the amount of the Beamont legacy fund, be transferred to the 't picture purchasing account" of that committee.—lt was fur her resolved that the Town , Clerk be instructed to R&amp;M', use for applications for the appointment of a professions il auditor at a salary of £BO per annum. The MAYOR moved, att4 Alderman FnAsarameseconded the adoption of the minutes. Councillor Dr. CARRELL moved an amendment that a professional auditor be ,not ,sppointed. He took that course because they haat...card a. great deal about high rates, and that the rates should be kept down, yet here was an extravagant proposal to pay- some ordinary clerk out of an accountant's aice a suns of £l6O per anntv.n. He did not think that there was ay necessity fov a professional auditer. The amendment not being seconde d fell tothe ground, and the minutes were confirmed. COUNCILLOR DIXON AND THE SMAL LPDXITTERIEIC. Councillor DIXON moved :—" That the hoarding across Dallam-lane, near the smallpox hos pital, be vemoved, also that the Chairman of the flea Ith Committee be requested to reduce the cost of the ho: ipital assnuch as possible." He explained that a nuns ber of incntabitants in the vicinity of the hospital had requ estedhireto move that resolution. He understood that in or to pass, the hospital to •Lengford Works a ry amber of: people climbed over on to the railway, and use d that as road. It was feared that some accident wou Id coccus in that way, so that he took the course he had, lone. The greater part of the members of th eCountr:,l:ncesS rose, and the noise that was created reno 'ered Councillor Dixon's remarks inaudible to the ren minder of the members. No one seconded the motion, winds arootc;- ingly fell through. The Council then rose.</p>
        <p>menl</p>
        <p>U / I</p>
        <p>IRE EXAMINER. SATITRI) AY, MAY 6. 1893. 1A SOCIAL QUESTIONS UNION FOR. WARRINGTON. A meeting, to consider the advisability of form- ing a social questions uniou in Warrington, was held in the Museum Lectere Room, on Monday ' night. There was a good attendance, and the chair was occupied by the Rev, J. Yonge. There were also present the Revs. E. T. Reed. W. H. Drum- mond, 4. W. Varley, P. Ross, T. W. Roberts, Dr. Starkey Smith; Messrs.. Garnett. J.P., K. J. Guest, J.P., T.irchall, J.P., C. H. Kerry, F. Monks, J.P, J. Gandy, W. Diggle, S. B:Stringer, R. Storey, W. Roberts, S. Weaver, J. Price, Jelin Shaw-Green, I. Mounfield, W. Rickman, J. Titethett, J. Taylor, P.C.M, Pick- evince, &amp;c. The CHAIRMAN saidthat he felt some diffidence that evening in assuminh the position as president of the meeting, but as the 'friends who inaugurated the social qircetions union were good enough to ask hirn to take that position, he occupied the chair ufpon the present occasion. He haelereceived a letter from Mr. John Fair- dough explaining that he could not be present that evening, and that he did not quite agree with all the elbjects they had tefore them. He had ilso received a letter from Mr. Thomas Morris. Re always liked Mr. lEdorria's letters, as they were so .helpful, and he 'was always ready to give pecuniary help to a good cause. He thought that the most easeful course for the meeting to follow would be to throw it entirely open for free discussion, For Ws part, he had written a paper on the subject rand he would proceed to read it. They met that evening to explain and advocate a! new movemerit in the town. Some of them were san- guine enough to believe that if they could only secure, for it a general acceptance and cordial sympathy, it was a movement which might more beneficially affect the community than any which has been inaugurated for many years. A feeling has existed in the minds of some for a considerable time that it was needful to discover a method, better than any hitherto arrived at, for evok- ing and expressing the public opinion of the community upon questions of public and social morals. It was to be regretted, perhaps, that the churches, as such, had been too content to address themselves to spiritual er.ds, and had appeared to regard as somewhat beyond their proper sphere the moral, social, and physical condition of the people. However, it was becoming evident that they were more fully realizing the true scope of.ttie Mastee's commission when he made His disciples notonly go and preach, but alsogo and heal. Of course, there had been various temperance and total abstinence or- ganitations, which had doubtless done good service both in reforming the intemperate and in training the young in temperance principles and habits. They had also rendered considerable service (especially the Temperance Federa- tion) in endeavouring to influence the course of events at Brewster Sessions. But these various organizations had not, se far,realized as much success as perhaps they deserved, and certainly not as much as the necessities of public morality required. Hence a new departure-seemed to be called for. The cause of public morals was above alatheir distinctions and -differences ; it was not sectar- ian, nor did it appeal to political partizanship. It ap- pealed to their coramon instinct of righteousness, to their philantrophv their Christianity. It was very difilcult to comprehend the position of such .e.e could decline to co-operate in a cause so intimately- allied to the highest and best interests of all alike. Perirmally,he did not envy the individual who could refuse to enter at least into intelligent conference with his fellow-citizens, with a view to thee:coral elevation of the community to which he belonged, in hope of mitigating public and admitted evils, and of promoting general welfare. If She efficacy of the -existing machinery were really as -great as its friends,represented, then it failare reflected very discreditably opon them, and they were bound to set themselves at once to work to make fullkuse of their ,powers and opportunities. (Applause.) That new .ethods for compaesing moral and social reforms were -deemed desirable was becoming more manifest every day. Consequently, in all large centres, movements of the kind they met to consider, had been inaugurated. They assumed different names and titles, but their aim was one. Liverpool had its " Vigilance Committee" ; Bristol had its "City Vigilance Society"; 'Norwich had its " Nonconformist Council"; Birmingham, Brad- ford, and other towns had their similar schemes, and Manchester lied its "Social Questions Union." In several of the places named, these movements are shared in loyalty by all sects and parties. :The friends who first conceived the idea of a movement in Warring- ton cherished the hope that some such general concert of work would@ be possible here. The clergy of the Church of England and also of the Roman Catholic Church were, therefore, invited te join in sign- ing the circular which convened the first private confer- ence on FebruaryStth. The effort to secure their co- operation proved unsuccessful ; and although between that date and the present time earnest efforts have been made to induce the clergy of the town to doin, he re- gretted that all these efforts had failed. He however, clung to the hope that they would enlist the cordial co- operation of many of the laity of the Mucha England. He hoped that thelmion would present a platform for those who were not themselves total abstainers to work upon with a view -pecially to the mitigation of both intemperance itself and facilities for it. (Mere must be a large number Or persons, not abstainere, who were still deeply desirous-of promoting sobriety,aott lessening " the facilities for drunkenness which so largely abound on every hand. (Applause.) Their adhesion to the Union would not commit them to personal total abstin- ence, but it would enable them to help the cause of tem- perance generally. They wanted to create and evoke and -express a publiceminion upon questions adicensing, of betting and gambling, of social purity, of popular recreation, of domestic and sanitary reform, that would compel local authorities to pay attention to it', and to do their duties in all matters. (Applause.) They convened the conference in the telief that the " Social ..galestions Union" which they desired to establish in Warrington might conduce to .this result. The circular that .had been-issued atated the .ebiects of the Union. They were "to unite members of the various Christian communi- ties And others, for the.purpose of studying, and taking united action, upon questions affecting the moral .and social well-being of the community." It will thus be seen that they contemplated careful inquiry into the habits of the people, the general condition. They would seek to "hasten slowly," by obtaining all necessary information, by informing and developing public opirdop, be putting existing social laws into .opera- don, and , promoting eresa legislation, by co-operat- ing with existing social organizations, and if need be ,initiating others. The qualifying subscription was is, a Fear—not less, but as much more as means and generos- ity would allow. (Laughter.) These members were to- form a series of committees. whose duty wouktbe `" acquire information and conduct our various opera.: dons." He toasted that there were gentlemen present .who would odd the conference that evening by their ;prompt and.willine counsel aad suggestion. They would ,be glad also of the sympathy and advice of the ladies present. (Applause.) After MreYoege's paper woe read, free discussion eras</p>
        <p>t, Invited . Mr..F..MONIS, who was ree4ved with applause, raid it needed no recommendation of his to support an organisation such as the Chairman had referred to. No oneliving in the town of Warrington, could help feeling very; greatly the necessity of suet] a union, particularly amongst the young men of the town. He did not hesitate to say that about forty years ago, a large num- bee' of .warkshops were simply training grounds for immorality of all kinds. He hoped they were better now,, ,but from what he saw, he doubted very much whether they had rec.lly much unproved. He could well understand the hesitation with sthich fathers and mothers „seat their children into workshops to learn a tiade fortfear of the ccrruption which very frequently takes placehy people much older, and who ought to know better. Wherever his voice could be heard, he strongly urged foremen in the various workshops, to encourage anytyoung man who showed by character and conduct that,they did net intend to Sink to the level about them. (Applause.) With reforence to the various questions touched upon by Mr. Wonge, he did not think that there was any necessity to be perfectly in agreememt with all the,cbjects of the cnion, and he believed Bar. Pairclough.to be sufficiently in sympathy with most of the objects. It was desirable that the Church of England clergy...should take part in it, and he could not understand why the Roman Catholics should not do so. Euttexfould not let that interfere with the object. He wastunwilliag to believe that their absence arose from airy want of interest in such a movement. , Possibly they tied other objeetc and means of their own which at present made them that they did not feel that they could woida with this 'union. He thouett there was great need for a committoo that would look after the habitations of the people. There were some:people •to whom from their condition little, drink was almost a 'necessity. Suck unhealthy ,Condition s tended to a croKing for drink. He therefore looked forward to a time when better dwellings wouldte provided. ISE was tquite willing to take his dual share of the necessary work to tiring about better habitations and better domectic conditions among the work people. He was sorry that the 'cookery classes begun in People's College, had net succeeded as they desined. Between £5O and £6O bad been spent on them, and the result was practically nil. They could have given good teaching in cookery to 250 or 550 persons per week, but the thing had been a lam- entable faguro, and it wan a question whether another class would be started. Such a state of things was to be regretted. He was willing to take his part in the proposed union. (Applause.) Mr. J. GANDY expressed his hearty sympathy with the scheme, and he did not think it was formed before it was needed. There were evils in Warrington which had needed reform tor many years. A great deal had been said about drunkenness, but there needed a great re- form of the conditions under which people lived. A walk through the slums of the town would disclose some awful scenes. People lived in a manner in which it was impossible to be decent, as large families were crowded together in one room. This mode of life caused people to go to the public house. He thought it was time that this should be altered. Homes should be provided for the people in which they might live as became Chris- tian men and women, and that their lives Might be made tolerable. Mr. Picaavance next gave some graphic illustrations of the scenes he had witnessed at the Ship Yard en-</p>
        <p>trance of the Lion Hotel, and said that he heard a mother pi-Rising her mild for taking some drink. Al- though not a teetotaller he had signed a petition to close every public house in the United Kingdom. Tfie Rev. T. Ross hoped that one result et the social questions union would be to generate moral courage in the town. If they had it a year age, the entrance to the Lion Hotel in Ship Yard would have been closed. There was not a man in Bridge-street who had courage to come and witness against it. He also gave some instances of gambling among women which ought to be put a stop to. Mr. W. Roomers hoped that the union would be abun- dantly successful. Unfortunately in Warrington they had not been able previously to form an organisation in which Christians of every denomination could unite. He was sure that whether the church clergymen were with them or not, they would have the laity. He was delighted to bear Mr. Pickayance speak that night. He had been stung bitterly the last twelve months by the Ship Yard entrance, and he was sure that when the time came, Mr. Pickavance would be at the post of duty and ready to do hie part. (Applause.) There was an Act of Parliament to prevent the payment of wages in public- houses. He was awry to learn that in spite of it, this was practised very extensively. He knew of a son who had a widowed mother, and was compelled to go into a public .house to receive his wages front the foreman, and onetime he only took home ss. The law prevented this thing, therefore let them see that the law was put into force. An ereanisation like the one formed that night should help to put it in force. The Rev. W. H. DRUMMOND said that be had come from the Manchester Social Questions Union to find one being formed in Warrington, and he was convinced that it would do great good. From the little he had seen of Warrington, he was sure that it was needed. There ought to be no jealousies, and nothing to prevent them uniting together to make possible a righteous life for men and women. He thought that it was desirable to hold penny concerts on Saturday nights, as they have in Manchester, and he hoped for the enlistment in the cause of the medical men of the town. Mr. Blear, as a workman, expressed his sympathy with the scheme, and hoped that something could be done to get small gardens, &amp;c., for the workpeople to spend their leisure in cultivating. Mr. HORTON wanted to know what the Town Council was doing to allow the entrance in Ship Yard. It was scandalous that they had allowed it to be put right in the midst of the people of that yard. Mr. S. B. STRINGER said that those who took part in this movement must be prepared to have stones and mud thrown at them, for if they would do any good this would be the result. The Rev. T. W. ROBBRTS gave an account of his journeys through Warrington slums, and was of opinion that they were worse than the East end slums of Lon- don, in which he had laboured for some years. The Rey. E. T. REED as a comparative stranger was of opinion that Warrington needed such assistance as this union was intended to give. Mr. HALEY wished that more workingmen had been plesent. Profane language in the streets ought to be put down, as there were laws to stop it. Mention had been made of old buildings which had been condemned, but he thought that a lot of the new buildings which were erected were almost as bad. He did not know how they were passed by the borough surveyor. Mr. J. Pince also condemned the insanitary houses in Warrington. Mr. E.Boora hoped that the committees would not be fully organized until the union was better known. Mr. R. STOREY hoped that the meeting would not end in talk, but that much goed might come out of it. Mr. T. BIRCHALL endorsed everything that had been said. A. great deal might be done with the existing laws if there were stfficient public opinion behind them. He had done his best in times past to these objects, and he believed that the union would do something in this direction.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. J. GUEST 'thought that the meeting should come to some definite conclusion in the matter, and that they should do so at once. A number of papers for names to be filled in were handed round, and a good number were filled. On the motion of Mr. John Gandy seconded by Mr. T. Eirchall, Mr. S. B. Striager was appointed treasurer, and it was decided that the Y.M.C.A. should be requested to pro- vide a room.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks to the chairman closed the meeting, the chairman announcing that the first meeting of the committee might be held next week.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightlymeeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held on Wednesday morning in the Council Chamber of the Town Hall There were present Mr. G. Eateliffe (chairman), Messrs. R. Heaton, W. Houghton, Balharry, Turton, Davies, Shaw, J. Houghton, A. J. Jolley, Piling, Clarke, Mather, Faircloegh, Gill, Dignum, Muir, Hill, A. Jolley, Dr. Patterson, J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (deputy clerk), Word ( Workhouse master), Mines (Superintendent), .Basil Shearer (inquiry officer), Garnett (relieving officer). The minutes of the last meeting were read and passed unanimously. MEESE COMMITTEE. The House Committee reported that they had elected Mr. Davies chairnaan,-.and Mr. E. Aukland, vice-chair- man, for the ensuing twelve months. The Board con- firmed these appointments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>MORNINU.</head>
        <p>MORNINU.</p>
        <p>BY MELROOZ. Au! lovely -scene of early morn, The sun's fresh rays thy front adorn; Nor pen may, tell thy beauties o'er, . Nor ready ;pencil paint thy store ! , On brilliant purple-bedded line, , Piles of rich,crimson clouds recline And there a sky of modest blue, i Is shaded by the pink's gay hue. Like splendidhe a v en , sa or wc hn wr ai tdh vi i an tv bi d t o wt i ni s bent; Whose form -extending with the eye Would seem to join the earth and sky. , Two lovely gars scarce yet concealed, , Like diamonds sparkle half reveaPd ! , The silvery mist now heavenward leads, , Veiling the radiance of the meads; _ Towering alcdt-to reach the skies, , As incense unto Heaven doth rise! The gentle robin pours his strain Of melody o'er hill and plain; So sweet, so clear, the groves among, As if it were his matin song! O'er nature's face, look where wetmay, The scenes omnipotence display ; In shades of eve or waking morn, 'Wisdom and power the whole adorn ! .Amiist the peace. of Nature's form, Amidst the o'erwheirning blast and storm ; We see alike the power of God, . His mercy or avenging rod !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SMALLPDX AT LYlni. ;DISGRACEFUL MIDNIGHT SCENES. INDIGNANT INHABITANTS BURN lEFFIGIES _</head>
        <p>SMALLPDX AT LYlni. ;DISGRACEFUL MIDNIGHT SCENES. INDIGNANT INHABITANTS BURN lEFFIGIES _</p>
        <p>OF IDE. FOX.</p>
        <p>LyMnl.was a veritable ,pandemonium eon. Thurs- day night last week. The.etreets were the centres of seeness7hich were gruesome, weird, end passion- ate to an =tent scarcely imaginable. Things were perpetrated which in themeekves were of a disgrace- 'Jul character; but some excuse may be found for them in the fact that they arose simply, and purely , out, of a panic. And it was a panic arising.from a 'fresh outbreak of that abbcrred and dreaded, pestilence, the smallpox epidemic. The village bed been deelared free from emallpex, but last week taco fresh cases.broko out, and on,it , cominz known that the Board propesed removing.them -to .the old hospital on Booths Bill the maidests in the neighbourhood raised strong objections and depetations waited on the Board.during Thursday 4 and .protested in forcible tad clarnarous language atgainst any smallpox cases being brought into crowded district talch was free front contagion. On nxirsdayoight it was reported ie the district that the Board intended convoying the two Acaaes into the 6054 pita after ...dusk, and.a large and ezeited crowd paraded' tbestreets, declaringthey would turn any ambulateeei party back should an attempt be .made to reach the hospital. Effigies ,of Dr. J. M. Fox, the medical officer of health, and a dummy dressed as a sm4llpow patient were burnt, and the whole neighbourhood was a perfeet pandemonium throughout the night, cancer- tinapotc., being played .s.nd dummy figures taken to the door .of the smallpox 'hospital and aclmis.ion asked for them.. bya man dressed up to imitate Dr. Fox, with beard and slough cap. The people cooled down towards two o'clock in the morning, when it was sees that no attempt was being made to gat the patients through. Early on Friday morning, Seawever, the local author- ities quietly placed the cases in the hospital witliont any opposition. A number of the residents of Booths Hill and Newfield have published a large poster in which they" respectfully but most deliberately and emphati- cally protest against the action of Use Board in determin- ing, in the face and against the wishes of the said residents, to remove tite said infeetious cases into cur midst, thereby bringing upon our neighbourhood the stigma of its being considered an infected area. We protest the more earnestly, as we believe the the present cases could have been effectively isolated on the pre- mises in which the infection broke out, such isolation having proved most successful in the last case of small- pox which occurred in this district." The protest is signed on behalf of the residents of Booths Hill and Newfield by Messrs. F. 0. Moston, John Hatton. E. Dennett, W. Mercer, M. Thornieroft, W. Groves G. Richards, and John Niehtingale. The action of the Board and the Medical Officer has been very severely criticised in the neighbourhood, and much indicnetfort continues to prevail.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>SCIIOCLS COMMITTF.E,</head>
        <p>SCIIOCLS COMMITTF.E,</p>
        <p>The Schools Committee reported chat Mr. Ratcliffe had been appointed chairman. and ?dr. Clarke vice- chairman of the Committee. Specifications for painting certain parts .of the school premises were submitted, and the superintendent was 'recommended to receive tenders from different tradesmen for the work. The superintendent reported that for .be fixing of the necessary appliances for fire, and alterations of the reservoir, he had employed three labousers at sd. each per hour, and a bricksette.r at6id. per .hour, and the superintendent was recommended to pay .the wages out of his petty cash book. tOn the motion of Mr. W. Houghton, seconded by Mr. Clarke, the ,committee's report was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>PLACES ma LUNATICS.</head>
        <p>PLACES ma LUNATICS.</p>
        <p>The CLMIL said tlaat there.was the greatest difficulty in finding places for lunatics. He noticed tlist the Sal- ford Board of Guardians had instructed their Clerk to write to all the lunatic asylums within 100 miles of Lan- cashire to get their lunatics places in them, and of course this would mean a payment of 14s. per week for each patient who was sent out of the county. 4t the last meeting of the ‘Varrington Board, they had deputed him and two members to interview the superintendent of the Rainhill Asylum. They went over, and the result of the interview was that they would get admission for their lunatics, unless there was extreme pressure of new cases. At any rate, he thought so.</p>
        <p>-LIST OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The CLERK reported that the Local ,Government Board had sent papers to be filled up with theriames,payments, andsalaries of all officers, assistants,aul servants of the</p>
        <p>AN ANOMALY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wm. Ilonowrox wished to point out an anomaly. There was a paying station at Thelwall. where there was a salary attached to it, although there was nething to do, while 275. a fortnight was paid at Martinscroft, without any salary. Lie thought they ought to abolish the Thelwall station in favour of the IMartinscroft sta- tion.—Mr.SHEAßEn said ;he people at Thelwall did not require the station. The matter was deferred for further consideration.—The public business then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>EXTENSIVE THEFTS BY YOUTHS.</head>
        <p>EXTENSIVE THEFTS BY YOUTHS.</p>
        <p>ATTEMPTED SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>Walter Smith and John Syerett, two youths, were charged at the Warrington Occasional Court, on Tuesday, before the Mayor (Alderman E. Greenall) with stealing on Alonday, the 24th April, at Latchford, a gold albert guard, valued at ..E7 75., a silk handkerchief, value 75., and .£7 in money, the property of Richard King, employed on the ship canal, and living at Thelwall-lane, Latchford. The prisoner Smith was brother to King's housekeeper, and be (Smith) and Syerett had been staying with him, but on Tuesday morning week the two lads left the house suddenly. On the following Thursday the small hamper in which the money was kept was found lying in the ashbin. This led to King's box, in which the money and clothes were kept, being searched, when it was found to have been ransacked. The two prisoners were apprehended in Shrewsbury through endeavouring to pawn for 30s. the gold chain and a metal watch which they had bought. All the money was gone. The com- plainant identified the articles as his property, and said he saw them in his box on Sunday the 23rd inst. He first missed them on Thursday the 25th. lie kept them in his clothes box, in the kitchen. The police applied for a remand until the next Daresbury Sessions, bail being allowed in sureties of £25 each.</p>
        <p>At the same coart Mary Ann Smith, siostermaoyf losnt,eaotf the prisoners was charged with having n the complainant's house taken some poison with intent to kill herself. Richard King said the woman was his housekeeper and had been in his employ for 17 months. she went to the doctor for some medicine for skin dis- ease, and he gave her about a pint of lotion, for out- ward use, and she drank the whole of it. He thought the stealing case had prayed on her mind. Miss Black- hurst and the Rev. B. B. Prescott had offered to get her to the Cancer and Skin Diseases Hospital, in Liverpool. The case was dismissed on the understanding that the woman would be taken to the hospital.</p>
        <p>DO NOT NEGLECT! Sufferers from Gravel, Lumbago, Piles, Pains in the Back, Dropsy, Wind and Water Complaints, Diseases of Kidneys, Bladder, Urinary Organs, Stone, Meet, Stric- ture, Sciatica, Rheumatism, and Gout, will find a positive cure in Holdroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a small box, and If not satisfied your money will be returned. Price is lid. Of all chemists, or post free for 12 stamps from BOLD- BOYD'S Medical Hall, Cleckheaton, Yorks. Don't be put off. If you cannot get them write to the Proprietor, and box will be sent next post.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>WHAT'S TO BE DONE?</head>
        <p>WHAT'S TO BE DONE?</p>
        <p>IMPERIAL FEDERATION.</p>
        <p>BY MR. H. WHITHAM.</p>
        <p>It is with some trepidation that I venture to say a few words on Imperial Federation. To begin I with, Imperialism is a somewhat distasteful, as well as formidable word. It savours coo much of that Jingoism which has pervaded the minds of the least patriotic of Englishmen since the days of "Peace with Honour." At the present time the Tory would be the last man to credit the Home Rule party with patriotism, and yet,what is patriot- ism ? Tried by its records since the days of North till the present time it has been one series of failures. And why? Simply because it was a false Egoism, and not a true Patriotism. Looking at the doings of certain Englishmen at the present time, and from the British Federation standpoint, one can only wonder how, in the name of all that is right and just, they can hope for I success, or claim to be patriots, or even think with- in themselves that they are acting as He would have them act, who said " Whosoever shall smite thee on thy right cheek, turn to him the other also," and yet the spirit was equally applicable to a nation as to an individual. The Tory meaning of the word patriotism seems to be a mixture of pride prejudice, selfishness, ambition, revenge, and cruelty. It is filled with a ridiculous, and odious conceit. True patriotism is the friend of good government, seeking by well reasoned enactments to attract, and not to repel, and the truest patriots are not the army, or the navy, or the Brummagem tinsel that is now trying to spread through the British nation. Fenian methods, that proposes, traitorously, to line the hedges, and ditches ; the Tories seem to forget the history of the past, and, worse still, not 0 learn current history. That a nation of 30,000,000, should fear a mere hand- ful of 5,000p00, and should tremble in their shoes be- cause they fancy they see in the distance a red rag on which faintly may appear the words' Rome Rule 'is beyond all belief. That they should appoint special prayers, and select special collects to offer to the Almighty is in no way surprising. They always mem to have known the ways, and intentions of the Great Ruler better than He knew them Himself. Were the Liberal party at all superstitious they might easily claim that the G.O.M. was being preserved beyond the ordinary span of life andonental activity to carry out .a great point in the world's redemption. An idea takes, or fails to take according to the name its godfathers give it on ushering the bantling into thi s sea of troubles. I have no doubt that Ireland might have gained all she desires had her leaders got hold of some more "fetching" word, and still the cry has come to stay, however much it may have to struggle for existence. With "Imperial Federation" I am not so sure that it has come to stay, although, as a cry it is fetching enough. In dealing with the question, one naturally asks first : Why has it been raised? Is it with a contrite heart, that Englishmen have said, "Come, let us practice a little of that unselfishness, which we preach.; let us do good unto them that spitefully use us." fear not; the cry is the result of continued bad trade, of repeated cycles of depression, of a dread lest the markets of the world should slip beyond all recovery out of the grasp of the mother country. We all remember the stale olel chestnut about what was the secret of England's greatness. We don't believe in that power any longer, and we give all sorts of reasons but that as the secret of England's greatness. Some- times it is her geographical position, then it is the sup- posed well known tenacity, and, I must say, pertinacity of the stout old gentleman, John Bull. Again it :s her mineral possessions, and the dash, and daring of her Industrial captains—sometimes a little praise falls to the mere workers. I think several reasons might be as- cribed, some of them after the manner of Buckle, but the chief one is, the English are a mongrel nation. This is obvious from the fact that the more mongrel they of the long line of aristocrats become the more useful as citizens. To carry out the idea: What is America to- day? Made up of all the nations of the earth, he is pressing forward to take the lead. But I am not of those who believe that England must " take a back seat "—at least not for a few years to come. In her language, her freedom, her institutions; in the high in- tellectual position she holds amongst the nations of the world; in the great forward movements that have seized hold of the minds of the people, lies her streegth and , permanence. _ _ .</p>
        <p>I am sure that England, with not one soldier, nor yet one iron-clad, would be the strongest nation on earth. What is it the man of intellect recognises to-day most? Brute force, or moral force? As with the individual so with the nation. What nations of the earth do the Americans always compare their own great country with? Not Germany, France, or Russia, but England, every tirne. Perfidious Albion used always to be on the Frenchman's tongue. America is always on the watch as to what Englishmen are doing. Now it is the easiness and slovenliness of her workmen. Then it is an attack on her effete morality, and the snobbishness of her aris- tocracy, or the selfishness of the British in wanting to own the globe. But one can always see the jealous ad- miration that runs through these articles. Every American newspaper of standing keeps an English cor- respondent:; not any other country is honoured in such ' a manner. Canada is now the most prominent subject for discussion. Hawaii has been the uppermost passing , text upon which to base a tirade against England. Not that the States wanted the dusky Queen's possessions; but then, England must not have them. The .question of Canada is a permanent one. Will she continue as she is? Will she become an independent Repablic ?or will she consent to be annexed?</p>
        <p>And now what of Canada I have a great deal to put forward as to the dominion. The time has gone by when the value ,of the British Empire can be gauged with weights and scales. If a 'colony is worth no more than the value of its export and import trade it had better go. There is something else for the British nation to do. It is now, and will ibe„ the greatest instru- ment for good that the world has ever seen. The importance of Canada to England cannot be over-esti- mated. That is a settled fact she lies in the fair way of intercourse between Great Britain and the distant East. She has fairly tried to develop her resources. I think so far she has comparatively failed. And why? lam of opinion the only answer is : Cecause of unfair treatment by the House government, but chiefly by the trickstering spirit of the so-called imperial Federa- tionists. The mean .spirited diplomacy which has driven all sentiment out cf Newfoundland for the mother country, will assuredly drive it out of Canada, and that before many years are over. And yet Canada may not go altogether scot free as to blame for her present position. With a high pretective tariff she ilhS shut herself in to such an extent that something like social ruin new stares her in the face.</p>
        <p>have no doubt that perfect free trade would be to the advantage of the Empire in the long run, but it is useless to expect it. It seems to be absolutely opposed to the fiscal policy of the colonies with only one cloutit- ful exception. All the tie the crown has upon the colonies is one of sentiment ; a common origin, com- mon religion, common institutions, and so on. Perhap:, this should be enough experience tells us it is not. John Bright when opposing the much-needed Factory Acts, threatened that if he and his fellow capitalists were to be dictated to they would take themselves and their capital to America. So it is all round—capital will not stand martyrdom. It is shy and soon takes wings. The only consolation is, it will soon have no- where to fly to.</p>
        <p>Where lam placed I have a pretty fair chance of see- ing what is going on in Canada. Only A few hours on the cars, and the border is passed. A very determined effort is now being made to establish reciprocity between the New England States, and Canada, and, I think in a short time It will be accomplished, and then, what becomes of your Dominion? Let me say right here,and very straight, Canada is no place for the emigrant. I know that a determined effort is being made in England to prove the contrary. Lectures, and literature about the country will be plentiful. The government working with the railroad companys are going to spend heaps of money in advertising the benefits, and glowing prospect to the emigrant classes of Europe. They may do this, and the emigrants may go te Canada, but they will not i stag there. They will land ultimately n the States. Sixty per cent, of the emigration from Great ?Intern comes to the United States, as against 13 per cent that goes to Canada. For one dollar the Englishman invests in Canada he invests ten in the States, and yet the Imperial Federation League in England has suggested the formation of an Imperial Council to give united voice to the empire in time of peace, and provide for the defence of the empire which I read in a speech recently was madeup of 250,000,000 Hindoos, and 50,000,000 Africans.</p>
      </div>
