      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COUNTY COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COUNTY COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>SCHOLARSHIPS AND EXHIBITIONS.</p>
        <p>A circular has just been issued by the Director of Technical Instruction for the county of Lanease ter, announcing that 41 scholarships and 175 exhi- bitions will be awarded en the result of examina- tions to be held next month. It will be remem- bered that in the competition last year two £6O scholarships and six £lO exhibitions were gained by students of the Warrington School of Art. The following is an abstract of the conditions under which these valuable prizes are granted. The full par- ticulars may be seen at the Warrington Museum or School of Art. The County Council offers for competi- tion fourteen scholarships for proficiency in science,four for proficiency in art, six for proficiency in commercial subjects, and twenty for proficiency in agriculture and horticulture. Each scholarship (except in agriculture) will be of the value of £6O per annum, and will be ten- able for a term not exceeding three years. Each agri- cultural scholarship will be of the value of £2O per annum. Each science and commercial scholar will be required to attend a three years' course of instruction at such public institution as may be approved by the Technical Instruction Committee of the Council. Each art scholar will be required to pursue the practical study of some definite branch of art in an approved institution. Each agricultural scholar will be required to attend such courses in agriculture, horticulture, and such other subjects as may be prescribed. For the science, art, and commercial scholarships no candidate under 16 or over 25 years of age will be eligiWe. For the agricultural scholarships all candidates must be over 15 years of age, and the children of farmers or gardeners, or of persons engaged in agricultural or horticultural pursuits, or must be themselves intending to engage in agricultural or horticultural pursuits. All the above scholarships will be open to students of either sex who are natives of the United Kingdom, and who have resided during the twelve months immediately preceding the date of the examination within the ad- ministrative county of Lancaster. Tlie administrative county of Lancaster includes all places within the area of the county of Lancaster, excluding the county boroughs. Examinations for the above scholarships will be held at iVarrington and other placer, on the 12th to the 16th days of June, 1893. Of the 14 science scholarships, 12 will be reserved for candidates who are engaged, or preparing to engage in the following indus- tries, viz. :—Engineering (2), chemical industries, in- cluding dyeing and bleaching (2), textile indus- tries (4), plumbing (1), electrical industries (1), mining (2), whilst two will be open to per- sons not connected with any of the above industries. In case a sufficient number of qualified candidates does not enter from any of the above industries, these scholarships may be appropriated to any of the other industries in which qualified candidates have presented themselves. Successful candidates will be required to pursue a course of study designed to fit them for the industry in which they have obtained the scholarship. The scholarships will not be tenable at day training col- leges for teachers, nor can they be held with any other scholarship. For the Science scholarships, papers will be set in the following subjects, of which four, includ- ing mathematics, must be taken: Chemistry (inorganic and chemical theory) ; physics (heat, light, electricity, and magnetism) ; theoretical mechanics (statics, dyna- mics, and hydrostatics) ; applied mechanics; mathema- tics (algebra, geometry, trigonometry, elementary conics); mechanical and geometrical drawing. A prac- tical examination will be held in chemistry and physics. For the Commercial scholarships, papers will be set in the following subjects of which mathematics and one foreign language are compulsory : Mathematics (arith- metic and algebra) ; commercial geography; political economy ; English (including grammer and composition) French, German. For the Art Scholarships the follow- ing papers will• be set : —(1.1 elementary principles of ornament ; (2) shaded drawing of ornament from the cast ; (3) drawing from the antique ; (4) design for orna- ment ; (5) painting ornament from photograph ; (6) modelling ornament from photograph. Every candidate must take the papers marked 1,2, 3, and 4, and in addition, either sor 8. For the agricultural scholar- ships, papers will be set in the following subjects : Agricultural and horticulture; arithmetic and mensura- tion; and elementary chemistry. The Lancashire County Council also offers for compet- ition 100 Exhibitions for proficiency in s;:ience, 25 for art, 25 for commercial subjects, and 25 for agriculture and horticulture. Each of the above Exhibitions will be of the value of £lo,and will he tenable during the session beginning October 1893. Payment of the income derived from the Exhibition will be conditional upon regularity of attendance. The Exhibitions will be open to students,. of either sex, who are natives of the United Kingdom,. and who have resided during the six months immedi- ately preceding the date of the Examination, within, the administrative County of Lancaster. The examinations- e for these Exhibitions will be held simultaneously at the following and other places if a sufficient number of candidates enter at each place :—Liverpool, Manchester, Warrington and Wigan. Of the 100 science Exhibitions 90 will be reserved for candidates who are engaged, or who are preparing to engage in the following indus- tries, viz. : Engineering (15) ; chemical industries (including dyeing and bleaching),(ls) ; textile industries, (30) ; plumbing, (10) ; electrical industries, (10) ; min- ing, (10) ; whilst JO will be open to persons not connected with any of the above industries. Successtul candidates will be required to pursue a course of study designed to fit them for the industry in which they have obtained the Exhibition. The examinations will be held on the evenings of Wednesday and Thursday, the 14th and 15tks June, 1893. C4ndidatee for the Science Exhibi- tions will be required to take not less than three of the following subjects :—Chemistry, inorganic ; physics, (heat, electricity, and magnetism) ; mechanics (theoreti- cal and applied, treated experimentally) ; mathematics (arithmetic, algebra and geometry). The candidates for Art Ethibitions will be required to execute—a) an outline from a caet of ornament in low relief ; „(2) a shading from a cast of fruit in high relief. Candidates , for Commercial exhibitions will be required to take not lees than three of the following subjects :—Arithmetic ; English granuner and composition ; French or German ; *Commercial geography. Candidates for the agricultural and horticultural exhibitions take the following sub- ject § :--Eeglisti (grammar and composition) ; arith- metic f mensuration ; agriculture and horticulture. Students, elio at the date of the examination are in rtiri):l may yes at an institution at which attentdvansccetioaita" county be held are not eligible for these 'Exhibitions, !tor are persons who are already hold- ing county scholarslilps, nor are persons who have twice. held Exhibitions awarded by the Lancashire County Council.</p>
        <p>MINERS' DISPUTE AT GOLBORNE. The Wages dispute between Messrs. Evans and their Gol borne minHs culminated on Monday in 400 men giving a fortnight's notice. The remaining, miners will follow suit unless the employers yield. Mr. John Robinson, the general manager of Messrs. Richard Evans and Co., on Tasday informed a press representative that he had not known of any grievance' at the collieries prior to Saturday, with the exception: of tie Go'borne question and one or two small matters at the Wood Pit. The bulk of the grievances handed tot him by Mr. Woods were entirely new, and he had no, heard of them before. lie was of opinion that ktio, could have been sati.factorily settled by othoeroopl:n•mean e Wigan 4ft gers. The only real grievance waaSaatth the • Col- liery in the Pemberton 51tanine,u ne where the men wanted the masters to agree t Icertain yardage allowances which they had been recetvins• as a fixed wage. For several years a numberof the met had been working in difficult places, and where the coal is very bad. The men could not make much tonnage, and the masters made certain allowances per yard of coal so as to give the men a good wage. Now the coal has been worked further back, and is tnuch easier to get, and r.e colliers, irrespective of the yardage allowances, make e- very good day's wage. The circulars demanding a bal— lot had been printed before there was any grievance, a• it was only on the day that they were sent out to t men that two men employed in the Golborne CoP received notice of reduction in the yardage alio. Aerr owing to their having got into a very good w wsnco. place. It was altogether a new departure for .oskgf- ation to send out circulars demandiag a he eder - b Allot grievances which were not known to on officials, and it looked as if it was beinr- exist by the off a general reduction. One imports .4 done to stave circular referred to him (Ur. Robinson ,at point in the important agreements and neglectir .4 openly violating pondence. Mr. Woods admitted important cones- Jll only correspondence that had no Saturday that the letter in connection with the Go . been replied to was a inson had written to the assir ,Lborne men. Mr. Robin- aid was under the impress; agent, Mr. Greenall ,trant , s unnecessary. At the ' ,on that any further reply m. m were better treated • tiolborte'Colliery, he said, the otl er collieries. rad totter ',paid than any of the</p>
