      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>TIIE EXAMINEE, SATURDAY. MAY a, 1893.</p>
        <p>ight Hours Thu Miners Eight Hours Bill r for Miners has been very much to the front the this week, and the promoters of rneas3nre have achieved a ' very notable and airgnificant Parliamentary success. The second eading of the Bill was carried in the House of Core moos on Wednesday evening by a majority of 1:4) less than seventy-eight. Well might it be said t no other political or social movement has, in th"7ll,_t times, made such rapid progress as this for hour6:ertening by legislative enactment of the labour of miners. In 1887, Mr. Srarnsx lie4,7ll44:tiosnol‘f t ved an amendment to the Mines Zillea to limiting the period of labour in eir,lit hours, but that amendment was I),fPoP6rted by only fifty-four votes. Three Months re the dissolution of Parliament last year a 13ill to that which has now been so deci- hvtlY endorsed was rejected by a majority of one „114,(1red and twelve. Contact with the eonstitti- i—‘eies has led to many conversions. The division CoMmons on Wednesday was not on party Cc*. The second reading of the Bill was moved Vl:** S. Woons' the Liberal and Labour member 'ae Ince division, whose speech, according to tgeoeral testimony, was terse and lucid and admir- -1137 delivered. Its rejection was moved by Mr. l'il°l44B, Who was returned to Parliament with eih,verwheioling majority by the South Wales 14" Lg Constituency of Merthyr Tydvil. The Bill Naa both uPperted and opposed by members on sides of the House. Mr. GLADSTONE th:c6lneernent that he should vote for eot sc'e"d reading was, as might be expected, busiastically welcomed by the advocates :a e ?gilt hours. In the division lobbies there was till further effacement of party distinctions. usT°Sk was followed by a majority of the but Mr. Joss Mosixx, and tacgs,,,:r two others still adhered to their an- of —341 to legislative interference with the hours ittn adult workpeople and they were numbered "gat the minority. The Nationalist members • Ce• gaindisplayed their sympathy with the Plrations of the democracy of this country, and voted theY • et, ed solidly for the Bill. Lord RANDOLPII '"ultotxr.L, as became the inventor of the erratic era• Unreliable quantity known as "Tory demo- er A'as the most prominent t..mongst the sup- Pirters of the Bill on the Opposition benches. -41 r. BALFOUR, Mr. GOSCELEN and all the ts:"Pe of recognised leaders of "solid" Conserva- tlhatlt—of that Conservatism which allays "rules 1, roost" when office has been secured—were as :,ostile as over, and gave effect to their hostility t23' their vote. This is significant of the kind of trsatment that official Conservatives are still pre- pared to mete out to labour questions. 4-3 was to be expected the most was made '4 the debate by the opponents of the Bill of the differences that are said to exist :111'ngst miners themselves as to the wisdom of 'lriog an eight-hours day by Act of Parliament, ld Particularly of the opposition of the miners °I Durham and Northumberland. But, Mr. Woons, in his opening speech, took a good deal cf the edge off these arguments. His asser-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <p>tion that the minority of dissentient miners is so small as to be hardly worth five minutes notice was rather too sweeping; but, at the same time he pretty clearly demonstrated that the minority is much smaller than many people imagine. In Northumberland, where there are thirty thousand miners, less than nine thou- sand had votad against the Bill. it is true that only two thousand six hundred had voted in its favour, but it seemed a reasonable presumption that those who did not take the trouble to vote are not violently afraid of the effect of an Eight Hours Bill. In Durham there are between sixty and seventy thousand miners, and of these not more than twenty eight thousand have voted against the Bill. In addition to this, it should not be forgotten that the younger section of ' workers in those two counties, who put in the , longest hours underground, were not allowed to vote in the ballots that have been taken. If they ' had, they would probably have materially altered the figures. Taking everything into account, Mr. Woons seems to be amply justified in claiming that at least five-sixths of the miners of the coun- try are in favour of the Bill, and it is rarely pos- sible to obtain a larger majority than that in sup- of any debateable question that is submitten le arbitrament of the House of Commons.</p>
        <p>MR. T. W. LEGIT, the member for " Electoral • le Newton Division, seems dis- Consideratione."tl posed to pursue that vein of rhetoric which consists mainly of " flouts and gibes." It is true his flouts are not very forcible and his gibes have little point, but still, such as they are, they find a place in his speeches. As a rule, of course, he reserves his sarcasm, which is mild in spite of himself, for the benefit of his opponents, but in the debate on the Eight Hours Bill in the House of Commons on Wednesday lie seemed inclined to distribute his favours impartially, Mr. Lao a, as we all know, stood out against the Eight Hours Bill at the General Election, although there are some miners in his constituency, and on the strength of this he assumes a position of superior virtue. Ile I spoke rather contemptuously of the conversions that had been made in regard to the eight hours question. Some Conservatives, he said, had been particularly late in finding salvation; and some had been too late, for they had lost their seats. Mr. LEou sneered at those members who had been "partly influenced by electoral considerations." This is Intended for censure, but it is hard to see that censure is due. What Mr. LEGIT means, we suppose, is that certain members have regard for the opinions of the electorate they represent, because if these opinions are disregarded, they may be unpleasantly emphasized at an election. But that does not reflect any dis- credit on the representative of a consti- tuency. A member of Parliament may be in- fluenced either by "electoral' or by "personal" considerations, but no one will contend that the latter is a more respectable motive of action than the former. Mr. LRGII'S constituents can hardly, feel flattered by his obtrusive repudiation of "electoral" considerations, even though it was only done for the purpose of getting in one of his "flouts and gibes."</p>
        <p>THOSE amongst us who are concerned at the predictions of a disastrous drought will not find any consolation in the reports which are sent over from the North of France. It is said that the good people of Normandy are greatly distressed by the agricultural outlook. For over three months not a drop of water has fallen in the province. The fruit trees and vegetables are simply covered with caterpillars, and so numerous are these in- sects that in two days they destroyed all prospects of a successful pear or apple season. The meadows are so parched up that even in some of the most fertile valleys it has been found necessary to allow the cows to browze on the young shoots of barley. We are told further that the season is drier than the one that preceded the great war, when the drought which began in May, continued to October.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <p>MAY QUEEN FESTIVALS. To-day (Saturday) May Queen Festivals will take place at flaydock and Golborne. The crowning of the May Queen at Haydock will be performed in the Haydock football field. A procession of over 600 children, with brass bands from Haydock and Earlestown will parade the village. The queen Miss A. Carter, will be crowned by Miss Grimke- Drayton at 4.30. A trained set of dancers will also plait the maypole. An entertainment will also be given by the Union Jack Minstrels. Prizes will be given for the best turnouts. At Golborne, the crowning of the May Queen will take place on the Recreation Ground. Over 1,000 children will take part in the procession. After the crowning ef the queen, a host of attractions have been provided, including the May-pole and gipsy dance, the morria dance by the Hindley Green Morris Dancers,and the dancers V.Po.Bo.troupe of juvenile dancers. Air.W. G. Hirst, the clever bicycle rider, and the Manches- ter Miniature Volunteers with three other bands will be present. Further particulars will be found in our advertising columns. UMBRELLIB RECOVER/M.—J. and W. DurroN Saakey-st. WaiTington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>MIRACULOUS ESCAPE PROM DROWNING IT RUNCORN. _</p>
        <p>Another illustration—oiiii-e-beneflts arising from the knowledge imparted in connection with the St. John Ambulance Association in rendering first aid took place at Runcorn, on Tuesday evening. It appears that Elizabeth Nicholls, wife of John Nicholls, captain of the narrow boats Minnie and Brenda, stationed at the Top Locks, left the vessels for the purpose of fetching some water, leaving her two children aged four and seven respectively. On returning in about ten minutes she missed her eldest son, and asked his brother where Isaac was. He replied "In the cut." She promptly secured a boat hook, and put it into the water,when the body floated to the surface,and she succeeded in rescuing it. The child was uncon- acious, and apparently dead. Mr. Thomas Moore, , however, of the Vine Hotel, Lowlands-road, came I upon the scene, took the child to his house, and wrapped it in blankets. F.C. Booth was called in, I and he and the landlord did their utmost to restore respiration. After continuing their efforts for an hour and a half, the child regained consciousness, and when examined by Dr. McDougall, he pro- nounced it to be out of danger. The child is little the worse for the mishap. Evaws' Norm) Lc 610. Km, GLOTES in Tan, Brown and Black, 17, Sankey-Bt.—Advt. DEATH OF MIL WALTER ROBINSON. The news of the death of Mr. Robinson, formerly of Overton, at his residence in London, which took place on Saturday evening after but a brief illness, and which was announced from the pulpit at Trinity Chapel, Frodsham, and the Wesleyan Chapel, Five Crosses, on Sunday morning,was heard with deep regret by the congregations, and much sTmpathy expressed for his widow and family. The deceased for many years past took very, great interest in the several branches of work connected with Trinity Chapel and Sunday school, he being one of the superintendents of the latter, until his removal to London caused by the amalgamation of the Parr's Banking Company, Limited, with the Alliance Bank, Limited, and his kindly help and sympathy with the interests of the officers, teach- ers, and scholars of the school will ever be remem- bered and treasured. The deceased was only taken ill lass Wednesday week, but the illness, which was a complication of influenza, rheumatic fever, with pleurisy, was considered to be a very serious one, although the highest advice was sought. Mr. Robinson was well-known in Warrington, where he resided at one time,and his death will be deeply regretted by a large circle of friends.</p>
      </div>
