      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>port i to thl</head>
        <p>port i to thl</p>
        <p>-- - LOCAL NEWS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>SMALL-PDX AT RUNCORN. The latest information received is that there are 30 cases of smallpox in the hospital</p>
        <p>TIIE JOINERS' STRIKE. AT RUNCORN. The jainers employed by Messrs. Farrell and Son, re- sumed work on Tuesday, the advance to Bd. per hour being conceded. ROBRINO FELLOW-WORKMEN AT WIDNES. On Thursday,at Widnes Petty Sessions, Wm.McGlove, of Rainhill, was tined 203. or 14 days, for stealing a shirt and a pair of socks from an outhouse at Crenshaw Hall, Bold, the property of two fellow-workmen. Go to PRITCHARD'S. 51, High-street, Runcorn, for the best FIVE GUINEA WATCH a workingman can have.—"ldet. ACCIDENT ON TUB CANAL. A man named Jones, employed as a labourer on the Ship Canal at Weston Point, had his leg broken while at work on Monday, and was taken to the hospital at Latchford, where he is progressing very satisfac- torily. SUCCESS OF A HALTON MEDICAL STUDENT. The friends of Mr. John Aldren Wright, son of Mr. Thomas Wright, of Halton, will be pleased to learn that , he has passed his third examination for medical and surgical degree at Csmbridge. Idr, Wright is a medical student at St. Mary's Hospital, Paddingtoni iiandon„</p>
        <p>THE OPENING OP RtINCORN DOCKS. The tidal basin of Rune inn Docks is bei hiproved by the blasting of the rock which forms the bottom. It is expected that the depths will be increased from 31, to 41 t. The Ship Canal is being gradually filled with water from Weston Point to Runcorn, and it is anticipated that Rune= Dockv will be opened for traffii:. by the end of the mohth. •</p>
        <p>RUNCORN WOMEN'S iffrAiRnANCE ASsoCIATION. 'A meeting in connection with thit association was held on Tuesday afternoon in St. John's Schoolroom, ! under the presidency of Mrs. Leach. Addresses were delivered by the president and Miss Meredith. Two pianoforte solos were played by Miss Halldlng, gOngs were rendered by Miss Wilson, and teading was i given by Mrs. F. S. Norman. At the close two persons signed the pledge. PRITCHARD'S have how stood the test for fifty years, and quality and first-class workmanship are still guaranteed.— Adrt ST. MARY'S CHURCH SUNDAY SCHOOL, HALTON. On Tuesday evening, the members of Mrs. Wray's Bible Class for young women, and zilso the Men's Bible Class, met in the girl's schoolroom to spend a social even- ing together, in response to a kind invitation of Mrs. Wray. The proceedings opened in a substantial man- ner with an excellent tea, to which 78 sat down. After the room had been re-arranged the evening was spent in dancing, singing,reciting,and other amusements. 110BIIRRY AT A WIDNES WAKE, On Tuesday last, is. wake was held over the mother et John McMullen, of 48 Wellington-st., and a youth 18 years of age named Samuel Bushed, was there. Next morning when the "wake " was ended and the house had resutned its ordinary aspect, a pair of boots worth 103. were missing, and it being ascertained that Bushell had pawned them during the day, he was apprehended. The Widnes magistrates on Thursday fined him 103., or, in default, seven days imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>RUNCORN PUBLIC HALL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PUBLIC HALL.</p>
        <p>A special session was held at Runcorn Police Court on Monday morning for the parpose of hearing an applica- tion from Mr. Arthur Salkeld for a licencs enablin stage plays to he performed at Runcorn Public Hall. The magistrates on the bench were Major Hazlehurst, Messrs. James Reynolds, C. Reynolds, and G. L. Davies, and the application was granted.—Messrs. W. Whittaker and Thomas Brewer were swore as the sureties. HOLY TRINITY SUNDAY SCHOOLS, RUNCORN. The usual quarterly social evening in connection with the above schools was held on Tuesday evening, in the infant school when a presentation was made (subscribed to by the teachers and workers) to Mr. and Mrs. E. R. Westbrook, on the occasion of their marriage, which took place at the Holy Trinity Church on the previous Wednesday. Mr. Westbrook has acted in the capacity of secretary to the Sunday schools for nearly two years, as well as teacher, and he is held in very high esteem by his vicar, curate, and fellow workers. The present consisted of handsome cut glass. AN OLD DESERTER CAUGHT AT WIDNES. On Wednesday, at the Widnes Police Court, before Mr. Deacon, a young man named Patrick Casey, lodging in Wellington-street, was brought up. charged with being a deserter from the Royal Lancashire Regiment stationed at Lancaster.—P. S. Strickland stated that he noticed defendant's name in the "Police Gazette " of 19th December, 1891, as being wanted as a deserter. He made inquiries, and eventually found that defendant was workinr, at Messrs. Hutchinson's works. The ser- geant accused Casey of being a d2rserter,and after soine hesitation he admitted the charge.—He was remanded in custody to await an escort. It yen want your WATCH REPAIRED by skilled workmen take it to PRITCHARD'S.--ildet.</p>
        <p>BREAKING A MAN'S JAW AT RUNCORN. At Runcorn Petty Send Gas on Monday, James Finegan, a young man, was charged with causing grievous bodily harm to Frank Lydon by kicking him.—P.S. Gosling stated that about ten o'clock on Saturday night he went to the fair ground iu company with P.C. Morris, and he there saw the prisoner kicking a man who lay on the ground. They ran, but the prisoner seeing them at- tempted to get away, but they captured him.—Superin- tendent Atherton said the- man's jaw was broken in three places, and he had that morning been taken to the Royal Infirmary at Liverpool. He asked for a remand for a week. The application waR granted. •</p>
        <p>JOSEPH PRITCHARD, 51, High-street, Runcorn, keeps SPECTACLES to suit, all sights, from a shilling a air.—Adrt.</p>
        <p>- . ST. JOAN'S BAND OF BOPS, RUNCORN. The choir of this society, assisted by a number of friends, gave a service of song entitled The Oiled Feather," in St. John's Schoolroom, on Wednesday even- ing. There was a good attendance. The solos were rendered by Mrs. Teinperlev and Miss S. P. Hayes ; the duet by Mrs. Temperley and Miss Sefton; the trio, by Miss B. Harrop, Miss M. Booth, and Mr. A. Littler ; and the quartet by the Misses M. Jonea, B. Ilarrop, and S. Pritchard, and Mr. A. Robinson. The narrative was read by Mr. W. G. Jackson, and Mr. J. Pritchard was the accompanist. The conductor, who deserves great praise tor the way in which the service was gone through, was Mr. M. Thomas. A collection was made in aid of the building fund of the proposed new church. When in want of a CHASTE and ELEGANT WEDDING Or BIRTHDAY PRESENT go to PRITCHARD'S, 51, If lei-st.—Advt. LICENSING MATTYRS AT RUNCORN. At Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday the magistrates ' granted the transfer of the following licenses :—The Wheatskeaf, Bridge-St., from Samuel Worrell to William Faulkner ; the Union Vaults, Union-st., from Mary Eliza Santley to William Faulkner the Bull's Head, Halton-road from Thomas Riley to William Powell ; the Fleetwood Arms, Church-st., to Mrs. Martha Oates, widow of late proprietor ; the Egerton Arms, Bridge- it., to Mrs. Elizabeth Waterworth, widow of late pro- prietor; the Devonshire Hotel, Ashridge-st., from George Shone to Archibald Flaming ; the Village Inn, Halton, from Albert Edward Mathews Milton to Thomas Moly- nenuz ; the Millstone, Mill-st., from Thomas Owen, administrator of the late Annie Elizabeth Ashbrook to William Fuller. Mrs. Sarah Grounds, widow of late William Grounds, was granted permission to sell at the Dock Hotel, Weston Point. JOSEPH PRITCHARD, Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, High- ld., Runcorn.— Adrt.</p>
        <p>CRUELTY TO HORSES. NEAR RUNCORN. At Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday before Major Hazlehurst and Mr. C. Reynolds, Thomas Hulse. a boat- man on board the "Edmund of Winsforcl, pleaded guilty to having on the 18th April cruelly illtreated a horse at Norton. Inspector Dowty, R.S.P.C.A., said the animal, which was drawing a boat, was suffering from mange and there was a suppurating wound upon which the collar was pressing. The Inspector said the defend- ant was a poor man and he did not press for a heavy penalty. The bench ordered the payment of costs, Bs. 6d.—Andrew Hackney, boatman of Macclesfiel4, was also summone&amp;fcr crueliy to a horse at Norton on the 18th April. In this case the horse was excegsively lame, suffering from a di-eased bone. It was incurable and not tit to work. It transpired that a week previous the defendant had been warned that he must not work the horse and he had been tined.—The magistrates said they could not overlook such a case arid the defendant was fined 20s. and costs,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
