      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY. MAY 6, 1893.</p>
        <p>tlifkMEltdßi MIS:i101i BAZAAR AT RUNCORN. 'Chit hair tenhzed £143. The debt upon the Insti- tution is £5OO.</p>
        <p>n NEW RATE FOR SUTTON. On Mo lay, at Runcorn Petty Sessions, the magis- trates signed a new poor rate for the township of Sutton, of is. 4d. in the L, estimated to realize £493 7s. 6d.</p>
        <p>1 B ETITESOA CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH, RUNCORN. Another people's service was held am Sunday evening, when the ftev. T. Simpson, preached a sermon entitled "Heaven on earth." Miss Simkin and Mr. Leathwood sang solos. Mr. R. W. Pierpoint gave a performance on the cornet, and Mr. Clifford Le Conteur played on the organ.</p>
        <p>THE REV. G. H. GARRETT, FORIIERT.Y VICAR OF Wirmss. The Bev. G. H. Garrett, B.A. preached his farewell to the parishioners of Widne' s on Sunday morning, in St Mary's Church, and in the evening, hi St. Paul's Church. On Tuesday evening, Mr. Garrett was fonnally instituted to the incumbency of St. Paul:s Princess Park, Liver- pool.</p>
        <p>SWIMMING HANDICAP. At the Boundary-road Baths, St Helens, on Monday, the first monthly handicap in connection with the St. Helens Recreation Swimming Club resulted in J. Mart- lew, with five seconds start, securing the gold-centre medal by about half a yard from F. Bridge (9 secs.) whb received the silver medal.</p>
        <p>FATALITY ON THE SHIP CANAL WORKS. About one o'clock on Thursday afternoon John Price, aged 18 years, of 6, Surrey-street, Runcorn, was killed on the Ship Canal Railway at Weston Point. Price was foreman on the engine Kenyon, and whilst uncoupling a wag,cn, he fell between the locomotive and the truck with his legs across the metal. Ile was run over by the engine and both his legs cut off. He was at once placed on an engine and conveyed to Latchford Hospital. He expired Just as he was being carried into that institu- tion.</p>
        <p>CRUELTY TO A DONKEY AT WIDNES. At Widnes Petty Sessions. on Thursday three boys named John Twambiey (I 3 Win. Twambley (11) of Cromwell-st., and John Philburn (12), of Sutton's-lane, were summoned for cruelly ill-treating an ass. On Sunday afternoon last, the boys were seen kicking and beating the Ras in a most unmerciful manner in a field. They then drove the animal to the edge of a ditch and pushed it into the mud and water, where it remained until pulled out by some men. The boys were ordered to pay the costs on their parents promising to adminis- ter a thrashing to them at home.</p>
        <p>TdE ROYAL BETROTHAL</p>
        <p>It is officially announced that Her Majesty re- ceived on Wednesday evening the news of the betrothal of her beloved grandson, the Duke of York, to Princess Victoria Mary ef Teck to which union the Queen has gladly given her consent. His Royal Highness George Frederick, Duke of York, is eldest surviving son of the Prince of Wales, and is in his twenty-eighth year. having been born on the 3rd June, 1885, just seventeen months after his late elder brother the Duke of Clarence. The two brothers, who were much attached. eutered the navy together in June, 1877, and in 1890 the Duke of York was appointed to the command of the gunboat "Thrush" on the West In- dian station, opening the Jarnacia Industrial Exhibition ,in the same year. In October, 1891, he visited Ireland, and contracted there a dangerous fever, but gradually recovered In the early part of the following year. Through the death of his much beloved brother he be- came heir to the throne, after his father. On the 28th June last year his Royal Highness took his seat in the House of Lords as Duke of York, and on the following day hoisted his permatit en board the cruiser "Melampus" , The Princess Victoria Mary Augusta Louisa Olga Pauline Claudine Agul, only daughter of the [duke and Duchess of Teck, was Weft On the 26th May, 1867, 80 I 8!)1,j8hi her 24th year.</p>
        <p>FAILURE OF A WIDNES GROCER. At the Liverpool Bankruptcy Court, on Thurs- day, the case of John Fitzpatrick, grocer and town couucillor, cams before Mr. Registrar Cooper. Mr. F. J. Mole appeared for the debtor, who was a grocer and provision dealer in Widnes, and a coun- cillor in that borough, and Mr. W. Rudd representd the petitioning creditorS. The etatetnent of tifialrli showed liabilities of £837 and assets £l4O, less £27 12s. 9.1. claimed by preferential creditors. Replying to the Official Receiver the debtar said he commenced business in 1878 with Mr.J. A. Johnson as partner. They traded together as grocers and provision dealers at Simm's Cross, Widnes, and afterwards at 58, Victoria - road, Widnes. They could not agree, however, and the partnership was dissolved in 1881. He carried on business at the Victoria-road shop until the 3rd ult., Mr. Johnson attending to the Simin's Cross shop. At that time he assigned all his property to Mr. Thraves, a Liverpool accountant, for the benefit of his creditors, after holding a meeting with them, and oa the advice of his solicitor; In addition to these shops he had also traded at a shop at 59. Mersey-road, but owing to business falling off he sold the shop to the manager in January this year for £l2O. The cause of his failure was bad trade.—The Official Receiver ordered the debtor to file a cash account from January 1, 1892, and the public examination was adjourned to the Ist June.</p>
