      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>PRICE OF IGNORANCE AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THE PRICE OF IGNORANCE AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The usual batch of parents who neglect to send their children to school were brought before the IVidites magistrates on Thursday. Amongst them was one named Margaret Carr, of 30, Cromwell-st., who was brought up in respect of her son George. Mr. Danby, Clerk to the School Board, informed the Bench that the defendant and her husband had previously paid 86 fines under the Education Act. The Bench ordered the woman te pay the 67th, and the five shillings was duly forth- coming. A calculation shows that the parents have paid £l6 1. fQr the privilege v.,f p ftlw:,4ting their thilOren.</p>
        <p>11/11itEM)A CONGRE4ATIONAL CHUBCI RUNtORM. Under the auspices of this chapel an " At Home" was given on Wednesday evening, in the school.' oorn. rue pastor, the Rev. J. Simpson, presided. 'there were a large number of persons present, and during tbe eveniug an interesting programme was gone through. The fol- lowing sang selections in excellent style :—Messrs. Weedall, W. Leathwood, and E. Worrall ; and the Misses Heatherington and Bowden. Duets were ren- dered by Mr. Wcedall and the Misses Ainsworth and P. and M. Heatherington. A pianoforte solo was given by Miss Cartwright. Mr. Outhwaite's party also played a selection of music. A cornet solo was given by Mr. R. W. Pierpoint, and a solo on the mandolin was contri- buted by Mr. F. Smith. A dialogue was also given en- titled " A Wife's Mistake," by the following :—Mtsers. C. Temple and E. Roberts, and the Misses E. Stone' C. Owen, and M. Heatherington. During the interval re- freshments were partaken of, and at the close a collec- tion was made to defray the expenses. Much praise is due to Mr. James Steele, draper, High-street, for the effective decoration of the room for the occasion. RUNCORN WESLEYAN CIRCUIT SUNDAY SCHOOL UNION. The annual meeting of this union was held on Wed- nesday evening, in Brunswick School vestry, under the presidency of the Rev. W. Arrowsmith, in consequence of the enforced absence of the Rev. T. Wilkes, through indisposition. Among those present were the Rev. J. Hanby, Mr. E. Taylor (secretary), Messrs. A. R. Norman and E. Marshall (visitors), and representatives from the following schools :—Brunswick, Mill-brow, Camden, Halton, and Weston. The secretary gave a statistical report, showing that in the four town schools there were 153 teachers and 1,016 scholars, and the amount collected for missions during the year was £124. In the country schools there were 69 teachers and 653 scholars, and the sum subscribed on behalf of missions was £3l. The totals in the union were-222 teachers, 1,689 scho- lars, with £155 collected E,r missions. rhe visitors gave an account of their visits to the schools in the circuit during the past year, and made several recommenda- tions, which will be forwarded to the officials of the respective institutions. An animated discession took place on the various points raised. Votes of thanks were accorded to the officials for their services. Messrs. E. Marshall and J. M. Frith were appointed visitors for the ensuing year ; and Messrs. B.Crosby and W. Milling- ton were elected secretaries.</p>
        <p>TONIC SOL•FA. TRACKING IN RUNCOKR.-4 GOOD REWARD. The conclusion of the session of the Runcorn Tonic Sol Fa Classes was signalised on Monday evening in Runcorn Public Hall by the performance of Mr. George F. Root's new cantata " The deliverance of England." The platform was occupied by a choir of 200 voices, , composed of present and past pupils of Mr. John Hal- ford, L.T.S.C., and grouped on a specially constructed Igallery, they presented a pleasing appearance. The members wore an emblem of mourning, as a token of I respect to the memory of Miss Ball, who should have taken part in the proceedings. but had suddenly died ' during the previous week. The cautata treats of that interesting'historical event, the defeat 'of the Spanish Armada, and the principal characters were Queen Elizabeth, Mrs. Timperlev ; Lady Catherine Howard, Mrs. Leathwood ; Lord Leicester, Mr. T. Burrows ; Lord Howard, Mr. E. Worrell; Arthur, Mr. E. 0. Evans. The principals acquitted themselves with credit, and the choir, guided by Mr. Holford, who weilded the baton, gave an excellent interpretation of the work. The audi- ence, which was of large proportions, considering the adverse condition of the weather, frequently gave de- monsrations of warm approval, and the performance was a decided success. Mr. Win. Cooke efficiently played accompaniments on the piano. During an interval Dr. McDougall presented the icertificates to the success- ful students.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>TIUNGS HEARD .4,.ND SEEN AT</head>
        <p>TIUNGS HEARD .4,.ND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>EY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Canon Maitland Wood mentions in his magazine that the Easter just passed was the seventh he had spent in Runcorn and adds that he sees many reasons for thankfedness."</p>
        <p>Smallpox is again rapidly spreading in Runcorn. There are now over '2O patients in the hospital and a number are being treated at their own homes.</p>
        <p>--- " Deliverance of England was the title of the performance given by the Tonic Sol re clasa, but the proceed,inge altogether were a splendid demon- stration of the " Deliverance of Runcorn," from the charge of indifference to music. The eulogiums passed upon Mr. Ifolford by Dr. McDougall ars fully deserved. The record of pupils who have passed through that, gentleman's hands is one ef which Mr. Holtord has every reason to be proud. May The sol fa classes continue to prosper.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Water Rill bas passed the Hones of Lords, but not without undergoing some modifica- tions. The most important is that referring to the saspension of repayments of principal for five years. The Lords have struck this clause out. It was in expectation of this concession that theßoarZ of Commissioners calculated that they coald sue- ceed in carrying On the works without burdening the rates; we may now expect to have Itel. or 21. added to the district rate in consequence of the purchase of the Water Company's undertaking. Like Mr. Speakman we shall have to bear the bur- den in order that posterity may benefit.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. Preston and Mr. JaMOS Pritchard have returned from their sojourn in the east. The tour has been full of interest. • The Rev. James Handley of the Runcorn Wesley- an circuit, has been appointed to the illackpool circuit for 1894.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED INFRINGEMENT OF TRADE Matut.—.-Mr. Justice Cave has recently had before him for trial an alleged infringement ef trade mark case, the parties being the A. Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Cempany (Limited) e J. Davies. The plaintiffs claimed damages for • infringement of their trade mark, and an injunction restraining further infringements. The plaintiffs who are the famous C,mvarly founded by Mr. Gwilym Evans. in 1874, proved that the defendant, who carried on business in Swansea under the title of "Quinine Bitters Manufactury, Swansea." had infringed upon their trade mark by copying their labels very closely, and drawing up circulars which would tend to confuse the two Companies. Defendant opened his business in 1890 after having been a wholesale customer of the plaintiffs. Judgment for the plaintiffs was given for , damages, with costs, end the injunction applied for was 'granted.</p>
      </div>
