      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>THE PAROCHIAL COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of the new-elected members of tho Frodsham and Frodsham Lordship Parochial Committees was held in the Town flail, Frodsliam, on Friday •even- tug, there beimg present—Messrs. A. Thomas, J.P. , (chairman). James Percival, Thomas Lewis, H. E. Hey- wood, T. Davies. A. Lineker, P. Riley, W. E. Shore, R. Bate, G. Beech, H. Tiles', J. M. Williams, I Thomas Eartam, Colonel Ashton (clerk), and Mr. J. Farrington (inspector of nuisances).—lt was resolved that Mr. A. Thomas be the chairman of the committees. —A *letter was from the Local Government Board, inti- mating that they would proceed with the arrangements for one of their inspectors to held an inquiry as to urban powers and as to proposed loan for water works.—On the motion of Mr. Heywood, it was resolved that the Local Government Board be asked to send down an inspector at the very earliest opportunity.—A long re- port was read from Mr.L. B. IVells,C.E., of Manchester, on the works required with regard to the sewage oetfall works at flea Flans, which were required to be kept in order by the Manchester Ship‘Canal Company.—A letter was read from Messrs. Morrell and Son, on behalf of Miss Perryn, in whether the committee were now Los position to proceed with the purchase of the land at Overton required for the reservoir.—The clerk was re- questedae acknowledge its receipt.—A letter ,was Also read from Mr. W. Diegle, the road surveyor, st-iting that hem Information rec,-ived, the repairs to the conduit in Church-street, Frodsh m , could not be done by the Highway Board. after r.crue discussion, it was resolved to write direct to the Clerk of the West Eddisbury Highway Board, drawing the Board's Attention to it, and asking that steps be taken to make the watercourse ea f for foot passesigers.—The Inspector submitted the analysis from Dr. J. Carter Bell, the county analyst, of a sample of water taken from the trial boring at Frodeham, of which the follow- ing is a copy:—" Second sainple of water taken from the borehole on the 23rd Merck, 1893. Grains per gel- len Total solids,27 ; total minerals, 1 ; loss .an ignition ; chlorine, 2-5 ; oxygen required to oxyclise organic matter -002-15 minutes, .1.10-3 hours. Hardness.; rather hewed ; colour, very clear ; parts per million free ammonia. nil ; albcininoid ammos.ia, •01 poisonous metals, nil. This is a first-clase water." —Mr. Heywood referred to ii. report which was in -circulation in Frodshain, that the water scheme was a -failure, and said he hoped that fo;1 publicity would be given to the analyst's test. It was decided that the Inspector send another sample ca the water to the analyst, in order to obtain his opinion as to the action of the water on lead piping.—A bad 'case of overcrowding of cottages at Bradley, in which theft were seven persons residing, rive adults and two children with only one small sleeping apartment, was reported. The attention of the medical officer was directed to be called to the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>TEE MANCHESTER SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>TEE MANCHESTER SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>• PROBABLE DATE OF OPENING. At Wednesday morning's meeting of the Manchester City Council, Alderman Sir John Harwood, made A state mint as to the urgent necessity for making provision for the new trades which would inunediately be brought into Manchester and district by the Ship Canal. Sir John, who is the leading representative of the Council on the Ship Canal directorate, also stated that he was quite confident the canal would be opened in January next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <p>,</p>
        <p>• . i . ~ ~ . . . , 4 • . -•-.. . . , . __.__ • . .. -. . . . _ ... - - ._ . , ,</p>
        <p>SALE BY AUCTION AT FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>-- Before a large company at the Bears Paw Hotel, Frodsham, on Tuesday evening, the Frodsham Farmers' Auction Co., Limited, offered for sale the picnic and pleasure grounds known by the name of " Belle Monte "on Overton Hills. Mr. Jones the auctioneer said the property was freehold and fully licensed, and it was seldom that such an oppor- tunity was offered in that district of acquiring so ' valuable v lot. It was exceedingly well adapted for the purpose of which it bad been used, and anyone desirous of carrying on a similar business could not acquire a more beautiful and suitable place. The views to be obtained from Belle Monte were not surpassed in three adjoining counties, and it was within easy distance of Manchester and Liverpool and many large towns. There was a capital dwelling-house, two bars, a large tea room, a small tea room, an excellent supply of water, and in fact every accommodation and everything in good order for carrying on the business. 'rho grounds were well laid out and there was a walk on the hil's which could not be surpassed. It was mentioned that the takings in ‘'t hit•week had amounted to .2240.—The only bid made was 521,000 made by Mr. Ben Rigby and the property was thefore withdrawn.—There were also offered use thirteenth share in the Frodsham Town Hall Company (said to be originally of the value of £64) leaving an average of five per cent. The bidding started at £4O and quickly rose by small amounts to £64 10s., when a share was knocked down to Mr. W. Farrell, jun., of Runcorn. Two ten pound shares in fradsharn Gas Company, fully paid up were sold to Mr. Henry Briscoe of Runcorn for £32 103. The bidding commenced at £39. Messrs. Linaker and Linaker, Run- corn, were the solicitors for the vendors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held at the Town Hall on Thursday. Mr- F. Wiswall presided, and there were present Messrs. H. J. Grice, J. Meadowcroft, Dr. Mangan, D. Bisbrown, E. W. Humphreys. T. Lea, W. Tayloz,W. Hankinson, J. Dobson, H. Abel,F. A. Lake (law clerk), E. Marshall (secretary), M. Barker (surveyor).</p>
        <p>THE LAW CLERK. Before the business commenced, the Law Clerk thanked the Board for the kind vote of condolence passed at the last meeting to himself and Mrs. Lake. If any. ing could have healed their sorrow it would have been the universal sympathy they had received on all bands and not the least had been that of the members of the Board and his brother officials.—The Medical Officer (Dr. McDougall) reported: The number of deaths registered during the past month amounts to 27; of these one was an inquest case, and of the remaining 26 deaths, 6 were due to pneumonia. 3 to diarrlicea, 2 to pthisis, 3 to mes- enteric disease, 2 to bronchitis, and one each to mal- formation of the spine, apoplexy, vurpuric variola, 'debility, cancer, erysipelas, kidney disease, and dip- theria and one of uncertain origin. The 27 deaths shows! ! an average death-rate for the twelve months of 162 and ! exclusive of inquest cases of 156 of the estimated popu- lation—The Surveyor said there were 35 deaths in April I' last year and 39 in the previous year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>HEALTH COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>HEALTH COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee recommended that the sum of £2 be paid to Mrs. Eleanor Jones, Greenway-road, as compensation for the destruction of infected bedding, that a mortuary be construtted at the hospital.—Trio minutes were approved on the motion of Mr. Hankie- son.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>RIVER WEAVER TRUSr, COUNTY COUNCIL. BOUNDARIES.</head>
        <p>THE RIVER WEAVER TRUSr, COUNTY COUNCIL. BOUNDARIES.</p>
        <p>1 - _ The Parilamentary Committee reported that a letter been received from the Clerk to the County Council en- closing a communication from the Overseers of the poor of the township of Runcorn relating to the alteration of the boundaries of the Runcorn North and Runcorn South Electoral Divisions asking the Improvement Commissioners to express an opinion thereon. The committee recommended that the matter be deferred until the Boundaries Committee had reported. They further reported that the petition apainst the River Navigation Bill had been referred to them, and they recommended that it was not advisable to affix the seal of the Board to it. Mr. Humphreys moved the adoption of the minute with regard to the Weaver, and that the remainder be referred back to the Committee. Mr. Lea seconded, Mr. Taylor contended tkat the Board ought to deal with the subject at once, and other members expressed a similar view. Mr. Bisbrown expressed regret that the petition against the Weaver Bill had not been adopted. He strongly urged that the Weaver should be managed by a public and representative body.—Mr. Meadowcroft said, not a single fact had been adduced to justify their interfer- ence with the Weaver Trust, and he urged that, as a local authority, they had no locus standi in the master. Eventually Mr. Humphreys motion was passed. When the ordinary business was concluded the letter from the Clerk to the County Council and the enclosure was real. After giving the resolution passed by the ratepayea in favour of the alteration of the boundaries of Runcorn North and Runcorn South, the overseers pointed out that the existing division was originally arranged for Parliamentary purposes and only included Clifton and Weston with 394 electors. These they understood were to be formed into a separate polling district which will leave a comparatively small number in Runcorn South. The suggested alteration was the natural division of the township, inasmuch as the Bridgewater Canal which formed a distinct line of division. The electors have always considered the northern divi- sion too large and the southern division too small (Run- corn North 2,575 electors, Runcorn South 902), the al- , teration will transfer 451 electors to the South, leaving , them 2,124 and 1,353 respectively. The overseers ven- tured to hope that the Committee would see their way to recommend the change as it was one in which the electors were keenly interested. Mr. Humphreys con- tended that the subject had been referred to the Com- mittee, but Mr. L. Taylor denied this, and Mr. Lea said he had seconded the motion because he understood that the subject would be dealt with by the Board that morn. ing.—The Law Clerk ruled that as the lettir was ad- dressed to the Board they could deal with it, notwithstanding the motion which had al ready been carried.—Mr. Dobson thereupon moved. that "this Board stronlyrecominends the County Council to take steps for the re-arrangement of the two polling districts by constituting the Bridgewater Canal the boundary line between the electoral divisions." Mr flankinson seconded. Mr. Humphreys again urged that. the matter should be considered in conjunction with the division of the town into wards.—Mr. Taylor said there was not the slightest connection between the two.—Mr. Meadowcroft opposed the motion.—Mr. Humphreys moved, and Mr. Meadowcroft seconded an amendment that the subject be referred to the Committee.—This was defeated by six votes to three,—Mr. Nlcadowcroft gave notice that at the next tneeting he should move that the resolution be rescinded inasmuch as they had received no notice of it.—Mr. Humphreys gave notice that lie should move that the CountyCouucil be request- ed at the same time that they were holding this inquiry to inquire into the advisability of dividing the town into I 1 wards.—Mr. Taylor and Mr. Grice remarked that the resolution passed took effect at once notwithstandin„v the notices given.—Mr. Meadowcrolt said be questioned that.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>FMINCE.</head>
        <p>FMINCE.</p>
        <p>The FinanceCammittee reported that dnving the past month the n-ceirts amountsed to £7O 4s. Se general fund. Vie treasuser's balances were : General fund overdraft E 98715. 7d.. 4i nking £438, Techncal Instruc- tion amount i:2.84 13s. Id., loan suspense account— overdraft £7OB 12s. Bd.—The minutes were passed on the motion of Mr. Ilankinson.</p>
        <p>sYMPATIn ACK NOVI LEDGER . Mr. William Leach, writing from Southport, acknow- ledged the•reeolutiou of sympathy passed by the Board. He was thankful to say that he was feeling stronger and hoped througtt God's blessing in a few weeks to be again with the members.</p>
        <p>TRE "WATER TULL. the+ SPcretary asked for a uthoritv to; draw a cheque for £ll3Dr: Mr. C. E. liker., in order to pay the fee of the llouseifif Commons for the Water Bill.—The Law Clerk moved and Mr. 1,1.1 nkinson seeowiled a resolution aecordingly.—Mr. liumidireys said it would have been very muchtetter &amp;they had given some information as to how the ail! had.got on in the House of Lords before asking for loch a cheque.—The motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>WRF.Y.LY REPOOLT OF THR 3fRALTII</head>
        <p>WRF.Y.LY REPOOLT OF THR 3fRALTII</p>
        <p>We.nelNGTost. During the week, which was the 17th ef the curreat year, the following births and deaths were registered-:—Births : Warrington, 44 ; Latchford, 5; total, 49. Deaths: Warrington. 214 ; Latchford, 5. total j. The excess of births over deaths was 24. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 23.5 per 1.000, agaimst 20.2 the average of the previous .four weeks, ande.gainst 251 the corresponding week of last year. The death-rate from the seven chief xymotic diseases was equal to 1.8 per 1,000, against il.B,the avereele rate of the four previous weeks, and against 2-8 the corresponding week of 1892. The ages of mortality were :—Warrington : otol, 2; 1 to 5,42; Ste 15. 2; 15 to 25, 0 ; 25 to 60,5.3; 60 up. ; total, 20.5. The deaths included-4 from small- pox ; 1 from diptheria ; 1 from influenze ; I from gout ; 1 from puperal pe.mmia ; 1 from rickets ; 2 from phithsis ; 4 from cld agt; 2 from brain disease; 6 from lung disease; 1 frem heart disease ; 1 from droweing. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported dun ug.the week :—Smallpex,ls; scarlet fever, 6; dipthesta„ 0; typhus fever, 0 ; enteric fever, 0 ; relapsing fever,o ; puerperal fever, 0. lifetreology. The mean temperature WAS 54 Fah. and therefore 110 higher than the preced- ing week. The miximurn temperature 75F., was recorded on the 24th inst., and the minimum temperature 391., on the 24th inst. The rainfall measured.o4 inch. The general direction was SE., the reading of the Anemometer denoting 735 miles.—Jome 11. GoittteeL, ledical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>I The Social Questions Union made a promising i' start on Monday night. The Nonconformist minis- ters were there in good force, but the Church clergy were conspicuous by their absence, although invi- tations had been sent them. Tnis is only one more 1 proof that if reforms are to be carried out, it is the Nonconformist and not the Church clergy who will undertake the work. Of course this does not apply Ito the laity, as in many respects, they are far in advance of their ministers. There were some strong things said at the meeting, but the Rev. D. W. Roberts capped them all by saying that the War- rington slums were worse than the slums in the 1 East End of London. If this be the case, it is high time there should be a reform in the lower parts of I the town, and a union like the Social Ques- tions Union would do much in that direc- tion. Mr. Birchen showed this very pointedly. 'by his assertion that the reason why tnagis- trates did not put into force a number of ex- isting laws was, that there was not sufficient public opinion behind them. The object of the proposed , union is, among other things, to educate public opinion in these matters. Tnere was a great deal I said about the insanitary condition of old property I in Warrington, but Mr. Haley very properly de- nounced some of the new property which is being thrown up in the town. He said that he could break some of the bricks, which were used in the erection of some houses, with his hand. It will be remembered that the Rev. J. W. Varley. at the Bap- I ' tist bazaar last week, also mentioned the poor ' quality of Warrington bricks. Numerous other matters were discussed at the meeting on Monday night, and if the union proves successful it will be a great blessing to the town. At any rate it de- serves the support of every right-thinking man.</p>
        <p>I am glad to hear of the further progress in America of an old friend Mr. James F. Bate, for- merly assistant librarian at the Warrington library. Mr. Bate embarked utiou a journalistic career some four years ago at Chicago, but about twelve months ago he severed his connection with the Chicago" Herald " and entered into an engagement on the New York "World." I have just heard that he has now resigned his post on the" World," to accept the assistant editorship of the "World's Fair Bureau," run in conjunction with the Chicago " Herald." Another item of personal news comes from across the" briney " too. Mr. John P. Booth, carpenter, who left Warrington for America a few months ago, is engaged in the Government section Of the Worlcl'e Fair.</p>
        <p>It is not pleasant to learn that there is a super- fluity of lunatics Just now. Yet that is what may be inferred from a statement made to the Board of Guardians on Wednesday by their clerk Mr. Sutton. The counts, asylums are full,and the officials of some Unions appear to be in great difficulties to find places in which to place the unfortunate patients that came under their care. To send lunatics out- side the county means, of course, extra charges upon the rates, but Mr. Sutton announced that he and two members of the Board had made terms with the Rainhill superintendent, which would facilitate the placing of them in the asylum.</p>
        <p>There is a gruesome interest taken by many people in Warrington in the facts surrounding the terrible tragedy which occurred at Holy/laid, Gres- ford, near Wrexham, on Tuesday. Both the mur- dered woman and the man who murdered her and afterwards committed suicide were well known to many Warrington tradespeople with whom they usod to do business. No longer ago than Easter week, they were together purchasing stationery at a certain stationery shop in Bridge-st., and a little before that they visited together a sadler's shop in town. At each place they were constant cus- tomers, and I am told it was the custom for Shel- lard to order what, wac required, whilst Mrs. Whittle paid the money.</p>
        <p>Yesterday I had a chat with a Warrington trades- man who knew the murdered woman from girlhood She was the only daughter of a retired sadler named. Morton, who lived at Whitley, Cheshire. When he died his daughter showed a fondness for modes of enjoyment of an unbecoming character, and so thoroughly did she enter upon those questionable pleasures that quarrels between herself and her mother were constant, and it is said that her maternal relation died of a broken heart a few years ago, when it was known that her daughter had plunged in a life whose sole pleasures were betting on horse races and frequent potations from the cup that inebriates. She was only twenty-eight years of age at her death,and my informant assured nee that she was even up to her demise a very handsome woman. How to account for demise, rela- tionship with Shellard, he did not know, unless it was the moulding influence of betting upon her, as Shellard was a " horsey " man. He had been her groom, and was a very unprepossessing and ignorant man. He influenced her before her marrage with Councillor Whittle, of Chorley, (who is a much respected gentleman), and my informant says that he had an influence over her which she often tried to fight against, but unsuccessfully. She bad been sent to Gresford to see if the change of surround- ings would wean her from her obnoxious habits,and destroy the influence of Shellard; with a resultthat has shocked the whole country.</p>
        <p>A correspondent writes :—" Those interested in the Society for the Suppression of the Opium Traffic will be pleased to hear of an effort made by three little girls Miss Laura Campion, Miss Nina Gerrard, and Miss Nelly Gandy, to send a contribution to this society. They met together to make various small articles for a little sale, whicli they held on Satur- day last at 20, Parker-At. It consisted principally of doll clothes, bags of sweets, text cards, &amp;c. Mrs. E. T. Reed was asked to open the sale for the young people, which she did, expressing pleasure in see- ing this result of bringing the subject before the young people at one of the meetings of" the Chris- tian Endeavour Society connected with the Em- manuel Church." The result :of the sale quite exeeeded the expectations of the youthful pro- moters."</p>
        <p>MERLIN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>nrscoßN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>nrscoßN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held on Tuesday. even- ing. Dr. McDlogall presided, and there were pres- ent the Rev. Canon Wood, T. Handley, E. Imison S. Taylor, and G. Moss (clerk). THE ATTENDANCE AT SCHOOL. . _</p>
        <p>The visitors 're—uorts showed that the number of children on the rolls was 3,721, against 3,689 in the corresponding; month last year. The average attendance was 3,109 acainst 2,689, percentage ot attendance 83.5 against 82.8.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Tile FAIMMICT OF SCHOOL FEEL</head>
        <p>Tile FAIMMICT OF SCHOOL FEEL</p>
        <p>A letter was submitted from Mr. Ashton, clerk to Run- corn Union, statin?, that the Board of Guardians had had ctihnicliderreocownistildi within atthi eo nd,i s ttrl !me t . p aFyrmomenttneofmrsecuhmooaltfaenecsefoorf iatmaopupnetareodf ptuo 1:1hi ce la3cobaorodi was li e directed atepa to accommodationttL) a sttilifleeerehrsi nagnw ititoillisob' uluffit paid,cpi esntm en t of fees for children supply the deficiency. The Chairman said the parents . fifteenromveirjetaltirteeheasondnounnidiiiroar t that tyears. H e wouldvi a child had a perfect right to select which school the child should attend, and the Guardians had no right to compel the child to be sent to another school where no fees were demanded, by reason of its parents poverty._ At the request of Mr. Handley the clerk road the tenth section ot the Education Act 1876, which directed the Guardians to pay the fees of children who were unable to pay by reason of poverty, and that this payment did not deprive the parents of any right or privilege, GP , subject them to any disability or disqualification, nor eatclon yot the further rei dtffhneecortt paortfottvehni that should the payment be made on the condition that the child attend any public elementary school, other than fees W ato s :ortibecausetet necessaryht teiec child i l tud: aritiaeaknneds such as might be directed by the parents, nor refuse to any particular elementary school.—The Clerk was ißpnaosyat rrtduhct edei t clause and to add that is the opinion of the School ion for free schooling.</p>
