        <p>GOLBORNE.</p>
        <p>GOLBORNE PRIHROSE ----LEAGITE The annual t „Ntio.s. of the Golborne habit ation f n.theu Prti,tenrrostej.:e' 'le wa'; heldwas the Saturday,niaki ng when a laortre in e demonstration sht rraotwkr, Illatose::1117:in: _Ma to tea of the arrangements fo, al busineis iqotTbohreintleppraiirsatc.ip week in August. For this event it was a its,the second : the annual Lady Gerard had undertaken to foci , announced that Milner, M.P., who is a relative or A.T.or.Sir Frederick of either Lord Randolph Churchill, Al, ire the attendance expected that the Garswoorl he . her ladyship. It is Gerard is dame president, will Atation, of which Lady habitation in making the f; unite with the Golborne success. Mr. T. D. (Iriail," Amonstration a gigantic Drayton were reappointed A-Dray.ton, .1..1",,, and Mn. president respectively, w , knight president and dame cillor and Mr. Gregson . ,Ith Mr. Rubinson ruling coon- 410[1. secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>UNIVERSAL BOON.</head>
        <p>A UNIVERSAL BOON.</p>
        <p>It is seldom that any discovery of science is such as will benefit all classes of people. Same of the greatest discoveries of the age. while proving beneficial to thousands, have also at least for a time, injured other thousands. Ev n some of the best medicinal preparations, however, much they may do to remove one class of disease or suit some constitutions, at the same time either deepen the hold of another disease, or leave -it entirely untouched, or, if they benefit one class of patients they may injure another. Now,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>GREAT NEED OF THE AGE.</head>
        <p>THE GREAT NEED OF THE AGE.</p>
        <p>111 BOMB scientifically compounded preparation which will effectually cope with the prevalent diseases of this country. which will be certain to do good when fairly tried, which will be equally adapted to the needs of the merchant, the profes- sional man, and he who wins his bread by the sweat of his brow, the clerk. the factory hand, the miner, the workers in metals, and the roadside labourers. It should also be such a reparation as contains no injurious ingredients, and which may be taken with impunity by the weak and sickly child or tie delicate female, as well as by the stronger constituted moan. Such a preparatioa would indeed be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>BOON TO HUMANITY.</head>
        <p>A BOON TO HUMANITY.</p>
        <p>After long-continued and diligent research, this much- needed boon has beea found, and repeatedly tested by analysts, who declare it to be pure and harmless. This desirable preparation is that wellknown Vegetable Tonic railed Gwilyra Evaas' Quinine Bitters, and it has gained such a widespread reputation for efficacy that many unprejudiced doctors. who have given it a fai: trial, frequently reeommend it to their patients in cases of great Weakness, Nervousness, er any disorder of the digestive organs. Gwilyrn &amp;vans Quinine Bitters is so invariably efficacious and produces such satisfactory results, that when once tried it is always recommended. It has proved successful when all other medicines had failed to give relief. It has also In many cases been found to be permanently beneficial when other preparations at best only give temporary relief. Gwilym B vans' Bitters is purely vegetable, and contain s neither</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>MERCURY NOR IRON,</head>
        <p>MERCURY NOR IRON,</p>
        <p>aor any poisonous or other injurious substances, and thou- sands who have benefitted by its use agree in saying it is a specific for all symptoms of Indigestion. Nervousness. and Weakness. It is also confidently recommended for Low Spirits Liver Disorders, and Chest Affections. The unparalleled success of Gwllym Evans' Quinine Bitters has induced unprincipled persons to offer base and spurious imitations. somewhat similar in name and appearance, but possessing none of the virtues of this great national remedy. Remember that none are genuine unless the name GwilYnt Bvans is on label, stamp, and bottle. Sold by all Chemists, ia bottles, at is. lid., 2s. 9d. and 4s. 64. each, or may be obtained, carriage free, direct from the Proprietors :—Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>NOT" POPPING THE QUESTION</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court on Saturday, a domestic servant, who gave her name as Kate Garbin, was charged with stealing a gentleman's English silver laver watch valued at £5, and a lady's silver lever watch valued at £3. the property of Mr. Benjaloin Millichap, 'bus driver, 477, Knutsford-road. The Chief Constable said prisoner had been a domestic servant at the com- plainant's house for some time, but left two or three weeks ago. The prisoner went to the pawnbroker's shop known as " The Tower " and offered the gentleman's watch for sale to Mr. Dalton, maxager. Be put some questions to her, and she said her name was Mrs. Ball, wife of James Ball, 477, Knutsford-road. Mr. Dalton was not satisfied with her manner, and called P.C. Leithwood, who brought her to the police station. Near tin police station she was seen to drop the lady's watch, by John Warrington, the" waif " and Mary Whittaker. John brought the watch to the police station. At the police station the prisoner said that Mrs. Ball (the daughter of the complainant) had given them to her to sell. Wm. Millichap said both of the watches belonged to him, the gentleman's watch having his name engrav- ed on it. After evidence by J. Dalton, M. Ball, M. Whittaker, and P.C. Leithwood, the prisoner said she left her house at 2.40, and Mrs. Ball asked her (the prisoner) if she was going to Warrington. On replying " Yes " she asked her if she would take two watches for her to sell. She replied that she had better take them herself. Mrs. Ball then said " take them, you will do just as well as me, and ask 50s. for my father's watch." She took them off her, never thinking it would cost her any trouble.—The magistrates committed the prisoner to the next Quarter Sessions, allowing bail in two sure- ties of £lO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>LOOTING LODGER.</head>
        <p>A LOOTING LODGER.</p>
        <p>William Parr, a —collier,was charged at the Warrington County Petty Sessions, on Wednesday, with stealing on the Ist May,a silver watch and chain,and 3s 7d .in money, the property of Patrick McCormick, lodging at 83,Legh- street, Earlestown. From the evidence it appeared that the prisoner came to the house of Mr. Fahey, on Friday night and applied for lodgings. He lodged at the house until 4.30 on Monday morning, when he took frem the waistcoat pockets of Patrick McCormick, another lodger the watch and money, and left the house very hurried- ly. McCormick who had been downstairs getting ready for his work, went upstairs for his waistcoat, and found that his watch and money had been taken. He informed Wm. Fahey, his landlord, who ran after him, and in- formed P.C. Mason, of the theft, who apprehended him in Mill-lane, Newton. In reply to the charge, the prisoner said " He didn't know what possessed him to take them." He was sent to goal for one month hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAL—Before Mr. John Burgess (in the chair) Alderman Chandley, and Mr. Lewis Voisey. TEMPI' OF A CARDIGAN JACXET.—Patrick Carroll, was charged with stealing from a field in Wash-lane, on the sth inst., a cardigan jacket, value 35., the property of Morris Wm. Wheeler. The Chief Constable stated that the prisoner lodged at 27 Dickenson-st., and that on the sth inst., the boy Wheeler, was employed setting pota- toes in a field in Wash-lane, when he pulled off his jacket and left it near the hedge. When he went for his jacket at 12 o'clock it was zone. The following day he found that the jacket had been sold to Thomas Jeffery's employed on the Ship Canal, for fourpence by Carroll. After evidence by Morris Wheeler, Mary Jefferys, Thomas Jefferys and D.S. Monaghan, the magistrates sent the prisoner to gaol for seven days' hard labour. A DICSERTER.—CharIes Murray, was charged with being a deserter from the West Riding Regiment. Ser- geant Cheeney stopped the prisoner the previous day, and he in answer to questions, admitted being a deserter from the West Riding Regiment, stationed at Strensall, since the 21st inst. He was sent to Strangeways to await an escort. A BATCH or Snagezes OUT.—Wm. Parsons last from Northwich, was charged with sleeping on a haystack, the property of Mr. Robinson, farmer, Orford. Prisoner was sentenced to seven days' hard labour. John Smith, John Parley, and Peter Kerry, were all charged with sleeping in the stable of the Bay Horse yard, rented by Mr. Wm. Delooze. P. C. Turner, said about 2.33 he was on duty near the stable when he heard snoring. When he got to the stable, he found the door was fas- tened from the inside. After considerable trouble he unfastened it and found the three prisoners. Smith, who is an old offender received one month hard labour, and the other two prisoners seven days' hard labour each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <p>MONDLL—Before the (Mayor, Alderman E. Greenall) (chairman), Alderman Francomb, Alderman Harrison Messrs. F. Monks, W.Pierpoint, and S. Wright. ____ .</p>
        <p>CRUELTY TO a HORSE.—David Towers Widnes was charged with illtreating •a horse by working it while in an unfit state on Sunday morning. P.C. Lewis said that prisoner was coming down Wilson Patten-street with the horse and a van on Sunday morning. The horse had to stop frequently from pain. Witness followed prisoner and stopped him at Bridge Foot, and took him to the police-station. When at the police-station, Mr. Robinson, horse slaughterer bought the horse for 53. and it had since been destroyed. Inspector Dowty, R.S.P.C.A., said he examined the horse and found it in a very .emaciated condition, suftering from mange and other ,diserders. The horse was totally unfit for work, and they could not find anyone who would take it into their staAlle, owing to its condition. Prisoner was fined 103 including costs. Veenaarr.—Edwin Verdon, no settled abode, was charged with begging in Orford-street, on Saturday night. P.S. Chantler said that he caught prisoner in Orford-street with his pockets full of bread, and having 91. in his possesiion in halfpennies. Prisoner was ordered to leave the town. ' DRUNK AND DlSORDEetr.—Bridget Mooney for commit- ting this offence on Saturday night in Dial-street, was discharged on promising to sign the pledge. John McGuire and Mary Sinnett for the same offence, were fined ss. or seven days' imprisonment. ASSAULT ON A RAILWAY DETRCTIVE.—Edward Lewis of Earlestown, was charged with being drunk and dis- orderly on Bank Quay Station, and also with assaulting railway detective William Eden, on Saturday night. Detective Eden said that he was told to eject prisoner from the station as he was making a disturbance through missing the last train to Earlestown. Witness told him to leave the station quietly, but he refused to go, and took off his coat and wanted to fight. Witness closed with him, but prisoner got on the floor and took hold of witness's legs throwing him on the ground. They were struggling together for about twenty minutes until a policeman came and prisoner was locked up. The Mayor characterised prisoner's behaviour as abominable, and if he had his own way, would give him a long sentence, but the magistrates inflicted a fine of 103. and costs or 14 day's imprisonment with hard labour. FErtocious Do° AT Lae:.—Henry Tranter, 71, Cart- wright-street, was charged with suffering a ferocious dog to be at large, unmuzzled. P.S. Taylor said that in consequence of a complaint, he went to the house of Mr. David Ellis, 23, Cartwright-street, on the 23rd, whose little girl had been bitten by defendant's dog. The dog was a black retriever, and witness, on making inquiries, found that two other children had been bitten by the same dog. Defendant said that he was sorry for what the dog bad done, and he bad destroyed the dog. Defendant was fined 2s. 6d. and s:osts, making a total of £1 Os. 6d., or in default 14 days. CHIMNEY ore FiaE.—For this offence, Thomas O'Day' 12, Green's Court was fined is. TUISDAY.—Before Mr. R. Garnett (chairman), and Mr.</p>
        <p>E. J. Guest. DRUNK LSD DISORDERLY.—Martin Burns, Horsemar- ket-street, on Monday, fined 53. and costs or seven days imprisonment ; Annie Ryan, Bridge-street, 14 days' imprisonment Thomas Hall, Priestly-street, ss. and costs or seven days' imprisonment. SCHOOL CasEs.—John Barnes, King-street, 23. 6i.; Ambrose Foden, Tilley-street, boy sent to the ship Clio; Ellen Hayes, Eg.erton-street, 55.; Samuel Ryles, Quay Fold, 55.; John Leonard, Bewsey-street, 23. 6d. ; Hannah Mapp, Friars Green, ss. ; Patrick Nestor, CockhedgT, ss; Thomas Shovelton, Winwick-street, 2s. 6d. ; William Webster, Trinity Place, 23. 6d. ; John Messenger, Mersey-street, son sent to' the Ship Clio.</p>
        <p>WEDN-ESDAY.—Before Mr. John Geddes (in the chair),</p>
        <p>_ . Alclerman Wright, Councillor J. H. Fleming, and Mr. John Fairclouv,h. . . .</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND BISORDERLT.—Annie Quinn was charged with being drunk and disorderly at 10.40, behind Boy- den shop,lying on the floor, and making a disturbance. P.C. Turner said she had to be carried to the Police Statien.—She was fined ss. and costs, or seven days. THORSDAY.—Before Alderman Chandley (chairman), _ _</p>
        <p>Alderman Sutton, and Councillor James Fairclougli. THEFT OF A Vim—James Hestor was charged with stealing from the yard of Messrs. Roberts, Dale and Co., Mersey-street, a vice the property of Walter Cooper, on Saturday. He was seßtenced to one month's hard labour.</p>
        <p>A SOFT "COP."—David Fleming was charged with steal- ing, from the Public Benefit Boot Company, on Wednes- day, a pair of men's boots. The Chief Constable said, that en Wednesday, Herbert Rowden, employed in a shop opposite, saw prisoner pick up the boots from the door of the Public Benefit Company, and walk coolly away. John Pitchford also saw the act, and informed P. C. Leithwood, who followed prisoner into Friars Green, and arrested him. Prisoner said with a laugh "This is a soft cop." The officer took him to the Police Station. Prisoner was sentenced to six week's hard labour. _ _ . . -</p>
        <p>THerr OF A MAT.—Bridget Murphy was charged with stealing on the 22nd ult., a door mat, the property of Charles °Laeld, hairdresser, 72, Winwick-st. She was fined 103. and costs or 14 day's hard lat..-mr.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY, —Before Alderman Wright (chairman), Coun- cillors Roberts and Fleming. . ,</p>
        <p>SUSPICIOUS CHARACTER.—James Towe-y was charged with being a convicted thief and with being found fre- quenting Bridge-st.,on Tuesday, with intent to commit a felony. Detective Monaghan said that he stopped pris- oner and searched him, finding in his trousers pockets three spoons. He said that he had bought them at Bolton.—The Chief Constable said that the police had been unable to find any owner of the spoons, bitt they intended to keep them. Owing to numerous previous convictions, prisoner was sent to gaol for 14 day's hard ,labour.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT ON THE POLICE.—Henry Wilson was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Winwick-street. on Thursthy, and with assaulting P.C. Lewis. The officer stated that prisoner and a man named Cunningham were drunk and refused to move away from Central Station, when requested. Witness took Cunningham into custody, but prisoner rescued him, and took wit- ness's whistle and threw it away. Witness then arrested prisoner, and after great difficulty, brought him to the police s tation. Prisoner was sentenced to 14 day's imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Major Pickmera (in the chair),and Mr. J. J. Bleckley. STONE THRONTING.—Arthur Henson, Sankey Bridges, was charged with throwing stones on the 30th April, and doing damage to the house of Mr. .T. Wright to the ex- tent of 4s. Complainant said he cr-light the boy throw- ing pieces of brick on his house, and after taking his name he went and examined the slates and found four broken. The boy was find 103. including costs.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. MONDAY.—Before Major Flazlehurst and Mr. C. Reynolds.</p>
        <p>BICYCLE RIDING ON FOOTPATHS.—Thomas Hayes, a youth of 10, Duke-st., was fined is. and costs Bs. 6d. for riding his bicycle on the footway at Aston by Sutton.</p>
        <p>Doc AT LARGE.—John Povah, of 49, Blastyre-st., was fined is. and costs for allowing his dog to be at large without collar or muzzle.</p>
        <p>A PALTRY CASR.—Edward Thompson, tlacksmitli, Ellesmere-st., was summoned for assaulting Thomas Dutton, a joiner of the same street. The magistrates characterised it a paltry case, and dismissed the sum- mons.</p>
        <p>RICH, PURE, AND FRAGRANT 1S • &amp; Is. 4d. PER LB.</p>
        <p>LB. CAN PRODUCE. 1s• • TAL• PER No Higher Price.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 6, 1893. FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 6, 1893. FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL BOARD PROSECUTIONS.—Thirteen parents were summoned for not sending their children regularly to school and the usual penalty of to. including costs was imposed in each case: .</p>
        <p>.. DRUNKENNESS.—John Howard, Mary H agan, and Robert Hardaster were each fined for this offence. _</p>
        <p>CARD PLAYING ON SUNDAYS.—George Shapherd, a middle-aged man, was charged with gaming with cards on the stonehills. It was proved by P.C.'s Juett and Gregory that the prisoner was one of a gang of men who were playing cards on the Stonehills on the previous afternoon. A fine of ss. and costs was imposed.—James Williamson, a young man, was charged with gaming with cards on the previous day.—P.C. Juett said the prisoner was one of a gang who were playing on the playground.—P.C. Gregory captured the prisoner with three cards in his possession. There was a two gallon bottle, which had contained beer, and a glass on the ground. BEOGING.—Two young men named James Williams and John Miller were sentenced to 14 days hard labour for begging.</p>
        <p>WIDNES POLICE COURT. MONDAY.—Before Mr. H. Wade Deacon.</p>
        <p>FINED FOR DRUNICENNESS.—NieId Feeney, no fixed abode ; George Whittaker, John-st. ; John Floolighan, Margaret-st. ; Patrick McCabe, Marsh-st. ; Griffith Jones Lugsdale-road ; John Sweeney, no fixed abode ; Eliza- beth Glasheen, Edward-st. ; and John McGee, Victoria- st.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT FROM A PAWNSHOP—John Howard, no fixed abode, was charged with stealing a pair of trousers from Mr. Houghton's shop door, Victoria-rd.— A remand for a week was granted.</p>
        <p>..• Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note that reports of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the bead office, Bewsey-street. Warrington, not later than ten o'clock on Wednesday morning. --- CRICKETBRS. Lawn Tennisers, and Golfers are reminded that GEO. G. BUSSEY &amp; Co. manufacture high-class requisites. Their new catalogue is obtainable from Local beaters, or the Sports Manutactory, Peckham Rye, S.R. Trade CCa (C Mark.</p>
        <p>RUGUY. WARRINGTON v. LEIGH.</p>
        <p>A mOre untntercsting game has not been seen on the Wilderspool ground for some time than the one between Leigh and Warrington tfirst teams, and evidently the exclusion of Leigh from future engagements has not been relished by the natives of the Bedford district. The visitors arrived late, and when they did make their appearance, the players were attired in jerseys of so many different hues that is not looked for from school- boys' combinations 'now-a-days. There was a capital gate, and the chief idterest was centred in the two new Welshmen who were doing duty for the Leighites, Fitz- gerald and Wilding. Coop started</p>
        <p>The fFiret Quarter, the high wind compelling the game being divided into four divisions. Turner's response was attended by quick following up on the part of Holmes and Higham, and capital passing by the Bates, Evans, and Potts brought about a permanent footing inside the Leigh quarters. Evans, Holmes, and Bandies tried to break through, but Anderton was safe is his tackling, and after Ewan had got the oval away to Heesom, the latter got his kick in under difficulties, and'the game ',was keenly fought on the Leigh lines. The home forwards passed well, but a few minutes after,Bate got finely away,and the movement being taken up by Holmes, the latter passed to Thorni- ley, who pushed Coop on one side and a try was notched under the posts, Heesom placing a neat goal. Leigh now attacked, mid Boscow was collared behind the posts but the scrimmage near the line was cleared by Nevins, who had taken Jchn Bate'a (retired injured) place behind the scrimmage, Turner and Wilding exchanged kicks with advantage to the Welshman, and it was left to Evans and Heesom with successive kicks to take the game direct to the Leigh 25. Thorniley again looked , like scoring when he passed to Randles, who was held I under the posts. Success, however, attended an effort I by Nevins, who took a chance smartly and ran in, the , point remaining unproductive. A change of ends now took place, and after capital play by Boscow. Law- less, and Potts, a cross kick saw Fitzgerald scoring, J. Coop makings good attempt with the place. Nevins and Heesom invaded the Leigh quarters by clever pass- ing along touch, and then another rush headed by Berry and Thorniley, was saved by Wilding. Good running by Anderton gave the visitors a chance, but BOSCOR was safe, and a quick return to the Leigh lines saw Potts ' missing a glorious chance, and half-time was called with the score as Warrington 1-1-1, Leigh 0-1-0. The third quarter was of an uninteresting description, tke visitors lying on the ball and adopting all manner of tactics to stay the progress of the homesters. Nevins ran in a clever try, but beyond this the game is not worth account, and the final result was Warrington 1-2-1 ; Leigh, 0-1-0. Warrington : J. BOSCOW, back ;C. Potts, J. W. Evans, and F. Heesom, three-quarter backs; John Bate and James Bate, half-backs ; Ashton, Turner, Nevins, Holmes, Berry, Bandies, Highain, Thorniley, and Lawless, forwards. Leigh : Coop, back ; Fitzgerald Anderton and Wilding, three-quarter backs ; Ewan and Shovelton, half-backs •, Smith, Boardman, Hawson, Macmaster, France, Eccleston, Winstanley, and Higgins forwards.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. ST. HELENS RECREATION.—PIayed at Run- corn. Both teams were short of prominent men. A nice shower made the atmosphere rather pleasant. Riley started. Crompton picked up and passed to Warder, who dropped a magnificent goal. Restarting from the centre, play was quick,but chiefly forward. There was some good passing by Runcorn ; Faulkner,Bollingsworth, Crompton and Warder taking part. Crompton scored, Warder kicking a goal. After this,play was of a rather desultory charactbr, and free kicks were frequent. At half-time the home side had scored 2 gaal to Recreation nothing. After the interval Runcorn held the upper band,the for- wards playing strongly. Warder made a sprint along the touch scoring behind the posts. From this the Rec- reation side made a strong attack, but the defence of Runcorn was extremely safe. Final scare:—Runcorn 2 goals 1 try, Recreation nil.</p>
        <p>WIDNES AND DISTRICT CHEMICAL WOBICS FOOTBALL COMPETITION.—The final contest for the gold and silver medals given by the United Alkali Co. and Whines Foot- ball Club respectively, was played on Saturday on the Widnes ground, before three thousand spectators. Half-time result : Sullivan's. 1 try; Widnes Alkali, nil. Eventually Sullivan's won by a try to nil. Harrison and Dawson just before the game closed were ordered off the field. Mrs. Brock afterwards distributed the medals.</p>
        <p>caught at mid-off. Smith and Clarke being easily dis- posed of, the game looked a certainty for Leyland, but thanks to a capital stand by Clarke and F. Williamson time was played out, and on the stroke of time, and with the score at 61 for six wickets, Clarke was bowled for an excellent dozen, and the game terminated in a draw. Score and bowling analysis : LRYL•ND. WARRINGTON. Whiteside c Sugg b J J W Mortimer run out 3 Crosfield Kershaw b S M Cros- J J Crosfield c Gerrard b field 6 Nash 2O Ward b Smith A Tinsley not out 6B R Smith b Tongs 3 Pike et Clarke b JJ Cros- G Clarke b Nash 0 field W Silcock o Clarke b S M F Williamson not out 4 Crosfield 1 Bell Hill b R Smith Gerrard bJ J Crosfield Stanning Nash to bat Tanga . .</p>
