      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>"'MOOSE GRAPPLIN' IRONS ! "</head>
        <p>"'MOOSE GRAPPLIN' IRONS ! "</p>
        <p>A SOLILOQUY.</p>
        <p>"No wonder that aw'm feelin' bad, An' look in' very glum; Aw've lost my greytest chaunce i' life, An aw'm welly strucken dumb ! " Fer two'thry yer my breetest thowts Wur fixed th' "Public" line; But neaw that is another's. so It never con be mine ! I "Aw us'et to fancy aw shud like To hang my hat up theer ; An' sit an' welcome SW my friends To reet deawn honest cheer ! " " Wen aw behowd that Tavern dur, The seet on't makes me ill : Avi've hardly nerves to go agen, An' hey another gill ! "Aw geet so low, aw cudn't sleep, Aw uset to sit an' mope; , Aw felt so mollincolly, just Ez if aw'd lost aw hope! "Aw geet so very deawn one neet, Aw crope eautside a hole ; An' thowt aw'd eend it, theer an' then,— Aw did, upon my soul!</p>
        <p>" Aw just abeawt wur slippin' in, Wen sumdy heerd me sheaut ; An' run fer grapplin' irons sharp, To try an' fish me eaut ! " By Gyow ! lads, it wur very nigh The final stroke fer me ; But aw shud ne'er ha' grievd if they lled let my body be ! "Still, thank the Lord !it did me good, -Arl An' neaw aw'm wed l an wick 74'7 Aw'm glad ez ever aw con be, • We chetted that Owd Nick ! ruMl•Prf " It wur a lesson just i' time, An' raised my sperrits up ; Once moor aw go quite reggilar, An' ha' my pipe an' sup ! "Aw feel no bally-warchin' neaw, , 7 :-.•5 Aw'm happy ez a king ; teAra r Aw'm quite another mon agen, An' daily daunce an' sing! " Thoose Grapplin' Irons did my trick, An' saved my precious life ; Th ay'n raised my pluck, an' showed me heaw To do witheat a wife! " Like David did to Jonathan, Aw'll hug urn to my breast; Like th' mon before thay hung him sed, " Its maybeaw fer th' best I' " An' if aw shad get richer, wi' A widow's wealthy charms, A pair o' Grapplin' Irons, lads, Shall be my Coat of Arms !" TI.IM 0' PEGS. Rose Bonk, 4,5, 93.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>PATtAFFIN LAMP FATALITY AT EAELESTOWN.</head>
        <p>THE PATtAFFIN LAMP FATALITY AT EAELESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The inquest on the body of the lad Charles cllveen, who was fatally b urned by the upsetting of a paraffin lamp, was held at the Wellington Hotel, Earlestown, on Friday, by Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner.—Mrs. Mcllveen, mother of the deceased boy, said she was the widow of Thomas Mcllveen, of 56, Earle-st., Earlestown. The deceased was eleven years old. She had another son named William 10 years of age. On Sunday night she left the kitchen, and the boys were on the sofa. A paraffin lamp was in the centre of the table which was close to the sofa. She ' was away not more than two minutes, when she heard 11 a noise. She had gone to get a book off the parlour sideboard. She heard a crash, and she rushed into the kitchen, and saw the boy on the floor blazing. She ob- tained assistance and managed to put out the flames. A doctor was called in, but her son died the next night, able Monday,about 10 o'clock. Her son William would be to tell the jury how it happened.—William Mcllyeen, bright little fellow, 10 years of age, said on Sunday night he remembered his mother leaving him and his brattier Charles on the sofa. She went into the parlour, and the lamp was burning on the table. His brother I kicked the table cloth with his feet, when he was kick- , ing them about, and the lamp was upset. The lamp fell on to the rug. The table cloth was set on fire, and the burning oil ran towards his brother while he was on the sofa, and set him on fire.—A verdict of "Accidental death" was returned.</p>
        <p>Tint POPULAR BEVERAGE tor Breakfast, Luncheon, Tea and Supper in all seasons is CADBURY'S COCOA.—Comfortlng. strengthening, nourishing—for old and young, robust and feeble. CADBURY'S COCOA is absolutely pure.—dfdrot.</p>
        <p>A military riot is reported from Aldershot, the men of the 28th Hussars having become greatly incensed against the Cameronians, owing to one of the Hussars having been knocked about in a music hall. The military police, assisted by the West Yorkshire Regiment. quelled the riot. IturruaE.—All who suffer from rupture, should write at once to S. J. SHERMAN, Hernia Specialist to his principal Chambers, 64, Chancery-lane, London, for his new publica- tion on ruptures, post tree. His branch chambers, are at 26, King-street, Manchester, where he attenels every alternate week, beginning May Ist, 1893.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S V. WARRINGTON.--The last Association match of the season in:Warrington, was brought off at Dalton Bank on Saturday. Warrington played four substitutes. Half-time arrived with the score—St. Elphin's, two goals; Warrington, nil, Tickle and Collin having been the scorers. Resuming, Warrington im- mediately pressed, and found Bates plenty of work. Pierpoint sent in a good shot, which the goal-keeper was unable to clear out, and Howgate put the ball through. Warrington again pressed, and Forbes scored, the Saints protesting. The home forwards were unable to get away with the ball, the wind being very strong, and Phillips defeated Bates but he was ruled off-side. The Saints played up pluckily and as a result cf their endeavours, the third and winning goal was notched. Fennelly made a good attempt at goal in the last minute. Final result :—St! Elphin's, three goals : Warrington, two.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>- -</p>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY v. BRWSEY-ROAD WESLEYANS.—This match was played on the ground of the latter, and resulted in a victory for the Trinitarians. Final score : —Holy Trinity 31; liewsey-road Wesleyans 27.</p>
        <p>CEMETERY WHITE ROSE V. PADOATE WESLEYANS (Juniors).—This match was played on the ground of the former on Saturday, and resulted in a win for the home- sters by 45 runs to 39. Dawson and Malpas scored well for the Juniors, and Haigh and Fairclough for the White Rose.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. LEYLAND.—The opening of the War- rington cricket season was attended by unseasonable weather. A bitter cold day, assisted by occasional showers, rendered the day anything but agreeable for this summer pastime, and there was no wonder that the " gate" was of moderate dimensions. Winning the toss, Leyland took up the batting. Tinsley for Leyland played a brilliant game of 68 runs " not out," whilst Whiteside came next with 23 runs. Warrington opened their venture with W. Mortimer and Sugg, a disaster- ous start being made. With the first ball of the match Sugg played on, whilst seven runs later Mortimer was badly run out. A better complexion was placed on the game when the Messrs. Crosfield became partners, and Tonge relieved Hill. The change came from the opposite end, for at 32 Nash caught S. M. Crosfield, whilst 31 runs later J. J. Crosfield was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>ALLEGED ILL-TREATMENT.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED ILL-TREATMENT.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court, on Monday, Hy 01liver, last Prom Chester, was charged with being drunk and disorderly at the Workhouse gate, and with wilfully breaking a window at the porter's lodge, doing damage to the extent of is. 6d., on Sunday morning. Mr. Arthur Browne appeared for the defendant—James Curry, Workhouse porter, said that about 3-45 on Sunday morning, prisoner came to the Workhouse door in Guardian-st., and commenced kicking and ringing the bell. On being asked what he wanted,he replied that he wanted to get into the Work- house. As witness, who spoke to him from the room window, did not let him in, he flung some stones at the panes of glass upstairs. Witness dressed himself and came downstairs, and when he opened the door, prisoner threw another stone at him. Witness then took a stick, which he produced, and which prisoner subsequently called a " life preserver," and went out to prisoner who had his hands full of stones. When witness showed pri- soner the stick he let the stpnes fall to the ground. Prisoner was drunk, and he gave witness and two inmates some trouble to get him into the Workhouse,and a police officer was then sent lor.—ln answer to Mr.Browne,witness tiid that prisoner was not ringing three or four hours at the ' gate, and he never struck prisoner with the stick, and did not know how prisoner came by the serious injuries he had received.—Two inmates, Michael Mackie and Peter Morris, corroborated Curry's statement--A woman who lives near the Workhouse, also gave evidence, and in answer to Mr. Browne, said that prisoner asked Curry to be admitted into the infirmary.—P.C. Baxendale said that about 12-30 on Sunday morning,prisoner came into the Police Station saying he wanted to stop there all night, but witness told him to go to the Workhouse. Prisoner was drunk at the time.—The Chief Constable explained that if a person comes into the Police Station wishing to go to the Workhouse, if that person is sober, the policemen will frequently go with them, but they do ' not like to take persons under the influence of drink as several disturbances have taken place in the Workhouse owing to this being done.—P.C. Cooper said that pri- soner was in a stone cell when he was called in to take him into custody. Prisoner made a complaint about Mr. Curry's behaviour, but said that he had not had all his own way. Mr. Browne said that his case was that prisoner had been ringing the bell for a long time, as he suffered from a painful disease, and wished to be admitted to the infirmary. He threw the stones at the window to attract attention. Prisoner says that when Curry came out, he brought his " life preserver " and gave him repeated blows with it, making his shoulder black. He also asserts that the inmate Morris, gave him a blow in the eye causing it to swell very much. The reason of his coming to the Workhouse at that time of the morning was because he had paid for lodgings at the Coffee House in Horsemarket-st, but unfortunately, had not arrived until after eleven o'clock at night. He had accordingly no place to go to, and that explained his visit to the Police Station and subsequently to the Workhouse. He admitted that he had had some drink. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Dr. Starkey Smith said that he was called in by the police to examine prisoner. He was unfit to be out of the infirmary. He had a large swelling an the side of the eye, and his shoulder was very much discoloured, which must have been done by violence. The wounds could have been caused by the " life preserver" produced. The wounds could not have been caused except by abso- lute violence.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wm. Pierpoint was of opinion that unnecessary violence had been used by the official, and that the ordinary "life preserver" (which Mr. Shearer admitted was in the Workhouse) had been used. Prisoner was handed over to the Workhouse officials to be taken to the infirmary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Comm—o-n-s Ton Monday, the Manchester Ship Canal (Additional Capital) Bill was read a third time and passed. Mr. ACLAND informed Mr. Hugh Hoare that managers of schools in receipt of State aid and giving free educa- tion could not claim payment for books and school mate- rials as a condition ibf The Hogs SECRETARY informed Mr. Bartley that he did not think Parliament could, at the present time, undertake the comprehensive revision of the law neces- sary in regard to directors who neglected their duty ; but meantime the matter would be considered by the ' Attorney-General. On the motion for going into Committee of Supply on the Civil Service and Revenue Departments Estimates, Sir C. DILKE moved that the time had now come when effect should be given to the declarations of successive Administrations as to the Government of Egypt. Mr. GLADSTONE concurred in the declarations of principle cited by Sir Charles Dilke, but argued that a declaration of the House, in whatever sense it might be made,would only by so much be a deduction from the power and influence of the Government to act in the best interests of the country. He hoped the motion would not be pressed. Mr.Labouchere,Sir Julian Goldsmid, Sir Rich. Temple, and Dr. Clark took part in the debate, and Sir Charles Dilke's amendment was negatived without a division.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords, on Monday, the Marquis of Londonderry resumed the debate on the Evicted Tenants Commission. Lord SALISBURY described the appoint- ment of a Commission under such circumstances as un- precedented, and, referring to Sir James Mathew, said that if they appointed a judge to execute a non-judicial function, they should have taken care that this was not connected with a subject in which he was known to entertain strong prepossessions. The subject then dropped. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday, Mr. ASQUITH. in reply to the Marquis of GraAby, said the question of registration of bicycles had been considered by the metropolitan police, but the opinion, in which he con- curred, was that the existing law, if vigorously en- forced, was sufficient for the protection of the public. Mr. GLADSTONE, replying to Mr. Keir Hardie, re- gretted his inability to hold out any encouragement as to setting apart an evening this week for discussing the Hull strike.</p>
        <p>The adjourned debate on going into Committee of Supply on the Civil Service Estimates was resumed by Admiral Field, and amonc., the subjects discussed were reformatory ships and Russia and the Pamirs. The debate was adjourned on the motion of Sir William Har- court, and a discussion ensued on the second reading of the Customs and Inland Revenue Bill.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords, on Tuesday, the Church Patronage Bill, the Police Acts (Amendment) Bill, and the Post Office (Acquisition of Sites) Bill were read a third time and passed. Some discussion took place re- garding a disturbance at a Church Defence meeting in St. James's Ball, on Monday, the 24th ult , and the alleged failure of the police to interfere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday Mr. WooDs moved the second reading of the Mines (Eight Hours) Bill. Mr. D. THOMAS moved the r2jection of the mea- sure,stating that the miners in his constituency (Merthyr Tydvil) believed that it must inevitably reduce their wages. Mr.JoicEv seconded Mr. Thomas's motion. Mr. GLADSTONE said the Government did not feel that this was a matter in which as a Government they would be justified in Interfering. A large majotity of his collea- gues were favourable to the bill, and he himself attached so much weight to the fact that a very considerable majority of the miners were in favour of it that he should vote for the second reading.He did not think,how- ever, that it would he safe until opinion had been far more ascertained, and perhaps matured, for Parliament to attempt the passing of a uniform and compulsory measure. In the subsequent discussion Mr. T. W. Liam and Mr. MATHER spoke against the bill—though the latter said he should not vote—and Mr. FLEMING and Lord R. CHURCHILL in favour of it. On a division the second reading was carried by 279 to 201.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lorois—o-n—iii;i-r-sday, tho Elementary Education (Religious) Bill was read a second time by a majority of U. The Local Authorities (Loans) Act (1891) Amendment Bill was read a third time and passed. The LORD CHANCELLOR, replying to Lord Stan- ley of Alderley, entirely disputed any idea that there was any more reason for a return of the magistrates appointed during the last six months than in any other six months, but added that the return which it had been customary to make to the House of Commons would make public all the inftgrmation the noble lord desired.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons, Mr. ASQUITH informed Mr. Keir Hardie that the soldiers and gunboats would not be withdrawn from Hull so long as the local authorities thought their presence was necessary. Replying to Mr. Hobliouse, Mr. ASQUITH said there was no intention to deprive lord-lieutenants of the right of recommendatLn of county magistrates. Mr. GLADSTONE, answering Mr. T. W. Russell, as to his grounds for saying that the Nationalist party con- curred in the proposal made in 1886 to exempt the north-eastern corner of Ir.:land from the operation of the Local Government Bill, said his impression was that the Irish Nationalist party did concur in the adoption of even so extreme a proposal, provided that by means of it they could open a read to peace.</p>
        <p>---- Mr. J. Havelock Wilson obtained leave to move tbe.„ adjournment of the House, in order to call attention ; 'the contravention of certain Acts of Parliament II 134 organisation called the Shipping Federation, Limited, e'„o the supplying cf the forces of the Crown in coun• ect!'o with a labour dispute." He accused the ShiPPlntr, Federation of concocting false evidence in orderted secure conviction against Labour leaders, and ins;ear that they were responsible for the disturbances and orders which had taken place at Hull and other placo" were Mr. ASQUITH said it was not the case that trool, ing sent to Hull in response to a demand from the ShIPP",,O., Federation. It was done on the demand 3f the Ing'oo, trates and the Watch Committee. Mr. John 1:3111:41 said the state of things in Hull was the precursor of a much worse state of things that . . , _ viog.•••</p>
        <p>- ensue in every part of the kingdom unless the illep; tactics of the Shipping Federation were resisted by rld Government of the day. Mr. MUNDELLA. said if it co°, be shown that the Shipping Federation or ancone had broken the Merchant Shipping Act he would at Of; institute proceedings against them. Mr. Glad sO.; agreed in condemning anything like an unnecess3l:t parade of military force as unwise and likely to defep the object of sending military to preserve the peace. .°. declared that picketing without abuse had legal sanct.itv and on his suggestion Mr. Wilson asked leave to Ifil" draw his motion.</p>
        <p>RUN CORN VOLUNTEER INTELLIGEO</p>
        <p>RISLEY AND ALTCAR PRIZE MEETINGS. tO° The Runcorn detachment having decided to sen- to men to Risley and live from each company to Cheshire rifle meeting at Altcar, three competitiovr have taken place to decide who should have the holt; the total of the two highest scores to count. r.A. Saturday witnessed the closing competition. 1"-$ marked with two asterisks will attend both ineetioft: and those with *tie asterisk will attend the Altcar rae ing only.</p>
        <p>F COMPANY.</p>
      </div>
