      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL SANITARY AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr. Mclntyre presided and there were present the Rev. J. W. N. Tanner, Rev. J. Hanby, Messrs. J. Knowles, (vice-chairman), John Weir, C. T. Whitley, E. Aaron, 11. E. Hey- wood, J. M. Williams, J. Brandreth, J. Stokes, John Parkinson, S. Woodward, T. Lewis, J. Ashton (clerk), G. Ashton (deputy clerk.)</p>
        <p>SMAI.LPDX AT WESTON. Dr. Adams retorted that a case of smallpox had oc- curred at Weston. This, it was stated, was the only case in the authority's district and it wEs decided that prompt measures be taken for isolation.—lt was deter- mined to maintain the hospital in its present condition tor another month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>RUNCORN UNION ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN UNION ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday morning, when Mr. Charles Reynolds, J.P., was unanimously re-elected chairman, and the following gentlemen were constituted a Smallpox Hospital Com- mittee :--Messrs. Williams (chairman), Whitley, Hey- wood, Stokes, Brandreth, Aaron, Hanby. Knowles, Jones, Weir, Parkinson, Mr. Williams desired especially to thank Mr. Jones, of Moore, for the great help he had been to the Committee and officials in the past in dealing with the outbreak.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>PENNY FLECK FARM,</head>
        <p>PENNY FLECK FARM,</p>
        <p>. Mr. W. G. Thompson, of Whally Range, again wrote with respect to the alleged disgraceful and unhealthy state of the house at Penny Pleck !cum. Not a single step had been taken by the authority to improve the condition of things, and the owner of the farm would do nothing. If the Union was pewerless to help his son he asked was he bound to remain on the farm and pay rent at the risk of his life ; and should he be struck down with illness whether he could sue for compensation and from whom. The reading of the letter caused much amusement, and Mr. Heywood advised the clerk to reply and charge 6s. Bd. (Laughter). After a short discussion it was deckled that the medical officer report on the subject.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>SEWERAGE AND WATER WORKS..</head>
        <p>SEWERAGE AND WATER WORKS..</p>
        <p>With regard to the resolution passed by the Authority, claiming that the initial cost of sewerage works and water works should be defrayed by the owners, the Clerk reported that the Bury Union had replied that although they could not agree with the resolution in tato, they were of opinion that the present mode of defraying the cost of sewerage works was inequitable, was much as the land owners do not bear their share of the cost in proportion to the extent of tke benefit received, South Shields Union had adopted the resolution with slight amendment, Poeklington Union had passedno resolution and Leigh Union could not see their way to adopt it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>NOTIFICATIONS OF DISEASE.</head>
        <p>NOTIFICATIONS OF DISEASE.</p>
        <p>The subject of the notification of infectious disease arose and the Clerk suggested that it would be well if the Medical Officer supply him with a list of the notifica- tions he had received for report at each fortnightly meeting.—Mr. Aaron agreed with this idea and to show the necessity of it, said a Runcorn medical man a week ago told him that Weston was a den of fever. In conse- quence he caused Dr. Adams to visit the township. Dr. Adams said there was no fever nor sign of fever in the place.—The importance of outbreaks of disease being promptly notified by the medical men in attendance was insisted upon and a resolution was passed requesting the medical official to supply the Clerk with a list of notifica- tions in time for each meeting of the authority. There was no other business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting was held at Dutton Workhouse OR Tuesday. Present Mr. J. Mclntyre, (ehairman), and Mr.J. Knowles (vice-chairman),the Revs. J. W.A.Tanner and J. Hanby,Messrs S. Taylor, T. Lea, A. H. Norman, S. Yarwood, D. 11. Jones, T. Handley, J. Weir, C. T. Whitley, E. Aaron, H. E. Heywood, M. Williams, J. Brandreth, J. Stokes, Jno. Parkinson, S. Woodward, E. Bretherton, T. Baxter, C.C., C. W. Davenport ; J. Ashton (clerk), and G. Ashton (deputy clerk).</p>
        <p>. _ PAUPERS AND INFECTIOUS DISEASE. The Local Government Board submitted for the perusal of the Board, a letter sedt by the Health Com- mittee of the Runcorn Board of Commissioners to them inquiring whether it was the duty of the Commissioners to maintain destitute persons in their hospital whilst suffer- ing from infectious disease. The Guardians were under agreement with the Commissioners to pay ter the treat- ment of pauper cases in the hospital,but they did not see their way any longer to pay for the maintenance of persons who,previous to being received into the hospital, were not in receipt of parish relief. In reply the Local Government Board intormed the Commissioners that no particular inhabitant of the district was empowered to claim'admission to the hospital as of right. The manage- ment of the Hospital was vested in the Improvement Commissioners and the Board thought it would be com- petent for the Commissioners to refuse to admit pauper patients into the Hospital except under an arrangement with the Guardians providing for the cost of the maintenance, Src., of the patient in the hospital where a person was not destitute and his removal to the Hospital was required only for the purpose of isolation, the Sanitary Authority, and not the Guardians, should deal with the case under the powers conferred upon them by the Public Health Act, 1875. If, however, a person attacked by infectious disease be destitute, either wholly or in part, of the means of obtaining all such necessaries (including medical attendance, nursing and accommodation) it seemed to the Local Government Board that it was the duty of the Guardians to give the relief that was needed.—Mr. Taylor contended that it was quite clear that the Guardians should pay.—Mr. Heywood dissented, and the Clerk said it was a ques- tion of when the person became destitute.—Mr. Brand- reth pointed out that the Rural A utliority had not charged any single person, rich or poor for treatment in their hospital.—Mr. Taylor said in Runcorn, people who could pay were charged and had to pay.—Mr. Lea con- firmed that statement.—Mr. Brandreth said the chief endeavour of the Rural Authority was to stamp out the disease, which appeared the most important duty.—Mr. Heywood said a committee of the Guardians had been appointed to endeavour to arrange matters, but at the meeting only be (Mr. Heywood) attended, and no de- finite arrangement could be made. It would be better if some amicable arrangement could be come to between the two authorities. It would be a block in the way of stamping out disease. if because people were destitute or likely to be in a few weeks had to wait until the reliev- ing officer gave an order and the medical officer gave a certificate before they could be removed to the hospital they might have the disease spreading to an alarming degree. On the motion of Mr. Aaron seconded by Mr. Handley, it was resolved to appoint a committee con- sisting of Messrs. Mclntyre, Brandreth, Heywood, Wil- liams, Norman and Handley. THE COUNTY ASYLUM. Mr. Alex Ilornby, clerk to the Committee of the County Asylum, Chester, informed the Board that for the quarter ending 30th June next, the rate of mainten- ance of pauper lunatics in the asylum had been fixed at 7s. per head per week. UNEXECUTED WARRANTS. It was resolved to draw the attention of the Chief Constable to the number of warrants remaining unexe- cuted against persons for deserting their families.</p>
