        <p>, 17, PICCADILLY, MANCHESTER, AND ler LEEDS. BRADFORD coA LONDON.</p>
        <p>"WHAT IS SALVATION?"</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln your recent editions, I saw letters by " Isos " asking "What is Salvation ? " and he is still asking it. He appears to me like a man going round a ring with a pair of gloves, asking anyone to put them on and try what he can do. It so happens, lam not the man. I got my learning when cockfighting and bullbaiting were at their height, but I will tell him something about sal- vation which I have learnt these 48 years. It is a great question, and, indeed, I cannot answer it fully. In the first place, I believe what the Bible says about it. I may say that if all the gas that is, has been ,and will be in the world, will not grow a gooseberry tree, neither will all I the wisdom that is, has beentor will be in the world save cne soul. Neither the angels In heaven nor God himself can save a sinner against his will. "By grace are ye saved; through faith, and that not of yourselves; it is the gif of God, not of works lest any man should boast." No doubt " Isos" has seen a crab tree somewhere in his life. It looks so much like an apple tree that it is mis- taken for one. Now,however if that tree is dressed and pruned, it xvi'l still yield crabs. There is only one way of making it bear apples, and that is by cutting the limbs of the tree and grafting the tree, and the fruit will be as good as the tree from which it was grafted. But how this is dcne I cannot say; it is a mystery to me. So is this great salvation. Every unsaved sinner is like the crab tree whether he believes it or not. He cannot bear good fruit, till Jesus Christ is grafted in him, and this is by faith in Him. Sir, suppose I take you to the river side and show you a ferry boat. I ask you "do you believe that boat will carry you ?" you say "yes !" That is not faith. You must get into the boat and rea- lise it for yourself, and then you will be able to know more than I can tell you. About thirty years ago, I attended a lecture in the Primitive Methodist Chapel, Old-road, Latchford. I had known the lecturer, the Rev. J. Barker. as a mihis- ter, Chartist lecturer, and infidel. He said that at about 23 years of age, he was a useful minister of the gospel, and always thought he would remain 89, but being of an inquisitive turn of mind, he found some defects in his creed. His sermons took a speculative and argumen- tative turn. He was determined to have his own way, until he turned on Christ altogether. After this he joined the Chartists, and helped them all he could, until he went to America, and then in the wilds and stillness, away from the busy life, God spoke peace again to his soul. Now God helping him, he would try to undo what he had been doing in late years. This is the gist of the lecture, and when Mr. Barker died, lie said he would die in the faith of the Lord Jesus Christ, and with a hope of everlasting life. This is the same Mr. Barker who, when lecturing in the Wigan Public Hall, , beat the record for blasphemy. He took off his coat and dared God to do his worst, if there was a God, which made some wish they were out. He found mercy, but it was a mercy he did. These lines do not give any encouragement to presume on the mercy of God. Get this salvation is the prayer of</p>
        <p>AN OLD CHARTIST.</p>
        <p>IS MAN AN IMMORTAL BEING?</p>
        <p>Sir,—Will you kindly allow me space for one or two letters on this subject? I have been attracted to it by some recent correspondence in your velualele paper. In the religious press of the times, as well as in the pulpit, there is no" article of the faith " which occupies so much attention, and is so emphatically insisted upon, as the doctrine that man by nature is an immortal being, destined to live for ever either in happiness or misery in some region to which the grave is the door. Our sermons are, for the most part, based upon it, and the various sectarian periodicals enforce it by the aid of fiction and other questionable devices. And yet the practical outcome of the labours of the preacher and editor is very small indeed. The things of this life are, apparently, of far greater importance to church and chapel goers than their destiny hereafter ; while the great hulk of outside professors live and die as if immortality and.heaven and hell were nothing better than myths to them. I do not infer from this state of things that the doctrine is false ; but I am certainly justified in pointing to it as showing how little the immortality theory has to do in moulding and guiding the lives of men. But sermons and periodicals are utterly inadmissible as data upon which to found such a far-reaching and momentous doenni as the natural immortality of created men. And I feel equally bound to reject the theories of beithen philosophers, and the crude fancies of learned gropers among the phenomena of nature for the same reason. To establish and confirm a teaching which places the poor, finite creature upon the same level as the infinite and eternal Creator as regards one of - His awful and nnsearchable attributes, we require nothing less than a Divine revelation. Such a revelation has never yet been made, and in its absence we must be content with what has been made known to us respect- ing the nature and destiny of man. Now, I believe most of the Sects are agreed that the Bible is the only true and infallible rule of faith and practice yet given to men, and I quite concur in this view. Well then, what does the inspired book teach about the natural or unconditional iinmortality of man? My answer is, absolutely nothing ! From the beginning of Genesis to the end of Revelation there is positively not a word or phrase standing alone or in connection with others which, either directly or by implication, affirms that man, by virtue of his nature, is an imnaor- tal being. This statement rimy startle some of your ' readers; but I make it in good faith, and have no fear that it can be truthfully contradicted. Such phrases as " immortal souls," " newer-dying souls," etc., which are se lavishly used in sermons and other religious produc- tions have no place at all in the Scriptures. Nor are there any expressions equivalent to them to be found in the sacred writings. Should the reader doubt the accur- acy of this statement he can easily satisfy himself by "searching the Scriptures," sat have done, with the aid of a good Concordance. This silence of the inspired penmen on such a tremendous subject asthe immortality of man is most remarkable, and I must leave the advoc- ates of the doctrine to account for it. The words "Immortal," and" Immortality "do indeed appear in the Bible, the former once, and once only, and the latter r flve times ; but the way in which they are used gives the lie direct to the popular theory of man, instead of supporting it. To make this clear will be my object in another letter.—Yours truly, lONA.</p>
        <p>THE IMMORTALITY OF THE SOUL. Sir,--4f the argument which "L'ver of Truth" gives in last Saturday's" Examiner" in favour of the idea of the "immortality of the soul" is the best which he has to produce, I am of opinion that he is likely to do little towards destroying the "Christianized Pagan Fiction," that man has an immortal soul. His argu- naent is equally applicable to other animals as well as man, for dogs, horses, &amp;c., are in possession of both will and intelligence, and by his line of reasoning "what is to prevent them from existing likewise." The idea of man having an immortal soul is certainly flattering to his vanity, but quite destructive of his chance of attain- ment of immortality seeing that the incentive to seek for eternal life is abrogated by that idea, for why should a men seek for that which he already hath. It is pretty generally conceded by candid critics, who profess to believe that man has an immortal soul, that the idea i 3 not taught in the Bible, but, as some say, it is "inferred rather than taught." But what follows if the Bible directly and emphatically teaches the opposite of the inference. Would it not be wise to consider the direct doctrine rather than continue trusting in one of man's ideas.</p>
        <p>Some Ofmy friends tel is me " God breathed the breath of life into man's nostrils and he became a living soul," and on my pointing out that there is nothing there stated about immortal soul, they retort, " it is what is meant, though it is not directly stated." A most em- phatic denial is given to my statement that the word</p>
      </div>
