      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>TI-TE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 6, 1893. NOTES ON GAMES AND AT OUR LOCAL CHURCHES, A nmEncs. _ WA It RINGTOPo . 4-444</head>
        <p>TI-TE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 6, 1893. NOTES ON GAMES AND AT OUR LOCAL CHURCHES, A nmEncs. _ WA It RINGTOPo . 4-444</p>
        <p>" soul " is used in the Bible for other animals as well as man, and when chapters and verses are given all they have to say is "I never noticed it." If by any stretch of imagination this very important animal "man" can be said to have an immortal soul, through becoming al living soul, then equally may "every beast of the earth,' every fowl of the air, every thing that creepeth upon the earth, wherein there is a living soul" also claim to be immortal, for they are spoken of thus before the record is given of man becoming a living soul.— See revised version marginal note Gen. 1. 30. It is a surprising thing to one to find men so persist- ently blind to the actual facts of the case, as regards the Bible teaching re man's destiny, awl at the same time see them accept a portion of its teaching which clashes so emphatically with the doctrine of the immor- tality of the soul. I refer to the resurrection of the dead and judgment, which would be an altogether unneces- sary and red-taped formality, if a man had already been in a state of joy or woe prior to being brought be- fore his Judge. I would put it to the readers of the " Examiner" who think their friends are in heaven, (did any one ever think his own particular friend had gone to hell ? ) and who also believe in the resurrec- tion and judgment ; is there any reasonableness, any order or fitness of things in bringing a man thence, at some future date, in order to see whether he has been sent to the right place? And at the same time I would ask them to consider the fact that at the judgment seat the good and bad are both represented as not knowing when they had earned their reward or how they came to merit punishment. Would they, think you, thus come confidently, if they had already, as is popularly supposed, been punished for hundreds or thousands of years, or, on the other hand, could they be in the com- pany of Christ, the apostles, &amp;c., Sm., for the same length of tire, and yet be in ignorance as to when they saw Him, hungry, thirsty. &amp;c. That some will come be- fore Christ expecting to be approved, who will be des- troyed from the presence of the Lord, is quite evident from the seventh chapter of Matthew. Paul says : Henceforth there is laid up for me a crown of righteous- ness, which the Lord, the righteous Judge, shall give me at that day : and not to me only, but unto all them also that love His appearing. Evidently Paul expected His reward at Christ's appearing. The advice of Justin Martyr (A.D. 150) is" 'fake care that you do not look upon those who say that the souls, as soon as they leave the body, are received up into heaven "—Dialogue with Tryphs, the Jew, Section 80. Neither Martin Luther nor William Tyndall believed in the immortality of the soul and Gibbon declares "The doctrine is omitted in the law of Moses."—Gibbon vol. 1. page 530-1. Coming back to the Bible, in conclusion I would point out that the word "immortal "is found but once in the scriptures-1 Tim. 1-17. _ _ _</p>
        <p>R. G. L.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The wheel of time has finished the turn of the season for manipulating the leather. And Warrington football enthusiasts have figuratively pointed the dexter finger to the horizon and said : "It's gone—The football sea- son." The last match was played on Saturday, the Leigh team being the visitors. It was a quiet match, and the death knell of the season seemed to be sadly pronounced as ever and anon, between the 'rounds of applause, could be beard the clink of the" willow "in fields hard by. The P.D. is already baitering for flan- nels.</p>
        <p>There was nothing about the match to cheer up the sad hearts of those who were saying good-bye to the game; there was nothing to stop those clammy tears, which did not well in anyone's eyes. The air was chilly and the players seemed slow because they paused so often to look at the ball—to take long lingering looks at it. In the first half Thornily and Nevins, each went ever the line, and placed the ball down, and the referee said " try." fleesom kicken a goal, Fitzgerald scored for Leigh, and afterwards NtVitlS scored again. And when the game ended Warrington had six pints—no ! I mean points. (Drat that imp of a PD). Leigh had troubled the scorer to hoist up 0.-14-0 for them. And he didn't mind the trouble.</p>
        <p>But since the season is dead, let us in a truly christian manner review its career before the undertaker, Time "hooks " it from our vision, alt6gether. Very well. Here goes with a plunge, deep down, into the depths of a year's statistics.</p>
        <p>" Yupt ! Spurzz ! Here we are !";The first team have played 34 matches, out of which lgewere won, 8 lost,and 7 drawn, against 43 matches played., 26 won, 10 lost. and? drawn last year. Not so bad is it P ' Lickings," ready made have been supplied gratis to Dewsbury, Rochdale St. Clements 2) ; Broughton Rangers (2) ; Stockport (2) ; Kendal Hornets (2) ;; Lane aster, Brough- ton (2) ; Mosley ; Wigan; Manchester (2) ; St. Helens Recreation, Barrow and Widnes. Then Swinton, Lan- caster, Salford, Tyldesley, Oldham,. Widnes and Wigan and Runcorn have kindly obliged us with defeats.</p>
        <p>Here are some rather interesting items of individual scoring :—Tries: John Bate, B ; Thornily and James Bate, six each ; Jolly, Plumpton, Heesom, Webster, five each; Evans, Ashton, and Potts, four each ; Fair Turner It. Lawless, Holmes, and Nevins, two each ; Berry, Tears, Barnes, Donohue, Bandies, HAmpson, Sudlow, Ashurst, and Frank Turner, one each. Placed goals: Heesom, eleven ; Fair Turner, six ;Barnes, three ; and Jolly, one.</p>
        <p>Now for the A team. " They aint been snoozing, eh 2" " Well not much—look :—They have won 20 out of 26 matches played ; lost 3, and drawn 3i Last year they pulled off 24 matches out of 30. They can claim yet to be the champion team of Lancashire. The only three teams who beat them were St. Helens Recreation, Roch- dale St. elements, and Salford. And the P.D. whispers —on the strict Q.T.—that they only allowed them to du it for fun.</p>
        <p>Now altlicuch Salford barred our local team and even the lions of Swinton, from taking a toast out of the Lancashire Cup, Mr. Percy Davies, the genial and generous president of the Club, is going to spread some cups amongst them. Bs is giving six. cups to members and 42 silver medals. The cups will go thusly :—One to Mr. H. Ashton, captain or the first team ; one to Mr. T. Davies, captain of the " A" team ; one to Mr. H. Rowe, captain of the second team ; one to Mr. Joseph Boscow. full back, in commemoration of his playing for the county during Mr. Davies's year of office, and for his sterling play for his own club ; one to Mr. James Jolley, for playing as forward to the county during the season, as well as for his excellent play for his own club. Then there is the sixth cup. This is going to the Parish Church team who pulled off the local championship.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY. The annlversary services in connection with Wyclife Sunday Schools were held on Sunday, and passed off most successfully. The preacher for the day was the well-known Welsh preacher, the Rev. E. Herber Evans,. D.D., of Carnarven, ex-chairman of the Congregational Union of England and Wales, and principal of Bangor Theological College. Large congregations were present at both services, the church at the evening service being crowded, over a thousand persons being present. The achool children were present at the morning service, the hymns being well s,lng by them. The chair under the leadeeship of Mr. G. Wallisigten, rendered the anthem "The Lord is my strength," Mr. and Mrs. Pearson tak- ing the solo parts. The preacher took for his text in the morning lieb. 4C., 14v. "Seeing then that we have a great high priest, that is passed into the heavens, Jesus, the 90 nof God let us hold fast our profession." The three chief points of his sermon were, Jesus, the glory of the past; the help of the present; and the hope of the future. lb the afternoon, a short musical service was held in the schoolroom, conducted by Mr. G. Walling- ton, the superintendent. Miss Green, of the Railway Servant's Mission, delivered an interesting and instruc- tive address to the scholars, Solos were sung by Mr. W. H. Wallington and Miss F. Booth. At the evening ser- vice, the solo and chorus "0 come let us worship" was suog, Mrs. W. H. Pearson singing the solo. The choir also sang the anthem "Here by Babylon's Wave." Mr. W. H. Payton presided at the organ with his usual ability. The preacher based his remarks from Ist Peter, Ist c. 25. v. (r.v.) "But the word of the Lord abideth for ever, and this is the word of good tidings which was preached unto you." Dv. Evans in his introduction re- ferred to the general epistle of Peter as bearing a greater resemblance of Jesus Christ's teaching than of any of the other epistles: The doctrinal and the practical were in- termingled with it. In speaking of the word of God he said he thanked God for his word, and that it came from him: Everything that came from the earth went back to theearth. Every old castle, every city, every human body, and all that came from the earth went back, but one thing had come from heaven, the word of God. We could not expect that teachers could meet all the array of questions in biblical history. It was not one of those books written by one man, or by a commit- tee of men, but a, book that took 1.500 years to complete and written by at least 4.0 men. The book was a divine revelation. The bible had withstood all manner of at. tempts to destroy it, but it had come out of the fire like the three Nonconformists placed in the furnace at Baby- lon by Nebuchadnezzar without being destroyed. He remembered when a lad going to watch soldiers practis- ing shooting at a target, and the school children were allowed to gather the old bullet% but many of the shots missed the target, and all struck an old rock, doing no harm to the rock but flattening the bullets. It was the same with the shots against the bible. He received catalogues of books written against the 'bible and al- though the leaves were uncut, they were offered at waste paper price. They were flattened bullets never to be shot again. Every infidel book was dead in one age, but the old rock,, the bible, remained without a mark upon it, and would abide for ever. An infidel in the "Times" newspaper had confessed Christ publicly, after reading for himself, the New Testament. The eloquent and impressive discourses at both services were listened to with close attention, and were much appreci- ated by all who heard them. The collections for the day amounted to £4, an increase on the previous year. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>DECREASE OF SMALLPDX. Vaccination is vexation ; Renewed 'tis twice as bad. Whiht the doctors puzzle me ; Smallpox drives me mad. An 011 Saw ziew'y set.</head>
        <p>THE DECREASE OF SMALLPDX. Vaccination is vexation ; Renewed 'tis twice as bad. Whiht the doctors puzzle me ; Smallpox drives me mad. An 011 Saw ziew'y set.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Thank heaven smallpox is less, Then 'tis said that vaccination has brought about this change, just as if there were nothing else in the world but vaccination. It is true that this hnprovement began only a little later than the introduction of vaccination, and this circum- stance enabled Jenner and the doctors to steal the credit of this improvement and give it to this their pet scheme. I use the word" steal" advisedly, because the change had been effected by other and more potent agencies. Vaccinators then and now have been well acquainted with this, and they have suppressed the truth in favour of vaccination. You see, sir, a man cannot speak the truth and maintain a lie at the same time. Well, I don't want to be hard with them. Facts were only so-and-so so they never mentioned them, that's all! What a beau- tiful thing truth is when it is on our side ; 'tis lovely. It makes one feel so awfully righteous too. On the other hand a man must feel very small when he and truth can- not agree. Now what vaccinators fight shy of is the following: Anterior to vaccination was the practice of inoculation, that is to say the practice of transplanting the smallpox by infusion of the matter from ripened pustules into the veins of the uninfected. This was spreading • the, I disease with a vengeance. To be sure they had a reM son for it. I have been told that man is a reasonable I creature and if he shoots his sweetheart, kicks his wile into a pulp, gets drunk or cuts his throat, he has a rea- son for the doing of it. Yes, he is a reasonable being, and the reason for cultivating this terrible disease was that it was assumed that each person must have small- pox once in life, and the disease was believed to be much lighter than when it came of its own accord. It was so light that thousands died, and vast numbers were blind- ed for life, or otherways disfigured. They had also another reason, namely, you could have it when you liked,and they argued that infancy was the best time to have it, on the score of economy, as there would be no working time lost when they should arrive at maturity. So the infants were inoculated with smallpox as soon as they were considered strong enough to bear it. Look- ing at this fearful practice is it any wonder that at the commencement of this century large numbers of persons who were met in public places were marked with small-1 pox. It was about the same time that Parliament voted Jonner £10,000,a nd then a further sum of £20,000 as a re- ward for the discovery of vaccination. At the same session I of Parliament they made a law forbidding inoculation with smallpox matter, and making the practice penal. Here then WRS a great source of this disease cut off at one stroke of his Majesty's pen. Nevertheless there had been a great deal of smallpox previous to the practice of inoculation which may be fairly accounted for by the habits of the people, by their diet, and by their neglect of sanitary matters.</p>
        <p>They were accustomed to be bled periodically and at special times. The reason for this suicidal practice—for, sir, they had a reason, or at least something which they thought was reason took unto itself this shape, namely, "that the blood was the stronghold of disease." This folly for which I cannot find a name, was taught by the doctors of that day. Bow like are these utterances in behalf of bleeding to those we hear now in favour of vaccination. "A child is a danger to society unvacci- nated." "When the child becomes a man he is still a danger unless he is re-vaccinated." After re-vaccination and he taken smallpox, he is still a danger. He dies of smallpox : "It's an exceptional case"; "there were com- plications " ; or some fault in the manner, the time, or , the matter of his vaccination ; in short, invention is i always ready with excuses when vaccination fails. Bleeding is dead and vaccination will soon be gone. But what I have to deal with now is the state of things that prevailed at the time when smallpox was , rife. Not only were medical practices bad, but the food and drink of our forefathers were such as tended to the I production of skin diseases—such as itch, scrofula, and I smallpox. Poor people had to eat barley-wax (or whac). Wheaten bread was not always to be had by everybody. Salted meat was the rule, especially in the winter. Tea, coffee, and cocoa were not in general use ; nor bad they the variety of vegetables and foreign fruits that we have. Beer was taken for breakfast, dinner, and supper. Sanitation was in a bad way of business at that time. Drains were scarce and badly constructed. Night air was considered an enemy to be avoided by all possible means. Beds heavily curtained, and a canopy that extended to the four posts of the bedstead, were con- sidered the proper thing. The mutcome of all these I things were scrofula, itch, smallpox, and other kindred diseases. Jenner tells us that he had received orders from the Commander-in-Chief to vaccinate the Eighty- second Regiment. Ile refused to do it, because every man, woman, and child had the itch. What an indus- trious lot of people! This shows us that itch, like king's evil and ernallpox,was very abundant. There were many cases of scrofula so late as 1830 They were to be seen frequently in any public place ; from this date their nuniber grew less rapidly. Now,what I want to point out is the fact that scrofula and the itch have long since left us,and that without the reputed aid of vaccination. Therefore, it is not too much to say that improved medical treatment, better diet, more healthy houses, saoitary surroundings, and the prohibition of inoculation have not only freed US from scrofula and the itch, but have greatly reduced the attacks of smallpox also. It cannot be denied that each and all of these are most powerful agents for good, and as they progress towards perfection so will smallpox and other diseases vanish. But, are we not to ascribe part of this improvement to the agency of vaccination. No, sir, no ! Vaccination is net an agent. It has no action, no capacity, n• power, no virtue, no attribute. Does it not act on living tissue? It does not ! What happens when it is put into a per- son's arm ? In every part of the living body there is living action carried on, and when this vaccine virus is inserted it hinders that action and there is heat evolved, just as a bar of iron that is pushed between the spokes of a wheel of a travelling carriage stops that wheel from turning round, friction is increased and head evolved. There is no action in the bar, it is simply an hindrance to the turning of the wheel. To hinder vital action is a very wicked thing to do because our lives and our health depend solely on the uninterrupted continuation of that action ; to expect any good to follow from the hindrance of vital action is folly beyond conception. We hear people talk and write about its " protective power." It has no power. Are the vaccinated any str•nger than other people? Can they do more work or do they work with greater ease. Are they more healthy ? No, sir, they are just as weak as other men. Indeed there is no manifestation of "protective power" beyond the vaccination placards that adorn our hoardings. Nature's method of making a man stronger is by eating, drink- ing, and sleeping. Yes sir, this is nature's glorious gospel and the man who tries te better her laws is a fool. Astrne as light cures darkness, as true as food cures hunger, so does life cure disease and protect from small- pox. But this accursed vaccine virus is dead I.' dead ! Yes, sir, as dead as a door's nail and ought to be buried. ,</p>
        <p>thoug</p>
        <p>Who'll dig hia grave? I said the owl with my spade and shovel, And I'll dig his grave. I am, sir, yours truly,</p>
        <p>481, Knutaford-rd., Latchford.</p>
        <p>F. SUMMERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>After the sudden bound which public curiosity took in the direction of the Association code, the season has stopped. But not so the elated enthusiasm of some of the exponents of the code. A number of them an dreaming castles in the air which are likely to figuratively crumble to dust during the " willow '' season. The craze has reached Stockton Heath, and what do you think has happened ? Mr. S. F. Welsby at the desire of several enthusiasts called a meeting the other night to try and rescm.itate th.a.t notorious team the Stockton Fleath Club ! " Hew, Leza—" But wait a little, give them a chance.</p>
        <p>They have not arranged any fixtures for the summer (which is queer but they have appointed officials for the new-old club for next year. Nothing like striking when the iron's hot. But will it be cold up that quarter by next season?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>CONFERENCE OF SUNDAY SCHOOL TEACHERS AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>CONFERENCE OF SUNDAY SCHOOL TEACHERS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A circuit conference of Weslyan t.inday School I teachers and workers was held on Thursday after- noon in Use Bold-st. Wesleyan Schools. The Rev. J. Kirtlan occupied the chair, and a paper on 'The conditions necessary to secure the early conversion of children, and their union with the church," was read by Miss Bessie Dutton, add another on " flow can we further increase the usefulness and efficiency of our adult classes," by Mr. W. C. Nicholson. An interesting discussion followed. The company then sat down to tea, after which a public meeting was held, Mr. Wm. Silcock in the chair. The chairman briefly opened the meeting, after which Mr. J.Hart- ley and Mr. Moston gave their experiences as teachers at Bold-st. and -Lymm,, respectively.—Coun. Shaw Green said that there was a good school at Silver-s. of which he was superintendent. They found all the senior scholars some employment in social gatherings, arc., although they did not use any extraor- dinary means to ksep them. Iu spite of this they would soon probably have to enlarge their borders.—Mr. R. ' Garnett thougat that discussions like the one they were engaged in were very important, in order to learn the ; most efficient way of teaching. He thought the ques- tion of the catechism ought to be discussed. Their ! I people could recite Wesley's hymns and quote passages ; ! of Scripture, but few could give a connected account of what they, as Wesleyans, believed. He thought that at least onee a fortnight the catechism should be taught. ; ! Referring to the P.S.A.'s, M. Garnett said that he had not a word to say against them. They brought together I the kind of people who would not otherwise go, but they had a tendency to take the teachers from their duties, ; and also to take away their elder scholars. He alsn found at the present day a great want of reverence in coming into places of worship, and also a want of reve- -1 rence to God's people.. All these matters could be touched upon in Sunday school conferences.—The Rev. Robert Culley (secretary of the Connexional Sunday School Union) congratulated those present on the good work they had done,and delivered an address which was ! listened to with great attention. A vote of thanks to Mr. Colley for his address was proposed by the Rev. T. Ross, and seconded by Mr. C. H. Kerry. On the motion of Mr. Pigot, seconded by Mr. Horton, it was decided to ; have another conference nine months hence. Bold-stand Silver-st. Ichools to send two delegates, and the remain- ing schools of the circuit to send one each. The meet- lug Wal closed by the pronouncing of the tiexediction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>PEN KETH.</head>
        <p>PEN KETH.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF AN OLD INHABITANT.—On Monday the- rem,iitis of Joseph 'rhornloy were interred at Sankey Church. Deceased was 83 years of age and was one el the oldest inhabitants of the village.</p>
        <p>MAY De.t.—llday Day this year passed off about the same as usual. A large number of people journeyed to Knutsford despite the depressing stat&amp; of the weather, in one of Mr. Connor's 'buses. There was also a pro- cession of children who decked with ribbons and ;flowers, went singing round the village. FOOTBAI.X. TEA PARTY.—Last evening week the second annual tea party and concert of the, Penketh Association Football Club was held in the Wesleyan school. About 130, people partook of the excellent meat tea whidh was provided by the following :—lilesdaines Longshaw, White, Handles and F. Hughes.. The tables were pre- sided aver by several young ladies, and the waiters were the members of the club. A.iter tea a most eig)oyable concert was given. Mr. G. handles, who took the chair apologized for the absence of Mr. J. B. Aitken. The Chairman said lie was much disappointed at the result of the match with Warrington, which in his opinion was wrongly judged. Mr. F. Spensely gave a pianoforte solo which was well rendered. A song by A. Oreenhalgh "Comrades " was next given and enthusiastically en- cored. Mr. A. Appleton in his rendering Of " The Pilot "was very good, and a song by J. Cavanagh " The Mountebanks "elicited much applause. MT. B. Spensely sang" Soldier Jim "in fine style and W. Ireland " The moon hath raised her lamp above." Mr. J. S. Haynes in the song " The shopwalker" received a hearty en- core. In the second part of the programme Professor T. Acton gave same of Ills wonderful sleight of handS tricks, and received a hearty round of applause. Songs were given by A. Appleton ", Jacks Wedding," J. Cavanagh " Jolly Jack Tars," W. Ireland "The Empress of the wave," B. Spensely " Pra off to Philadelphia 'a.nd another humorous song by J. S. Haynes" The waiter' and in response to a prolonged applause recited " The charge of the Light Brigade in different dialects. A vote of thanks was moved to the ladies who had pro- vided the excellent tea and to the helpers and artistes, by Mr. A. White and supported by G. Wilcox. Much praise also is due to Mr. J. Lewis and Mr. T. Earns. Mr. F. Spensley accompanier?, on the pianoforte,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>P:S.A. AT VVYCLIFFE.</head>
        <p>THE P:S.A. AT VVYCLIFFE.</p>
        <p>The Pleasant Sunday Afternoon service at Wycliffe Church was again numerously attended on Sunday afternoon. The chair was occupied by Mr. Richmond who delivered a short and interesting address on the motto of the P.S.A.—" Brief, Bright, Brotherly." Mr. A. Corney also addressed the men, basing his remarks on the parable of the unprofitable servants. Miss F. Booth contributed a solo. An organ solo was well ren- dered by the organist of the church, Mr. W. H. Payton. The membership of the brotherhood has now reached 522.</p>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES. WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL. The anniversary services in connection with the San- key Bridges Wesleyan Sunday Schools, were held on Sunday last. In the morning the preacher was the Rev. W.Wilson, returned missiomary from Fiji, who preached an excellent sermon from Psahn 23, Ist and 4th verses., There was as good attendance, and at the close of the ser- vice Mr. Ward rendered an oboe solo" fie shall feed his flock like a shepherd." In the afternoon a cantata en- titled " The captive maid of Israel " was rendered by an augmented choir, assisted by an orchestral band, under the leadership of Herr Praeger, the organist being Mr. T. Brough, and conductor Mr. W. Erough. The soloists, were Mn. Menlo, Mrs. J. Monks, Miss Morrell, Miss Cannon, Miss-Kellv,.Mr. A.. Scattergood, Mr. Cotterall, Mr. A. B.Bailey, Mr. It:Entwistle and Mr. L.Richardson. Mr. Ward. gave an oboe solo " I know that my redeemer liveth." In the evening there was a large congregation. and Islr. Wilson preached from the 63rd Psalm. Mr. Ward again gave as a oboe solo "Thou didst not leave." Me collections amounted-to £l7:oa. 3d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>TEE CHURCH AND THE WORLD.</head>
        <p>TEE CHURCH AND THE WORLD.</p>
        <p>In Cairo-street chapel on Sunday evening, the Rev. W. 11. Drummond spoke on the above-named subject. The rev 4 gentleman took for his text "God sent not His Son into the world to condemn the world, but that the world through tins might be saved,"John,3 c.,17 v. He said therc.. were not a few men at the present day who had been repelled from all religious associations by the prevalence of the distinction between the church and the world. They found little stimulus to the higher life in current modes of thought and worship, the church. seemed to forbid them the wide human fellowship and large freedom of thought for which they craved. Chris- tianity for them meant a spirit which confined thought and action within artificial limits, instead of imparting ' to them new, strength and expansion. It was creed, ceremony, An elaborate ecclesiastical organization, not the faith in which the soul learned its freedom, the truth by which men lived, grew, and were made strong. In such-auattitude there was doubtless some of the exag- geration inseperable horn every strong recoil frosn established custom; But it was the natural fruit of a system..which, railed off a small enclosure, as alone sacred in the wide field of human experience, and thus denied the right of the world to what was divine, and made entrance into the kingdom of God contingent as much upon. church fellowship as upon the unwritten faith of the heart. Might we not see in Christ something far nobler than the founder of an organised church ? Was not his aim Par more spiritual, and therefore far truer to human, nature than most of his followers had at all imagined Was net his method wonderfully plastic and original whets compared with the stiff and almost artificial methods, which now claimed the sanction of his name ? They approached ellristianity not from the side of ;forms, and beliefs, but as a universal principle of redemption,. which demanded the whole area of their experience for the field of its activity, and freedom to work how. and where it will. When life moved in a narrower sphere of interests, than it did to-day, sacred things naturally claimed a relatively greater attention and the secular could be dismissed into the second rank. ' But with the new zest for art, and literature, the unpre- cedented advance of scientific thought, the wide devel- opment of mercantile activity, the pressure of social conditions, the doubts, the questionings, the fresh enthusiasms which were slowly changing the whole aspect of the world for us, this was no longer the case. For a time at least these were the things which made themselves heard. And what had Christianity tc,say to it all, 2' Could it only repeat the old words, asd then mourn over the apostasy of a generation which 4 refused to hear ? Rather was it the day of its greatest oppor- tunity if only it would go out fearlessly into the world toiespire and spiritualise life. • BAPTIST ClfiAiEL, GOLBORNE-S7'.</p>
        <p>From aur advertising columns it will be setri that the above building will be re-opened to-morrow (Sunday), ,after having been closed for renovation and decoration, when the Rev. Abel anises Parry,of Cefn Mawr, /Illation, will preach the• Sunday School sermons. I,he Rev. Mr. Parry is perhaps better known in this parti of Lancashire , as having for same time tilled the pastoraze of Clough. told. He is one of the finest preacher. the Baptists possess in North Wales, and is a writer, and editor of no mean authority, he having rendered vea7 signal service in connection with the Welsh Press.. The friends at Golberne-st. are to be very highly congratulated upon the improvements made to the intenior of ths chapel. The building was erected in the yea 1811, and until about ten years ago was furnished with the now almost obsolete "square pews." In 1824, the interior was entirely renewed, and pitch pine furniture supplied, the decorating being left for a future effort. This effort hag. now been made, and the chapel was entrusted to Mr. F. J. Dell, of Sankey-st., the whole cf the work which has . been done in such a manner as todustify the Baptists in laying claim, to possessing one of the prettiest chapels, ao far as interior is concerned in the district. The ceiling has been finished in pale cream, with the cornice richly relieved la light tints, making, a nice bleeding with the ceiling ornaments to match. Dividing the cornice from the walks is a deep frieze of ornaments of fruit: and flowers and leaves,done in white en an azure ground,the ornaraeats tied with ribbons of pale gold colour. The walls are treated in a warm vellum colour, with stable corner pieces. Between the upper and lower range of windows a broad band of panels of pale blue,, richly decorated and surrounded by warm buff tiles, ren round I the chapel, dividing th,e walls from the wood work of I pews is a neat dado of terra cotta,with ornamentaof rich purple and dark green, surmounted by an effective border of various tinted blues. The most striking evi- dence of the decorator's hand is on the wall behind the I pulpit,which has been treated in a most artistic manner, the colours beisg in perfect harmony. Here era a series of texts, which give a happy relief to what was before a painfully monotonous scene. The pews and wood work have all been the subjects of careful work, and the result is that a most striking contrast has been made in the appearance of the chapel, mad what had formerly a gloomy,has now a roost cheerful aspect. WHAT THE QUAKERS RELIEVE. Mr. Wm. Edward Turner, a minister of the Saciety of Friends gave an address on " The Quakers, what they believe," at the Friends Meeting House. Alderman Holmes presided and introduced the lecturer, who said</p>
        <p>, thnt the endeavoar of the early Friends was to bring' . people bask to the simple teacbing of the Divine founder of the Christian religion. They felt that the Christian Church had grievously lapsed from the pure and primi- tive faith. The great central truth of Christianity, the presence of the spirit of Christ in the hearts of His fol- lowers, had come to be almost ignored. The times were ripe for another advance toward the simple and practi- cal religion of Christ,. and George Vox was manifestly called by God to lead in the work, The" Ten Talents of Quakerism " were formulated, namely-1, God's :spiritual light that lig.hteth every man ; 2, the indwelling , of the Spirit with the disciple ; 3; the headship of Christ in-his Church ; 4,,the priesthood of all believers ;5, the freedom of the gospel ministry ;81, the spiritual equality of the sexes ; gpiritual baptism and spiritual communion ; 8, the unlawfulness of war te the Christian ;. 9, the unlawfalness of oaths ; and 10, the duty of brotherly love and simplicity of life. In 'common with much of the best thought of the 19th century, the Quaker, considered that true religion did not depend primarily upon belief in any special formu- lated creed or theological opinions. It was an inward experience produced by the influence of God's Spirit on the soul. They (the Quakers). thought all eenabols out of harmony with the spiritual ideals of Christ's Christianity. They .felt that for them the presence of a rite administered by human agents,' lowered the concep- tion of direct union through man spirit with the one Lord, who is the everpeesent and guiding Head over all service and all development of life in His Church. They had no form of .worskin ;they had not even the singing of hymns or the pre-arranged public reading of Scripture they had no settled utterances of prayer or stated song of praise. They had no appointed leader or pastor set apart to conduct tkeir worship. Christ was their leader the minister of the sanctuary, and the true tabernacle which God had pitched,.aud not man. Whilst they disclaimed the idea of possessing the monopoly of the Holy Ghost, they regarded His presence and influence as an indispensable factor' in all true worship,. That the realization of His Presence might be their first object they sat down together in silence. The service of their preachers was without remuneration or salary. it wae not limited to men of• culture, and the gift was often held by men of small educational adVantages,. but whose. training had been in tbe•school of Christ. With regard te the practice of the early Church, their use of water baptism was simply a continuance of the recognised , Jewish mode of receiving converts; which on more made it binding upon them than their practice , of circumcision, washing of feet,, and anointing I the sick with oil, and having all things in common. They received the Scripture ot the Old and New Testa- ment as a Divine record cf a progressive revelation, of God, both as to His character and will, which culminated in the human life, teaching, and work of His Son, their Lord Jesus Christ, though, perhaps, no section of the ChristiareChurch had been more diligent inthe private and family reading of the Bible. Oa. the subject of war Mr. Turner said his friends had been almost alone in the belief that it stood outside the Christianity of Christ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
