      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>WARRINGTON, RURAL B•ANITARI</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON, RURAL B•ANITARI</p>
        <p>AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of this authority was held on Friday at the Town Hall, when there were pre- sent :—The Rev. G. F, Roberts, and Messrs. R. Heaton, Balharry, Dignutn,,Wm. Houghton, John White, J. Houghton, Jolley and Swinton ;.S. Sutton (clerk) and other officers.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF CHAIRVAR AND V•ICIDOSAIRMAN. The CLERK said that tha first business of the newly constituted authority was the election of chairman.and: vice-chairman.—Mr. Wm. Houronroic proposed the re- election of the hey. G. F. Itoberts.—Mr. It. HEATON. seconded, and the proposition was carried unanimously. Upon the proposition of Mr. H. FlitaToN, seconded by Mr. Diommt, Mr. Leong was appointed vice-chairman.</p>
        <p>ISOLATION OF 'SMALL-PDX PATINNTS, The Clarice read a letter from Mr. J. Lyon Whittle,. town clerk of Warringtor which contained anamend- ment to a resolution passed recently by the Warrington. Corporation, refusing to-take into their hospital any small-pox cases from the Hera! Sanitary Authority dis- trict. The amendment was that cases would be taken from that part of the district of Sankey Bridges which was adjoining the borough, and which.was in the extra,- municipal district.—The CHAIRMAN was glad to hear th:s and said he believed the epidemic had almost dis- appeared. PENIRTH MATER SOPPLT4—SEIRIOUS COMPLAINTS. The following letter from an unknown person, received by Mr. R: Heaton was read by the Clerk, relat, 'ing to the Penketh water supply i—"Penketh, 2nd May, 1893. Sir,—l drop a few lines to you. as you are a member of the Rend Sanitary Authority, touching the water supply at Penketh. The state of things hero. is really bad, and somethitig, must be. done. There-is a family now in -Penketh whose childremarein the doctorl hands suffering, it is said by the doctor himself, from. the deficient and impure water supply. Others.are complaining in theeliame wav, and if nothing is done we have a really bad prospect before us-here. A dry sum- mer would assuredly lead to an.outhreak of fever and its such a case to be fire-warned is-to be fore-armed, with Cuerdley main so near and .Sankey. Bridges is where-the- town water reaches, there's-no excuse, and Ido Imps you will use all the means in your power to impress rear Committee with the real state of things. I don't sign my name as landlords might feel rather touchy in the matter." —Yours, Stes" He also read the following letter which had been received by Mr. Dignum :=- Penketh Tannery, to Mr. John Dignum. Dear Sir,-1 understand that the question of a proper water supply. may come before the Rural Sanitary Authority meedng to-day. I trust that should occasion serve, you will see the necessity of this,being dealt with. At present we are in a state of water, famine,, referring specially to our own works where we feel the necessity of more water most in the naatter of our drainage. Although we have and are paying our full share towards the cost of- the village drainage we have not been allowed to make use of it although the inspector from the Local Govern- ment Board most distinctly said,. when the matter first came up, that we should be entitled to use this drain.. If, however, there was. sufficient water available to flush our own drain this deprivation would not matter but our drain, as your‘lnspector will know, on account of our scanty supply of water, is a source of constant trouble to ourselves and always liable of beconaieg a„ nuisante and danger to the neighbourhood.--Wure„&amp;c4 Charles E. Paller." Mr. Dignum considered it was an opportune time to bring the matter before the authority, for they. were hearing complaints on every band respecting the den: cent water supplyat Penketh„ some complainta bring about the quantity:of water and others being about it impurity. Ha believed that the complaints were wet founded, and he conldbear his testimony to the truth, of the statement made in the anonymous letter respect- ing the sickness of the children, who, were, upon the statement of a medical man suffering from the impurity of the water. Beewas sure-that if proper inqpiries were made Penketh would be found to be in pessession of a very inferior water. supply. It was an increasing place and many houses were being erected in the neighbour- hood, and he believed that it was three or four years , since the water was tested in some parts of the place. ' He had not heard the complaint from the Tanning Com- pany betore,•but he thought it was an additional. reason why the matter-should now be dealt with. The supply, he believed, was got from about 0 wells,. several of which had notteen examined for a considerable time. He was afraid that if the threatened draught were to take place, .during the summer and no better provision was made,the consequences might be serious to Peaked'. —Dr. Sepnton said that the water supply to all houses was examined when the houses were erected, and he had not heard of the case of the children suffering from the imiserity of-the water. He believed that a proper supply of water could easily be obtained in Penketh if they set. about- it properly.—The Chairman proposed that the. Streets Committee should be, deputed to look into the matter, and report to the authority upon it.— It was sostnested that a Parochial Committee should be reformat -to investigate the matter, but, ultimately it was agreed to add the name of Mr. Dignum to the Street Committee and that the Committee should in- quire into the matter.</p>
        <p>THE RIXTON LOAN, The CLEM announced that the loan of £1,660 for the erection of the Rixton Cemetery, had been placed in the hands of their treasurer, and it would he raid at a rate of interest of £9O 13a. (Id., per year,. for SO years.—Mr. Owen said that it would be neeessary that instructions should be given respecting the contracts,and the author- ity decided to instruct the Parochial Committee to meet Mr. Owen with a view to commencing the work as soon as possible.</p>
        <p>StIEDICAL OFFICERS' REPORT. Dr. SiIMITODT in his monthly report stated that tbsTs had been 39 births and 15 deaths during, the month. The death rate from all causes was 1.3.3 per thousand against 15.8 per thousand as an average for the past three months. and against 27.4 in the corresponding month of last year.</p>
        <p>NOTWE OF MOTION. • Mr. R. fIEsToN gave notice that he should at the next meeting move a motion for the re-appointment of Dr. Septiton as medical officer, and M. F. Kerfoot as nui- saace inspector.</p>
        <p>SA.NKET ZROOK. Correspondence was read from Dr. Sergeants county analyst, who has been examining Sankey Brook. He reported that the condition of the brook, although it might be a great public nuisance was not injurious to health as was apprehended. But if it was thought necessary to abate its pollution it would cost them a very large sum of money to work out the scheme he had to suggest.—The matter was discussed but no action decided upon.</p>
        <p>FALL PROM A SIGNAL POST AT WARRINGTON Thomas (lough, a railway potter, living in Lyon- st., Latchford, was admitted to the Infirmary on Monday night suffering from several severe contu- sions on the head nnd sides. It appears that he was cleaning the lamps on a signal post, when his foot slipped and lie fell on the side of the line‘ He is progressing favourably,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-05-06_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Li SM.</head>
        <p>Li SM.</p>
        <p>THE V.CLUNTEEng.—Theloca Voluntetis assembled the grounds adning Lieztenant (heves residence, 4?: Saturday, and were inspected by the Adfatant (CaPt3l.. ims Whitt). Prizes to the number of three to each sw were given for the best tumoral in the matting of cdtlir ments. On S.triday morning a church parade-took plac:i The men, about 100 in, number, under the c..;unna ro ' Lieutenant Groves marched tc, Onghtrington Chu " nd where they attended Divine service. rde Local. BOARD.—At the monthly meeting of the Boa on Tuesday, there were present—Mr. James Grego (chairman), Messrs. EL Elsrrison, :awes H01t,./b: WhiT legg, W. Brazerniale. A. Mclntyre, ii Holt, S. Apvieto„7. J. Perrin, T. J. Ridgway (law clerk), W. Mullard and J. Mort (surveyer). A letter vms read from Ward, organizing secretary of the Cheshire Cotrti' Council, stating that;.having heard frvin Mr. Listerthat the Board wished to have a course of lectures oa nursing in Lymm, he could supply a course the cost °I which would be 10s. adecture.—Mr. Breezendale: If their are attended no better-than the last, they will not be much use.—On the motion of Mr. .L Holt, seconded' Mr. Harrison, it wag decided to have a course of lecture; A letter was read from. the secretary of the Union Lancashire and Cheshire Institutes, informing the 1303.2: that Miss Annie Dalton had passed the recent exairdo" tion in cookery, but was not eligible for a prize in CI sequence of not having attended the required number o times. There was no other business of public interest.</p>
